}\uc1\pard\f0\fs24 P?S/8 Version 8Z Change Log.\par
\par
\f1 Copyright \'a9 2019 \'bbCLA Systems\par
CLASystems@gmail.com\par
\par
Introduction.\par
\par
All changes to P?S/8 Version 8Z (since the initial release on several devices) are logged in this file.\par
\par
Note: Since system patches may address many issues, the P?S/8 Master Glossary of Terms is an addendum to this document.\par
\par
23-Feb-1988\tab Initial system creation.\par
\par
This is the date of the initial release of P?S/8 Version 8Z.\par
\par
This does not include updates to various components created somewhat later.  The relevant files are currently under review; however, the only known issues relate to the simultaneous use of the \ul\i Logical\ulnone\i0  \ul\i Console Overlay\ulnone\i0  configured for the \i VT8E\i0 , and \i FOCAL\i0  customized with the VT8E-oriented \i VT8PCH\i0  binary patch file.  This issue has been tabled pending a more general release of the system.\par
\par
13-Nov-2014\tab Version 8Z for MDC8 diskette (FLP) image recovered.\par
\par
This image was recovered from a working diskette for P?S/8 Version 8Z configured for the \i MDC8\i0  5.25" diskette drives (\i FLP\i0 ) dated 23-Feb-1988.\par
\par
Most importantly, the .DUMP files that are used to generate system components beyond the initial starter system were recovered from this image after successfully transferring it to the SIMH environment.\par
\par
Note:  A series of utilities was required to create the SIMH version of this image.  The files were accessed and converted using existing bootable images of P?S/8 Version 8T.\par
\par
All known variant system-generation .DUMP files were recovered including the common file used to extend the starter system (created from binary files of the relevant source files) to a (nearly) complete system.\par
\par
Note: Several additional system components are generally added to full releases to include support for specific hardware such as media formatting or copying programs.\par
\par
The .DUMP file used to complete the FLP system is essentially the same as the similar file used for TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 systems with the exception of the device length (the capacity of the FLP system diskettes exceeds that of standard or premium \i DECtape\i0  media).\page\cf1 P?S/8 Version 8Z Change Log (continued).\par
\cf0\par
26-Nov-2014\tab P?S/8 Version 8Z \cf2\highlight3\f0 A\cf0\highlight0\f1  source files recovered.\par
\par
All known source files for P?S/8 that are part of the \cf2\highlight3\f0 A\cf0\highlight0\f1  collection (meant to be placed on an RK\ul\i A\ulnone x\i0 : drive or equivalent) were recovered from OS/8 format MDC8 5.25" diskettes.  Image files were transferred to SIMH and all files were checked for consistent contents and file validity.  These files have also been transferred to MS-DOS/Windows equivalent directories with complete file names (OS/8 file naming limitations have been removed).\cf1\par
\par
\cf0 13-Dec-2014\tab P?S/8 Version 8Z \cf2\highlight3\f0 B\cf0\highlight0\f1  source files recovered.\par
\par
All known source files for P?S/8 that are part of the \cf2\highlight3\f0 B\cf0\highlight0\f1  collection (meant to be placed on an RK\ul\i B\ulnone x\i0 : drive or equivalent) were recovered from OS/8 format MDC8 5.25" diskettes.  Image files were transferred to SIMH and all files were checked for consistent contents and file validity.  These files have also been transferred to MS-DOS/Windows equivalent directories with complete file names (OS/8 file naming limitations have been removed).\par
\par
VT8E and MDC8 issues.\par
\par
At some later point in time, the VT8E-related source files mentioned above were recovered along with many other files unrelated to P?S/8 (apparently as part of a general backup of an OS/8 development drive based on the MDC8).  These files are not part of the general release of P?S/8 per se; they are needed by users who configure the \ul Logical\i  \i0 Console Overlay\ulnone  and/or P?S/8 \i FOCAL\i0  for use with the \i VT8E\i0  pseudo-terminal DMA device; binary files produced from these files are passed to the respective program for customization purposes.  Future releases of P?S/8 may include these updated source files; at this time, it is not known if this work was finalized (or even tested).\par
\par
As of this writing, nearly all P?S/8 source files have been recovered with notable exceptions related to the MDC8 \i Winchester\i0  (hard drive) system, the two variant \i LINC-8\i0  system configurations supported, and all files related to the \i TD8E\i0  both with and without the \i MR8E-C\i0  read-only memory (\i ROM\i0 ).\par
\par
The MDC8 Winchester source files can be derived from other source files already recovered, notably the P?S/8 MDC8 FLP system and non-system device handlers, and the OS/8 MDC8 system and non-system device handlers for the FLP and Winchester systems.\par
\par
Note: All MDC8 device handlers are quite elegant due to the exceptional nature of the hardware (which was designed to avoid software issues).  As such, only a minimal effort should be required to restore any missing (or new) handlers as necessary.  Other than the usual relationships between P?S/8 and OS/8 regarding logical blocks of 128 words/block versus records of 256 words/block, an advanced feature was added to allow a master offset of a memory field of storage before starting all device handlers (in either system).\page\cf1 P?S/8 Version 8Z Change Log (continued).\cf0\par
\par
The purpose of this logical offset is to implement the essential element of \i MENU-8\i0 , a master bootup system resembling \ul\i Master Menu\ulnone\i0  as implemented on the DECmate II and DECmate III+ systems using hard disks; however, DECmate systems include hardware support of named volumes to isolate the menu system from the subordinate operating systems.  On the MDC8, a logical sector offset allows sufficient code space to implement the most primitive functions of a menu-based system; this includes configuring where on the device the rest of the menu system is located (based on absolute block addresses stated as a 24-bit logical block value).\line\line Because the MDC8 logical capacity includes a reserved area at the highest physical blocks, the rest of MENU-8 is intended to be located just before the blocks reserved for the bad block management area on the disk.  In general, this represents a small percentage of the overall device capacity; as such, MENU-8 reserving slightly more disk space is not a significant issue.\line\line While MENU-8 was never completed, the source code was also recovered along with the VT8E-related files mentioned above.  The work can be continued should appropriate hardware-specific circumstances arise.  (For new work on PDP-8 storage devices, the MDC8 is the recommended design to emulate assuming single-cycle DMA transfer operations.)\line\line As currently implemented, the MDC8 will boot into the lowest logical sector, which could contain the boot block of either P?S/8 or OS/8 assuming the respective source code files are assembled with the offset disabled.  If the offset is enabled in these handlers, it is assumed that the MENU-8 reboot/redirect code is present, configured to point to the desired operating system located further up the device.  Because of the logical addressing, well-rounded values are generally used as starting logical block addresses (if the offset is disabled) and one track higher-still (if the offset is enabled).  All configurations on any particular hard drive must use consistent offset settings for all structures.\line\line With regard to MDC8 support, the only handler source code currently unavailable is the P?S/8 system device handler for the MDC8 hard drive.  Fortunately, it shares much of the design with the corresponding OS/8 system device handler (which was recovered along with several of the other source files mentioned above).  Thus, it is fairly straightforward to recover the original handler by using the\par
P?S/8 system device handler for the FLP system coupled with the OS/8 system device handler for the Winchester system.  All of the relevant geometrical definitions are equivalently contained in the two handlers, thus the recovery can be easily completed with reasonable effort.  Note: The equivalent Winchester non-system handler was never written for P?S/8; however, this can be derived from the FLP non-system handler.  All of these handlers represent themes and variations on each other.\page\cf1 P?S/8 Version 8Z Change Log (continued).\cf0\par
\par
LINC-8 and TD8E issues.\par
\par
Bootable LINC-8 systems are available on surviving \i LINCtape\i0  media.  The missing source files can be recovered by performing a reasonable effort to produce a full binary disassembly; additionally, certain related utilities are available in source form (which can help with certain code fragments).\par
\par
All P?S/8 TD8E source files are presently unavailable.  Assuming the files are never recovered, programming for this device will require new work, in part based on the author's recollections.\par
\par
Note: A partial recovery of TD8E support has been initiated using a recovered P?S/8 TD8E with MR8E-C ROM DECtape image.  It is not clear if this is the final version of this configuration.  The author acknowledges this particular system variant was produced under expedient circumstances with known defects.  For the present, it is presumed this will be the best available recovered source code fragment; disassembly has produced a working copy that documents the known defective behavior.\par
\par
Additionally, a set of specific workarounds for the flawed aspect of this system have been proposed but not finalized pending overall source code cleanup; it is not certain the corrected source code will be compatible with P?S/8 Version 8Z, as most new work in ongoing projects is targeted to a next-generation Version 9A system which has slightly different internal features.  Considering the changes that will be required to produce Version 9A compatibility, it is deemed of little value to produce an interim release for Version 8Z.  Additionally, some of the recovery work will effect a TD8E with extended memory variant system, as certain components are either partially or fully compatible across both configurations.\par
\par
It is known that the missing files were never part of the \cf2\highlight3\f0 A\cf0\highlight0\f1  and \cf2\highlight3\f0 B\cf0\highlight0\f1  file collections due to lack of sufficient storage space at the various development sites used at the time.  Backup copies of these files may show up as part of an ongoing recovery process.\par
\par
All present efforts will be focused on releasing other P?S/8 configurations that are achievable using the various source files, most notably the TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 DECtape system, the TC12 LINCtape system, the RX01 system and the RK8E/RK05 system.  Several other sets of handler source code files exist for more obscure third-party devices such as the DSD-240-8/Western Dynex fixed/removable disk cartridge system.\par
\par
The most appropriate system to implement first is a modern release of the TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 system; surviving hardware is available in several locations; excellent support for this device is available within the SIMH environment.  Many internal aspects of P?S/8 development must be dealt with before any semblance of a workable system can be created.\page\cf1 P?S/8 Version 8Z Change Log (continued).\par
\cf0\par
15-May-2015\tab Hacking up a bootable system for the TC01/TC08 DECtape.\par
\par
Using P?S/8 Version 8T for TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 (available on the SIMH simulator), the system device handler for the TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 was kludged onto to a copy of the image of the Version 8Z FLP system.  Predictably, many functions do not work properly; with some manual patching of the directory, a viable temporary system was created.\cf1\par
\cf0\par
Most importantly, access to certain newer system programs is now possible to aid in a proper reconstruction.  The image of the Version 8Z FLP system can be accessed as a mounted logical unit (such as unit 6).\par
\par
Note: P?S/8 Version 8T is quite incompatible with P?S/8 Version 8Z. The only functional level of compatibility is the keyboard monitor commands (while somewhat different and more like those of the \ul\i R-L Monitor System\ulnone\i0 ) are equivalent to those in the newer system allowing access to the TFS directory on the newer system.\par
\par
15-Jun-2015\tab Bootable startup system for the TC01/TC08 DECtape.\par
\par
Various OS/8 BATCH files are available within the \cf2\highlight3\f0 A\cf0\highlight0\f1  and \cf2\highlight3\f0 B\cf0\highlight0\f1  file collections to assemble the binary files needed to create a P?S/8 release.  By studying the BATCH files, it was determined what source files are needed to create what is referred to as the P?S/8 \i starter\i0  system.\par
\par
The starter system is a bootable operating system with limited capability configured for the designated target device.  Creation of the starter system depends on the ability to run the target system device handler for the purpose of creating a clean image of itself (and other system components) from a working copy moved to the intended operating memory locations (07600-07777).  An internal system generation routine is used, essentially a series of calls to write out system component sections loaded into most memory locations of a 12K machine; the system generation routine is aware of the loading address of all components involved in the system generation (which is unrelated to their ultimate execution address when called into memory for normal operating usage).\par
\par
Note: Certain system device configurations require an extended memory device handler extension for basic operation.  In some cases, the complication of creating a functioning system device handler for the intended purpose may be cumbersome.  Future releases will avoid this problem by supplying an associated non-system device handler for the intended configuration.  In most instances, the non-system device handler will occupy less memory than the routines needed to force temporary viability of the system device handler beyond the bootable image form.  Additionally, in certain specific cases, the source code can be simplified to allow internal documentation that is more appropriate to the resultant coding.\page\cf1 P?S/8 Version 8Z Change Log (continued).\par
\cf0\par
The starter system is obtained by loading the following binary files in the stated order as follows:\par
\par
1)\tab Z12K.BN\tab\tab This is a memory-clearing file that loads 0000 \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab into every memory location in the range of 00000-\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab 07577, 10000-17577 and 20000-27577.  This pre-\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab clears the memory that will be occupied by the \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab system components that are loaded next.\par
\cf1\par
\cf0 2)\tab PQSMON.BN\tab\tab This is the primary code of the P?S/8 keyboard\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab monitor, P?S/8 \i BATCH\i0 , P?S/8 \i FIND\i0 , P?S/8 \i CHANGE\i0  and\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab various support elements of P?S/8 that must be\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab written to designated storage blocks.  The system\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab generation routines are also part of this file.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab Note: Future releases of P?S/8 may reduce this\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab file to merely the keyboard monitor.  By doing\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab this, these separate system programs can be better\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab explained.  Even using the present mechanism to\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab create the starter system (and extending it to\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab include most of the rest of the system programs\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab later) does not require these other system program\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab components to be functional or even available\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab (albeit highly recommended).  As such, a better\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab overall mechanism can relegate the creation of all\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab system programs to whatever ultimate process is\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab used to create the overall system.\cf1\par
\par
\cf0 3)\tab PQSDTA.BN\tab\tab This is the image of the system device handler for \tab\tab\tab\tab TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56.  Since this device also \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab supports a \i Slurp\i0  binary file loader, the image of\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab the hardware-dependent Slurp binary file loader is\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab included.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab Note: For systems that do not support a hardware-\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab dependent Slurp binary file loader, the system\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab generation routines write out a non-functional\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab dummy program stub to cause subsequent attempts to\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab deploy a hardware-dependent Slurp binary file\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab loader to instead use the device-independent\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab virtual Slurp binary file loader.  However, all\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab Slurp Binary file loaders include a reboot program\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab that starts at 07600 which is used in several\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab independent contexts (the dummy version of the\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab loader is not an exception in this regard).\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab Since this device supports a hardware-dependent\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab Slurp binary file\i  \i0 loader, the system device\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab handler reloader file-image (associated with the\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab P?S/8 \i BIN\i0  utility \highlight1 /I\highlight0  command-line option switch)\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab is also included.\page\cf1 P?S/8 Version 8Z Change Log (continued).\par
\cf0\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab Note: Once the system is created, the file-image\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab component described above is located at absolute\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab block 0060 on the system device; the required data\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab is sufficiently short to reside in a single block.\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab When the file-image block (0060 on the booted\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab logical unit) is passed to the hardware-dependent\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab Slurp binary file loader, the system device\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab handler is loaded over the same memory space\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab currently occupied by the hardware-dependent Slurp\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab binary file loader itself.  Accomplishing this\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab without impact on user memory loading is\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab completely functional (albeit counterintuitive).\par
\line 4)\tab BIN.BN\tab\tab This is the image of the P?S/8 \i BIN\i0  utility program\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab which includes the P?S/8 \i START\i0  and P?S/8 \i GET\par
\i0\tab\tab\tab\tab commands (created as aliases with alternate\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab starting addresses within the same program image).\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab Note: The P?S/8 System Programs directory contains\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab several sets of aliased entry points into the same\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab program image.  For example, P?S/8 CHANGE is\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab actually an alias (with an alternate starting\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab address) in the same program image as P?S/8 FIND.\par
\cf1\par
\cf0 5)\tab ODT.BN\tab\tab This is the image of the P?S/8 Octal Debugging\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab Technique (\i ODT\i0 ) program which implements a proper\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab superset of the OS/8 equivalent.  Additional\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab features include support for multiple breakpoints,\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab illegal breakpoint detection and memory dump\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab capabilities using any of several data formats.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab With P?S/8 ODT available to the starter system, it\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab is possible to make certain changes to the P?S/8\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab keyboard monitor and other system components if\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab unusual (or unexpected) situations arise.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab Note: While the P?S/8 starter system is quite\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab meager without additional utilities (that are\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab added later), there is sufficient utility\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab available for correction of minor issues.\par
\par
6)\tab DUMP.BN\tab\tab This is the image of the P?S/8 \i DUMP\i0  utility \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab program.  Recent releases of P?S/8 DUMP support\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab command files to automate block transfers and\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab block patching.  Once the starter system is\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab running, .DUMP files can be passed to P?S/8 \i DUMP\i0  \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab to implement the rest of the system.\page\cf1 P?S/8 Version 8Z Change Log (continued).\par
\par
\cf0 The system generation process creates a functional subset of the TFS directory (without necessarily supporting the proper file capacity). An appropriate .DUMP file is used to initialize the TFS directory\par
parameters to match the proper device size.  Advanced users can\par
override these values as appropriate; this will be needed in specific situations (such as supporting premium-length DECtapes or LINCtapes).\par
\par
Note: The .DUMP files are generally made available on a logical device\par
unit other than the system bootup unit (which is created without any existing files).  As appropriate, the files can be copied to the system bootup unit; at least one additional logical device unit is required for the binary data to be transferred as the starter system is updated in place.\par
\cf1\par
\cf0 The starter system binary composite is started at 00200.  The machine\par
halts to allow any manual changes of target media; pressing \highlight1 CONTINUE\highlight0  starts the main system generation process.  After many write\par
operations, the machine halts again; pressing \highlight1 CONTINUE\highlight0  will boot the newly-created starter system (assuming the system was configured for the current hardware; front panel switch options can override this in various ways).\par
\par
25-Jun-2015\tab Problems completing the common system generation.\par
\par
While not all components of the OS/8 BATCH command files have been tested to determine if a complete collection of required binary files can be obtained, a problem has already been identified:\par
\par
All components of every released configuration of P?S/8 beyond the starter system must be created on an OS/8 non-system device such as \i DTA1\i0 :.  Even if a specific program is not required for any particular system, error-free assembly of all components must be accomplished.\par
\cf1\par
\cf0 The reason for this requirement is the OS/8 non-system device will contain core-image copies of all of the P?S/8 components involved for many system configurations; the specific OS/8 records where these files are stored must be predictable.  The .DUMP files are completely pragmatic; the most minor change in core-image file placement will corrupt the system generation process.\par
\par
With two notable exceptions, all of the P?S/8 source files are compatible with \i PAL8\i0  Version B0, which is the standard version released with \i OS/278\i0  Version 2.0.  To within a minor operational bug that never affects most users, this version will function correctly under earlier systems such as OS/8 Version 3D (considered the standard release for all PDP-8 systems).  (The bug has to do with the \i TTY\i0 : handler and interaction with pressing the \highlight1 Control-Z\highlight0  character; the problem is completely avoidable by manually observing proper timing or simply not using the \i TTY\i0 : handler during assembly.)  The bug does not affect assembly of P?S/8 sources; however, some releases of OS/8 only include early releases of PAL8 that require certain patches for proper operation.\page\cf1 P?S/8 Version 8Z Change Log (continued).\cf0\line\par
Note: In particular, the original release of PAL8 associated with OS/8 Version 3D is not recommended for use; however, available patches can produce a version that is adequate for the purpose of P?S/8 system generation.  The OS/278 version of PAL8 is widely available since it is included in the Kermit-12 collection written by the author of this document.  Moreover, Kermit-12 is meant to be assembled on DECmates. As such, the requirements for Kermit-12 include the need for one universal release version reasonably compatible with all possible systems Kermit-12 runs on; no release of PAL8 from OS/8 Version 3D runs on DECmates.\cf1\par
\cf0\par
Two P?S/8 system programs (\i MARK12\i0  and \i TC12F)\i0  are adaptations of standard components of the \ul\i LAP6-DIAL/DIAL-MS\ulnone\i0  system for the PDP-12.  The source code was taken from a specific release of DIAL-MS and may not be the final release code (nor is this even important).  Apparently there are no operational differences between this code and the standard versions found on any newer\i  \i0 DIAL systems per se; however, these programs have been modified to be more suitable for use with 128 words/block LINCtapes (which are used by P?S/8 and OS/8 on the PDP-12 and the LINC-8) with the ability to format longer LINCtapes assuming premium media is available.  As such, these programs must be supported as modified.\par
\par
Note: Custom modifications exist in the P?S/8 version of these programs to overcome DIAL-specific issues which are in conflict with PDP-8 memory loading conventions.  When these programs are loaded into\par
memory, the P?S/8 kernel is destroyed.  As such, manual bootstrap procedures are required after using either program.\par
\par
When P?S/8 system generation was being performed circa 1988, these programs were assembled with a specific user-written assembler known as OS/8 \i PAL12\i0 .\par
\cf1\par
\cf0 PAL12 is based on a very early version of \i PS/8\i0  PAL8 (which is lacking many PDP-8 assembler features when compared to later PAL8 releases).  Despite major shortcomings, the LINC mode assembly facility compares well to the DIAL embedded assembler LINC mode implementation.  Very little PDP-8 mode assembly is used in the source code for MARK12 and TC12F (which are primarily LINC code); as such, This particular PAL12 release is an acceptable utility.\par
\par
Note: P?S/8 PAL Version 8S (released as a component of P?S/8 Version 8Z) is a proper superset of both PAL8 and PAL12; however, it is not an OS/8 program.\par
\par
Unfortunately, attempts to locate this specific release of PAL12 have not been successful to date:\par
\par
Earlier versions of PAL12 were located that cannot assemble the programs due to lacking certain assembler features (added to subsequent releases).  Apparent file dates confirm these are precursors to the release used in 1988.\page\cf1 P?S/8 Version 8Z Change Log (continued).\cf0\line\line Unfortunately, the PAL12 project was taken over by individuals with a\par
poor understanding of how a proper LINC mode assembler should function.  While the features missing from the earlier releases are present (and many irrelevant features of PAL8 found in newer releases), the ability to assemble most LINC mode instructions is incompatible other than when using simplistic subsets of the language.  The newer release is essentially worthless for practical assembly of LINC programs such as TC12F and MARK12.\par
\par
an OS/8 LINCtape for the modified 8K LINC-8 has been located.  This includes LINC-8 handlers loaded into OS/8 \i BUILD\i0 .  This LINCtape may be the only surviving copy for the LINC-8 due to its dependency on hardware modifications only applied to a specific 8K LINC-8.  (P?S/8 runs on another 4K LINC-8 with the same modifications.)  An included paper directory indicates the required PAL12 release should be located on the tape.  Once a reasonable recovery process can be completed, the issue of producing the common P?S/8 system components (and system-specific add-ons) will be revisited.\line\line It is the author's recollection the OS/8 handler used on this unique OS/8 bootable LINC-8 LINCtape does not depend on all of the available instructions provided by the final release of the modifications (which were implemented in stages).  As such, it is highly likely that even if the source code is accurately reconstructed, it can be improved to add useful features as were used in the P?S/8 system device handler.\par
\par
Note: Fortunately, LINC-8 LINCtape utility programs exist that take advantage of the same hardware modifications as used in these (and the analogous P?S/8) handlers; as such, a reasonable effort should recover the missing handlers (and implement performance improvements),\par
\par
Note: Some programming effort (using the SIMH PDP-8 simulator) has been performed modifying the MARK12 program in an attempt to make the source code compatible with the newer (albeit deficient) release of PAL12.  In theory, after a lot of recoding effort, the source code for the two affected programs could produce proper binary files for use in\par
the P?S/8 system generation process.  However, the two affected system programs are not present on the recovered P?S/8 Version 8Z (FLP) system; existing binary copies are needed to confirm the source code changes produce the intended binary output.  A P?S/8 Version 8R system for TC12 has also been recovered.  If a copy of the core image of the programs on this LINCtape can be transferred to the SIMH environment, the source code modification process can continue with confidence of an accurate outcome.\par
\par
27-Jun-2015\tab Hacking up a complete P?S/8 Version 8Z system for TC08.\par
\par
It has been determined that the P?S/8 Version 8Z (FLP) system is essentially the same as the TC08 system with regard to the process of creating the common system generation and system-specific extensions.  The only important difference is ensuring the TFS directory length is appropriate to the TC08 system.\page\cf1 P?S/8 Version 8Z Change Log (continued).\par
\cf0\par
Note: This was verified by comparison of the FLP-specific and TC01/TC08-specific .DUMP files used to implement additional system programs.\par
\par
P?S/8 \i BLKCPY\i0  was run by booting to the starter system with the hacked-up TC08 (but mostly FLP) system on logical device unit 0 and an exact copy of the FLP system (re-imaged as a DECtape structure) mounted on logical unit 6.  This allows the major sections that would be created by the .DUMP files to be copied en masse from the blocks on device unit 6 to the corresponding blocks on the booted device unit 0.\par
\par
Note: While P?S/8 is generally compatible with all standard bootstraps on all standard devices, the TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 (Version 8Y or newer) systems are also compatible with \i TCBOOT\i0 , an extended DECtape startup program that allows P?S/8 to boot to any DECtape drive unit in the range of 0-7.  TCBOOT can also boot most PDP-8 operating systems for the same configuration (as long as drive unit 0 is selected).  While TCBOOT is fully operational, usage other than booting P?S/8 for TC01/TC08 (on non-zero logical units) is not recommended since shorter bootstraps exist (for drive unit 0 only).\par
\par
A copy of TCBOOT.PAL is available from \i Charles Lasner\i0 ; this program requires manual drive unit selection via the front panel switch register; an automatic version can be derived that will detect the highest drive unit available (in a manner analogous to how most RX01/RX02 bootstrap programs function).\par
\par
Certain additional system programs were copied using P?S/8 \i DUMP\i0  in manual command mode; this included copying the System Programs Directory.  This allows the newly-added system programs to run directly on the newly-created TC08 system.\par
\par
After confirming (by direct inspection with P?S/8 DUMP) that all directory structures are consistent with the TC01/TC08 system, the first working copy of P?S/8 Version 8Z for TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 (in over twenty-five years) has been recreated.\par
\par
10-Jul-2015\tab P?S/8 PAL bug fix.\par
\par
Problem: P?S/8 PAL creates defective binary files only during an obscure scenario involving the simultaneous creation of binary files and listing output.  If literals are in use and there are no current page literals on the last page of code and page zero literals exist to be dumped because there is a FIELD directive in the source code, the field change associated with the new field setting will be created incorrectly.  This problem had apparently been present for many releases prior to P?S/8 PAL Version 8S, but was never discovered before due to the requirement of many specific conditions being in effect simultaneously.\page\cf1 P?S/8 Version 8Z Change Log (continued).\par
\par
\cf0 The problem was traced to improper usage of a subroutine header as a counter (generally a valid technique) which violates subroutine modularity.  As such, the count value must be moved to an available temporary pending future rework.\par
\par
Resolution: As a temporary fix, all such references to the temporary (\i XP45CH\i0 ) will be patched to instead use an available Page Zero location; future releases will reorganize the code to prevent the problem.\par
\par
Note: A valid alternative technique is likely available to avoid requiring an additional Page Zero location on a separate basis.  The two references a) set a data counter, b) count up using an \i ISZ\i0  instruction.  By using an alternate storage location, the intended logic will be preserved.\par
\par
For example, there is likely another subroutine header that is available (indirectly though an existing Page Zero pointer).  The coding would then be organized in a manner equivalent to what was attempted; the indirect bit would be cleared in both instructions if a locally addressable memory location is used.\line\line Note: While P?S/8 PAL coding is extremely optimized, there are a small number of spare locations.  These will likely be used for planned additional features and other modifications; proper optimization techniques should always be applied in all important system programs as required.\cf1\par
\cf0\par
Implementation: Using P?S/8 BLKODT, the problem is readily fixed:\par
\par
.BLKODT\par
\par
0:0266.001 /3315 3174\tab\tab\cf1 DCA \{Page Zero temporary location\}\cf0\par
11+\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\cf1 Setup address of next patch\cf0\par
0:0266.012 /1315 1174\tab\tab\cf1 TAD \{Page Zero temporary location\}\par
\cf0 S\cf1\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab Save latest block and exit\cf0\par
.\cf1\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab Command prompt\par
\cf0\par
01-Aug-2015\tab Date year base group update to 2008.\par
\par
In P?S/8 Version 8Z (and several prior releases), the date expressed is partially determined by the present value of the implicit base year group.  The base year group is calculated as the count of 8 year increments past 1960; when P?S/8 Version 8Z was released, the value used was 3 corresponding to a valid year range of 1984 through 1994.\par
\par
Note: While the internal implementation of the date allows a theoretical range of somewhat over 11 years, the P?S/8 \i DATE\i0  utility only allows valid dates in the present 11 year range to avoid unexpected date expiration during the next (partial) year following.\page\cf1 P?S/8 Version 8Z Change Log (continued).\par
\cf0\par
The base year values are implemented as internal locations within all programs that support the present date; the relative date within the 11 year range is stored in the system kernel as the contents of location 07610.  The system kernel value is updated by the P?S/8 DATE command; the other programs generally support printing today's date by reading the current date value in memory and calculating the overall date using the locally-stored base year group value within the image of the program.  As such, all such programs require a binary patch to reflect the desired base year group value.\par
\par
The replacement value to be applied is 6, which corresponds to a date range of 2008 through 2018.  If desired, the base year group value can be updated in the future to 7 to correspond to a date range of 2016-2026.\par
\par
Note: P?S/8 Version 9A will use an entirely different method of implementing the system date.  The entire date will be available in the system kernel; the maintenance procedure described here will become moot.\par
\par
