\cf0 17-May-2017\tab Certain P?S/8 system device handlers located.\par
\par
A significant P?S/8 system device handler file recovery in the form of a single very long source file has been found.  (The file was discovered on an extra-long (3300 block) DECtape; it is too long to fit on standard media.)\line\line Prior to P?S/8 Version 8V, a misguided attempt to keep every handler and related component in one file (along with all the other source code the components interacted with, especially the keyboard monitor) was the chosen method to create (most of) the P?S/8 starter system for any particular supported device; eventually, this impractical approach was abandoned in favor of the current method used in P?S/8 Version 8Z.\line\line The eventual \i portable\i0  approach to developing P?S/8 was carried out in the form of \cf2\highlight3\f0 A\cf0\highlight0\f1  and \cf2\highlight3\f0 B\cf0\highlight0\f1  collections.  This mechanism refers to the likelihood that sites where work might be accomplished (including the possibility of an available lineprinter) were typically organized as either RK05 packs or alternate hardware with comparable disk geometry.\par
\par
Note: OS/8 actually allows logical devices nearly 50% larger, but no larger devices were relevant at the time.\line\line In turn, the \cf2\highlight3\f0 A\cf0\highlight0\f1  and \cf2\highlight3\f0 B\cf0\highlight0\f1  files were stored on three designated DECtapes each; recovered OS/8 BATCH control files were developed to manage the file collection in this manner.\page\cf1 P?S/8 Version 8Z Change Log (continued).\cf0\line\line Eventually, the full complement of P?S/8 source code files could not fit into this limited structure; as such, certain file management problems surfaced leading to the need to recover several sets of specific device handlers (and related utilities).\par
\par
The MDC8 P?S/8 hard drive handler (Winchester) was never included in this collection, in part because the author's personal system had grown to be more than adequate, and primarily based on the MDC8 itself.  As such, the OS/8 logical volumes were the largest allowed in OS/8 (and thus nearly 50% larger than the RK05 logical size) where this was no longer a problem (but was prohibitive in terms of backup DECtapes).\line\line To date, This hard drive handler has not been recovered; however, the process to achieve equivalent source code using other recovered source files is easily accomplished given the high degree of internal documentation within the other handlers, and the elegant nature of the MDC8 itself.  In particular, MDC8 issues post-date the other considerations discussed here.\par
\par
The PQSMON.OL file has been analyzed as follows:\par
\par
a) All of the then-present handlers (some not even complete) are\par
   present; the file appears to correspond to P?S/8 Version 8U.\line\line b) A string of conditional assembly variables are used to choose which\par
   particular system should be assembled.  This roughly corresponds to\par
   the current method of creating P?S/8 for the same components.\par
\par
   Note: The current method has been expanded to also include adding\par
   in the P?S/8 DUMP and P?S/8 ODT components to create a more viable\par
   starter system capable of being patched when booted to; this would\par
   have been a necessary portion of a potential fixup when upgrading a\par
   starter system to a more complete system by adding common system\par
   programs.\cf1\par
\cf0\par
c) Conditionals exist to create a variety of "lost" systems including\par
   the unmodified LINC-8 requiring 8K, the modified LINC-8 requiring\par
   4K, the TD8E requiring 8K and the TD8E with 4K and the\par
   MR8E-C ROM (located at the end of memory in field 7 7400-7777).\par
\par
   Additionally, several other systems are present (such as the TC08)\par
   which are already available as modular system devices; when\par
   creating a starter system, the appropriate device handler complex\par
   is loaded along with the other binary files to create a starter\par
   system for that device.\page\cf1 P?S/8 Version 8Z Change Log (continued).\par
\cf0\line    While the recovery of these source file sections is extremely\par
   important, further work is needed to make use of them:\par
\par
a) There are some internal differences between Version 8U and Version\par
   8Z that must be resolved.  This is considered a moderate amount of \par
   work (but hardly insurmountable).  Much of this has to do with the\par
   newer modular approach and the specific environment described by\par
   the present starter system scenario.\line\line    Note: The entire method of creating P?S/8 is subject to change\par
   involving using some never-before-used methods that are now under\par
   consideration.\par
\par
b) All modular P?S/8 system device handler source files include\par
   interception routines associated with the \ul Logical Console Overlay\ulnone .\par
   At the time of this earlier work (nearly six years before Version\par
   8Z), this was not (yet) an established method.  As such, each\par
   handler must have new work applied to be fully compatible.\line\line c) A nearly-functioning P?S/8 system for the TD8E and MR8E-C has been\par
   recovered (and booted both on the actual hardware and on the SIMH\par
   simulator).  This has led to the discovery of an unresolved major\par
   problem (that some work has been done on to achieve a successful\par
   replacement device handler).  Assembling the identical\par
   configuration (using the \i PQSMON.OL\i0  file) produces the same exact\par
   system device handler (and identical bugs).\par
\par
   Note: The source code for the MR8E-C was previously recovered by\par
   direct disassembly of the ROM contents.  While arguably better\par
   documented, the original code (from the \i DEC\i0  author) is apparently\par
   available within the PQSMON.OL file.  It was common practice to\par
   develop the P?S/8 handler on machines with 32K; as such, the ROM\par
   image was (typically) loaded into RAM for testing.\par
\par
   The author recollects a special interaction between the TD8E and\par
   the vT8E-specific variant of the \ul Logical Console Overlay\ulnone .  This\par
   involves limiting the DMA overhead of the VT8E to an 8 character\par
   display of the word \cf2\highlight3\f0 WAITING\cf0\highlight0\f1  (using the blink attribute on every\par
   character).  To avoid rederiving old work, the recovery\par
   process will continue to investigate this particular DECtape\par
   image.\line\line This P?S/8 system is from a time when two major differences apply as compared to newer releases:\line\line a) There is no way to determine the exact release revision.\line\line    Note: This release is estimated to have been created circa 1974.\page\cf1 P?S/8 Version 8Z Change Log (continued).\cf0\line\line b) The \ul Logical Console Overlay\ulnone  was not created originally in the\par
   present form.  This system has the forerunner \i VT\i0  program that is\par
   VT8E-specific and not capable of configuration.\line\line    As such, disassembly of the VT program will be required; hopefully,\par
   this feature can be located within the VT code, and then recreated\par
   as an add-on to the current \ul Logical Console Overlay\ulnone .\line\line    Note: This issue is only related to the TD8E hardware due to the\par
   critical timing nature of the (primitive) DECtape controller).\par
\par
   From the author's recollection, it is not likely the VT program\par
   interaction was ever applied to the TD8E (with extended memory)\par
   variation.  There will be some differences between the two\par
   variant systems that may require slightly different implementation.\par
\par
   When using the extended-memory implementation, all device\par
   handler code can be intercepted as required; the ROM version\par
   requires the VT8E screen messaging to be limited for a much larger\par
   operational phase of the overall handler due to the preponderance\par
   of close-ended ROM-based code.  Since the ROM is not used in this\par
   variant, this will allow the screen to be used during the search\par
   phase with a longer screen message; the overhead of DMA cannot have\par
   as much impact on the block search code (as compared to having to\par
   be limited until nearly all of the ROM-oriented code has\par
   completed).\par
\par
17-Sep-2018\tab P?S/8 for RX01 (and related devices) created.\par
\par
Using the TC01/TC08 version of P?S/8 Version 8Z for most of the system blocks needed, the RX01 version was generated taking advantage of the advanced configuration features of P?S/8 for TC01/TC08.\line\line There may be problems with the RX01 handler feature of attempting to create a viable handler for the purpose of writing all of the starter system blocks by creating an in-place fixed-up version of the handler in memory; this coding likely was never tested.\par
