Glossary of Terms\par
\par
Note: Throughout this Glossary all terms used within term descriptions are marked with index numbers (such as DEC\sub 14\nosupersub ) for clarity and brevity.\par
\par
Certain terms are included regarding P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  or PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  topics beyond the scope of this document.  (This section is considered the Master Glossary for all P?S/8 documentation.)\par
\par
  1.\tab ASCII\par
\par
\tab The American Standard Code for Information Interchange (ASCII) is\par
\tab an encoding scheme widely used throughout the computer industry.  \tab The ASCII codes are used to represent the complete printable\par
\tab character set (and related non-printing control characters).\par
\par
\tab In more recent years, ASCII is often known as \i US-ASCII\i0  because of\par
 \tab its origin in the United States of America.  Variations exist for\par
\tab other countries in part due to localized character requirements.\line\line\tab On certain systems, the ASCII code is expanded from a seven-bit\par
\tab encoding (with 128 unique cases) to a more general code expressed\par
\tab in as many as 16-bits per character element.\par
\par
\tab Note: Certain extended character encoding methods take advantage\par
\tab of the high likelihood that the contents of files will tend to\par
\tab stay within the same eight-bit subset for most of the file; as\par
\tab such, case shift codes are reserved for the implementation of \tab generally infrequent code page changes.  This allows the overall\par
\tab file structure to support eight-bit bytes instead of groups of 16\par
\tab (or more) bits.\par
\par
\tab Most PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  operating systems support seven-bit ASCII encoding \tab or a subset code (such as upper-case only characters) which can\par
\tab be expressed in six bits.  Where relevant, there may be issues\par
\tab regarding an eighth storage bit based on the use of paper-tape\sub 99\nosupersub  \tab standards or an eight-bit byte storage method.\par
\par
  2.\tab BIN\par
\par
\tab The standard binary loading format in the DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  \ul Paper-tape\i\par
\ulnone      \ul\i0 operating system\ulnone\sub 99\nosupersub  is known as DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  \i BIN\i0  format (which is\par
\tab optimized for loading binary data from punched paper-tape\sub 99\par
\nosupersub\tab frames).\par
\par
\tab Data stored in DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  BIN format includes both leader and \tab trailer frame sections (to indicate overall sets of data).  The\par
\tab highest tape channel is used as a visual aid to help position\par
\tab paper-tapes\sub 99\nosupersub  in tape reader stations; all leader and trailer\par
\tab frames have only the highest channel punched.  Data frames may or\par
\tab may not have this channel punched; however, some combination of\par
\tab the other channels will be punched throughout the entire data \tab section.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab When reading in binary paper-tapes\sub 99\nosupersub , all leader frames are\par
\tab ignored.  Memory loading ends when the first trailer frame is\par
\tab encountered.  DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  BIN format incorporates all necessary\par
\tab elements needed to load 12-bit words into memory including a 12-\par
\tab bit checksum at the end of the tape implemented as two adjacent\par
 \tab frames; memory field change frames are included as required.\par
\par
\tab Note: field change frames have the two highest channels punched. \tab Additionally, field change frames are not included in the overall\par
 \tab tape checksum.  It is surmised this came about because the DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  \tab BIN format was upgraded when machines larger than 4K memory were\par
\tab first introduced; this could have caused compatibility issues\par
\tab with DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  BIN format tapes produced somewhat earlier.\line\line\tab DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  BIN format is inherently compromised in terms of loading\par
\tab efficiency because of the need to support leader, trailer and \par
\tab checksum codes that are superfluous in an operating system\par
\tab environment (such as P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub ).  Storage devices inherently have\par
\tab device-level error checking for all data; leader and trailer\par
\tab frame codes serve no purpose when data is stored in blocks on a \tab mass storage device.\par
\par
\tab While certain systems use an adaptation of DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  BIN format for\par
\tab binary files, P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  uses the more versatile and efficient \tab Slurp\sub 99\nosupersub\i  \i0 binary file loading format.\par
\par
 \tab Slurp\sub 99\nosupersub  binary file format is optimized for efficient loading.\par
 \tab The P?S/8 BIN\sub 99 \nosupersub system program includes functions to interchange\par
\sub\tab\nosupersub between binary paper-tape\sub 99\nosupersub  data and P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  binary files (in both\par
\tab direction of conversion) as well as the ability to load the\par
\tab contents of Slurp\sub 99\nosupersub  format binary files into memory.\par
\par
\tab Note: Throughout this document, BIN (with no direct reference to \tab DEC\sub 14\nosupersub ) refers to the P?S/8 BIN\sub 99\nosupersub  utility.  Detailed information\par
\tab about P?S/8 BIN\sub 123\nosupersub  can be obtained from other documents such as\par
\tab the \ul\i P?S/8 System Programs Guide\ulnone\i0  available separately.\par
\par
  3.\tab Binary Compatibility\par
\par
\tab In general, binary compatibility refers to the principle that,\par
\tab to the extent possible, sections of one program are identical to\par
\tab the corresponding sections of another program derived from the\par
\tab first program.\par
\par
\tab When one program is created from another program (through the use \par
\tab of overlays or other means), maintaining binary compatibility\par
\tab guarantees that additional program modifications or patches\par
\tab designed for the first program will be functional when applied to \par
\tab the corresponding sections of the second program.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\cf0\line\line\tab P?S/8 FOCAL\sub 99\nosupersub  is based on FOCAL, 1969\sub 99\nosupersub ; all modifications made\par
\tab to support FOCAL, 1969\sub 99\nosupersub  maintain binary compatibility to the\par
\tab fullest extent possible.  This guarantees support for patches\par
 \tab created by members of the FOCAL\sub 99\nosupersub  user community at large.\par
\par
\tab Failure to maintain binary compatibility is a serious problem\par
\tab of other releases such as FOCAL-8\sub 99\nosupersub  (aka FOCAL-71\sub 99\nosupersub ); this release\par
 \tab also exhibits additional problems beyond binary compatibility\par
\tab (related to deliberate attempts at sabotaging products made by a\par
\tab competitor).\par
\par
