\par
  7.\tab Central Processing Unit\par
\par
\tab The Central Processing Unit (CPU) is the term for the general\par
\tab logic and arithmetic section of a digital computer system.\par
\tab Memory and peripheral devices are often interfaced to the CPU in\par
\tab practical systems such as the PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub .\par
\par
\tab Most notably, the LINC-8\sub 99\nosupersub  includes a second CPU for the LINC\sub 99\nosupersub  \tab instruction set.  The main CPU is a modified version of the \tab standard PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  CPU which is used for support functions when the\par
\tab LINC\sub 99\nosupersub  CPU is running.\par
\par
\tab When the LINC\sub 99\nosupersub  CPU is disabled, the PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  CPU can be used for\par
 \tab (nearly) all functions associated with the original PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  \tab model.\par
\par
  8.\tab Command-line option switches\par
\par
\tab In various operating systems (for several DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  computers),\par
\tab program options are often expressed as \ul Command-line option\i\par
\ulnone\tab\ul\i0 switches\ulnone  as deemed necessary by the user.\line\par
\tab The general implementation refers to a collection of bits\par
\tab organized into words of system-dependent length; unreferenced\par
 \tab bits are set to zero while referenced bits are set to one.\par
\par
\tab On the PDP-10\sub 99\nosupersub  TOPS10\sub 99\nosupersub  operating system, each of the 36\par
\tab alphanumeric characters (A-Z and 0-9) are expressed as described\par
\tab above, all within a single 36-bit word.  On the PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub , a series\par
\tab of three 12-bit words is used to express the equivalent\par
\tab information; this convention applies to both OS/8\sub 99\nosupersub  and P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub . \tab The only difference between the two operating systems (with\par
\tab regard to passing \ul command-line option switches\ulnone ) is the specific\par
\tab address range used within each system kernel area.\par
\par
\tab\ul Command-line option switches\ulnone  can be specified using one of two \tab equivalent methods as follows:\par
\par
\tab a) Individual \ul command-line option switches\ulnone  are preceded by the \highlight1 /\highlight0\par
\tab    character such as:\par
\par
\tab    .DUMP \highlight1 /C\page\cf3\highlight0 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab    Multiple \ul command-line option switches\ulnone  may be invoked on the\par
\tab    same command line by using the same method with different\par
\tab    option switches.  Spacing characters are not required between\par
\tab    command sections; however, any number of spacing characters\par
\tab    can be freely used for clarity if desired.\par
\par
\tab    Note: Spacing characters include \highlight1 <SP>\highlight0 , \highlight1 ,\highlight0  and \highlight1 <HT>\highlight0 .\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab    In this example, P?S/8 DUMP\sub 99\nosupersub  is executed; because the \highlight1 /C\highlight0\tab\par
\tab    \ul command-line option switch\ulnone  was set, a check is made for\par
\tab    potentially available additional memory.\par
\par
\tab    If more memory is available (than is limited by the current\par
\tab    \ul Logical Core Size\ulnone\sub 99 \nosupersub setting), P?S/8 DUMP\sub 99\nosupersub  will utilize some\par
\tab    portion of the additional memory space.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab    The exact amount of additional memory potentially available is\par
\tab    dependent on the system configuration.  Either 3/4 of an\par
\tab    additional memory field x (x0000-x5777) will be available or\par
\tab    the entire additional memory field (x0000-x7777) depending on\par
\tab    system specifics.  This will allow P?S/8 DUMP\sub 99\nosupersub  to read (and\par
\tab    write) additional contiguous blocks in one TR\{ansfer\} command\par
\tab    than is normally possible in 4K memory.\par
\par
\tab    .DUMP \highlight1 /C\highlight0  \highlight1 /S\cf3\highlight0\par
\cf0  \par
\tab    Since the \highlight1 /C\highlight0  \ul command-line option switch\ulnone  is set as in the\par
\tab    previous example, the test for additional memory is performed\par
\tab    as described above.\par
\par
\tab    Because the \highlight1 /S\highlight0  \ul command-line option switch\ulnone  is also set, the\par
\tab    computer front panel switches are ignored when determining the\par
\tab    relative starting point of various octal dump printouts.\par
\par
\tab    Since the \i =xxxx \i0 command-line option was not specified, the\par
\tab    command is equivalent to explicitly using =0000 (which causes\par
\tab    all locations to be printed).\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab b) Multiple \ul command-line option switches\ulnone  are preceded with \highlight1 (\highlight0  and\par
\tab    optionally followed by \highlight1 )\highlight0  to close the current command line\par
\tab    section.\par
\par
\tab    .DUMP \highlight1 (\highlight0 CS\highlight1 )\highlight0  \par
\par
\tab    This produces the identical execution results as in the\par
 \tab    previous example.\par
\par
\tab\ul Command-line option switches\ulnone  may be passed redundantly in either\par
\tab format without causing command errors.\par
\par
\tab Note: For detailed information about P?S/8 DUMP\sub 99\nosupersub , consult the\par
\tab\ul P?S/8 System Programs Guide\ulnone  available as a separate document.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
  9.\tab COS-300 and COS-310\par
\par
\tab The DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  Commercial Operating System (COS) is a PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  operating\par
\tab system primarily oriented towards the use of the DIBOL\sub 21\nosupersub  \tab language.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab The initial release was known as DIBOL-8\sub 22\nosupersub  which is actually a\par
\tab thinly-disguised variant of the \ul R-L Monitor System\ulnone\sub 99\nosupersub ; the two-\par
\tab character keyboard monitor commands\sub 99\nosupersub  were modified for the\par
\tab benefit of concise execution of projected utilities planned for\par
\tab the next release.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab When COS-300 (and later COS-310) were released, the actual\par
\tab implementations were then-current variants of OS/8\sub 99\nosupersub  with an \tab alternate command layer expanding on compatibility with\par
\tab DIBOL-8\sub 99\nosupersub .\par
\par
\tab A notable difference between COS and OS/8\sub 99\nosupersub  is the redefinition\par
\tab of the base year for the system date from 1970 to 1974; this was \tab done expediently to avoid dealing with the impending expiration \tab of the OS/8\sub 99\nosupersub  year (which was never properly remedied).  As a \tab result, the reckoning of file dates is complicated by having to \tab factor in the proper base year depending on which operating \tab system is in effect.\par
\par
\tab Note: Certain programs include Byzantine internal patches to\par
\tab attempt to determine which operating system is in effect to allow \tab common program images that function when run from either system.\par
\tab Many OS/8\sub 99\nosupersub  programs cannot run from COS (and vice-versa).  Due\par
\tab to command processor differences, program execution requires \tab alternate command structure when attempting to accomplish\par
\tab identical functions; in some cases, the program must be located\par
\tab on the system bootup device when run from COS systems.\par
\par
\tab Certain COS releases are specifically designed for PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  \tab execution only, while others were released for use on various\par
\tab DECmate\sub 99\nosupersub  models.  This is an issue beyond media-based\par
\tab differences (several DECmate\sub 99\nosupersub  models support the RX02\sub 99\nosupersub  drive\par
\tab using the same format as similarly-configured PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  systems).\par
 \tab All DECmates\sub 99\nosupersub  share identical console hardware incompatibility\par
\tab with all PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  models.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab With the exception of very early releases, most PDP-8\sub 99 \nosupersub COS\par
\tab releases cannot run on Pre-OMNIBUS\sub 99\nosupersub  systems; this is the\par
\tab consequence of various managerial edicts which led to the hiring\par
\tab of incompetent maintenance programmers incapable of understanding\par
\tab the relevant issues.  As such, instructions incompatible with\par
\tab earlier models can be found throughout the program code.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: A competent programmer can remedy the incompatibility \tab problems at the source code level.  In general, little \tab constructive use is made of the newer instructions; compatible\par
\tab alternatives are generally easy to apply.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
 10.\tab Computer Special Systems\par
\par
\tab At various times, certain DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  customers requested special\par
\tab hardware \tab projects (perhaps including support software) to solve\par
\tab specific computer issues (often recommended by DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  sales \tab personnel).  The DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  division responsible for these projects\par
\tab was known as \ul Computer Special Systems\ulnone  (CSS).  In some instances, \tab DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  management might deem the solution worthy of marketing\par
\tab within an appropriate product line.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: The original customer was charged for all expenses incurred \tab in creating their project; however, if DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  marketed the\par
\tab product, the customer did not receive credit in any form (either\par
\tab recognition or some form of discount).\par
\par
\tab A notable CSS product is the RKS8E\sub 99\nosupersub  which is a software-\par
\tab compatible upgrade of the RK8E\sub 99\nosupersub  hard disk controller for\par
\tab OMNIBUS\sub 99\nosupersub  machines.  Included improvements are as follows:\par
\par
\tab a) Support for additional disk drive units 4-7; the standard\par
 \tab    RK8E\sub 99\nosupersub  only supports disk drive units 0-3.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab    Note: There are software issues with the support of the disk\par
 \tab    drive unit extension unless software is expressly written for\par
 \tab    the extended configuration.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab b) In order to avoid rotational latency, data transfer to the\par
\tab    next sector on the current track must be initiated within\par
 \tab    100 microseconds after the done flag raises from the previous \tab    transfer; this is often difficult to achieve when using real-\par
\tab    time interrupt-driven programming for data acquisition.\par
\par
\tab    To remedy this, a 4096 word control register is added that\par
\tab    implements automated transfers that must be a multiple of 256\par
\tab    12-bit words (which is the physical size of the disk sector).\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: By the use of sophisticated interrupt-handling techniques,\par
\tab it is possible to transfer data in real-time on the standard\par
\tab RK8E\sub 99\nosupersub ; however, CSS customers, while expecting a quality\par
\tab solution (in every sense) from DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  (and by extension CSS),\par
\tab often paid for a result that was a clumsy hardware-centric\par
\tab solution.  This may or may not have been the best solution for\par
\tab the customer, but it was always the best outcome for the internal\par
\tab CSS cost center.  In the case of the RKS8E\sub 99\nosupersub , it is surmised the\par
\tab support for disk drive units 4-7 was added merely to allow the\par
\tab product to be more marketable (since this aspect of the RKS8E\sub 99\nosupersub  \tab was not requested or purchased by the original customer).\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
 11.\tab Data Break\par
\par
\tab Nearly all DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  computers support Direct Memory Access\sub 99\nosupersub  (DMA\sub 99\nosupersub ) \tab peripheral devices.  Many DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  engineering documents refer to \tab this data transfer method as Data Break.\par
\par
\tab All PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  models (with the exception of the LINC-8\sub 99\nosupersub ) support \tab Data Break peripheral devices.  A distinction is made between \tab single-cycle and three-cycle Data Break devices:\par
\par
\tab a) Single-cycle Data Break Devices transfer data between the\par
 \tab    device and memory in a single cycle per 12-bit word\par
 \tab    transferred.  The peripheral device is responsible for\par
\tab    management of all data transfers.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab b) Three-cycle Data Break devices designate hard-wired memory\par
\tab    addresses reserved to manage data transfers; this tends to\par
 \tab    standardize the Data Break operations and simplify the\par
 \tab    peripheral device implementation.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab    For example, the TC01\sub 99\nosupersub  DECtape\sub 99\nosupersub  controller reserves memory\par
\tab    locations 07754-07755.  During a typical transfer, the\par
 \tab    contents of 07754 (known as the Word Count Register) are set\par
 \tab    to the two's complement of the desired number of words to\par
 \tab    transfer.  The contents of 07755 (known as the Current\par
 \tab    Address Register) are set to one less than the designated \par
\tab    memory address for the data transfer.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab    Note: The memory field for the Data Break is set by separate\par
 \tab    hardware within the peripheral device generally known as the\par
 \tab    Break Field Register.\par
\par
\tab    Once the data transfer is initiated by software, each data\par
 \tab    transfer proceeds when the controller is ready to obtain\par
\tab    multiple memory cycles from the CPU\sub 99\nosupersub . The first transfer\par
 \tab    operation increments the Word Count Register; if the contents\par
\tab    changes from 7777 -> 0000 a condition known as Word Count\par
\tab    Overflow occurs, which ends the entire Data Break transfer\par
\tab    operation.\par
\par
\tab    Assuming Word Count Overflow does not occur, the next transfer\par
\tab    operation increments the contents of the Current Address\par
\tab    Register; in conjunction with the Break Field Register, a data\par
\tab    transfer operation is initiated on the third cycle.  A 12-bit\par
\tab    data word is transferred between the controller and the\par
\tab    designated memory location.  Word Count Overflow (eventually)\par
\tab    occurs ending all data transfers; this can be detected by the\par
\tab    transfer initiating program (which continues execution when\par
\tab    Data Break operations are not in effect).  In most Data Break \tab    peripherals, the Word Count Overflow (and any associated\par
\tab    errors) can be detected by designated flag and interrupt\par
\tab    conditions.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab Depending on peripheral bus design, various converters may be\par
\tab required to interface specific devices to newer computer models.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab Certain OMNIBUS\sub 99\nosupersub  peripherals perform additional Data Break\par
\tab cycles as part of their operational design.  For example, the\par
\tab DP8E synchronous modem interface can check each data word\par
\tab transferred for a character match against as many as four\par
\tab possible values; this simplifies the implementation of various\par
\tab synchronous data protocols which require special attention when\par
\tab specific control characters are detected.\par
\par
\tab Note: All DECmates\sub 99\nosupersub  (past the DECmate I\sub 99\nosupersub ) do not support Data\par
\tab Break operations.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
 12.\tab DCC-112\par
\par
\tab The DCC-112 is a 12-bit computer manufactured by \ul Digital Computer\ulnone  \tab\ul Controls\ulnone\sub 99\nosupersub , a would-be competitor to DEC\sub 14\nosupersub .  The DCC-112 is\par
\tab compatible with the DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  PDP-8/L at the interface level, but is \tab known for better performance at a lower price.\par
\par
\tab Instead of engaging in fair market competition, DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  management\par
\tab decided to sabotage various software products including OS/8\sub 99\par
\nosupersub\tab and FOCAL-71\sub 99\nosupersub .\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: The affected OS/8\sub 99\nosupersub  release was eventually withdrawn (in \tab part) because of a prank perpetrated on Charles Lasner\sub 99\nosupersub  by \tab various members of the DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  12-bit programming group.  This \tab involved a fictitious Software Performance Report (a common often\par
\tab informal method whereby customers could send surface mail to\par
\tab DEC\sub 14\nosupersub ) which claimed the sabotage code failed to booby-trap the\par
\tab software when run on the DCC-112.  This was done without the\par
\tab knowledge or consent of Mr. Lasner\sub 99\nosupersub , which led to his temporary \tab banishment from all DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  properties until some time after\par
\tab managerial decisions were made to remove the sabotage coding (and\par
\tab the prank was revealed).\par
\par
\tab The existence of the sabotage is evident in surviving OS/8\sub 99\nosupersub  \tab source files indicating the "removal of DCC code" within internal\par
\tab file revision summaries.\par
\par
\tab In the case of FOCAL-71\sub 99\nosupersub , disassembly of the released binary\par
\tab paper-tape\sub 99\nosupersub  reveals the details of the \tab sabotage.  If execution\par
\tab is attempted on the DCC-112, the program will self-destruct in a\par
\tab manner suggesting hardware failure.  Undoubtedly this was done to\par
\tab cause would-be customers to lose confidence in the product.\par
\par
\tab Note: Had the manufacturer of the DCC-112 not gone out of \tab business, this would have likely been the cause of a major \tab lawsuit (and bad publicity) for DEC\sub 14\nosupersub .\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
 13.\tab DCC-112H\par
\par
\tab The DCC-112H was a 12-bit computer design announced by \ul Digital\ulnone\par
\tab\ul Computer Controls\ulnone\sub 99\nosupersub , a would-be competitor to DEC\sub 14\nosupersub .  It was\par
 \tab intended to be significantly faster than any then-current DEC\sub 14\nosupersub\par
\tab PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  model.  (The \highlight1 H\highlight0  would stand for high-speed.)\cf3\par
\par
\cf0\tab Little is known about the DCC-112H other than the intention to be \tab 100% software compatible with the previous model (DCC-112\sub 10\nosupersub ).  \tab Due to product announcement timing, it is surmised the DCC-112H\par
\tab would have been OMNIBUS\sub 99\nosupersub  compatible.\par
\par
\tab In the same time period, DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  distributed bogus specifications \tab for the \ul Extended Arithmetic Element\ulnone\sub 99\nosupersub  option of the forthcoming\par
\tab PDP-8/E\sub 99\nosupersub .  It is surmised this was done to sabotage (and cause\par
\tab production delays of) the DCC-112H to stifle competition.\par
\par
\tab Note: It is unlikely any DCC-112H computers were ever \tab manufactured (beyond prototypes shown to potential customers).\par
