\tab All DECmates have 32K of PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  memory (and 32K of Control Panel \tab memory, a feature unique to machines based on the 6100 or 6120\par
\tab series of one-chip CPU designs).\par
\par
\tab The first model was released as DECmate; however, due to the \tab release of subsequent models, it is now referred to as DECmate I.\par
\par
\tab Unlike the DECmate I, the follow-on models (DECmate II, DECmate\par
\tab III and DECmate III+) require loadable firmware from the boot\par
\tab device to provide additional support; the firmware is common to \tab all three models.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab While the typical configuration of the DECmate II includes a \tab single RX50\sub 99\nosupersub  dual-diskette drive, the system can be expanded to \tab include a second RX50\sub 99\nosupersub  dual-diskette drive.  Primarily for \tab backward compatibility, the RX278 option board can be added to \tab support up to two pairs of RX01/RX02\sub 99\nosupersub  series 8" dual diskette\par
\tab drives.\par
\par
\tab Note: The DECmate II and DECmate III+ also support a hard disk \tab option known as the RD50\sub 99\nosupersub . (The RD50\sub 99\nosupersub  is included in the DECmate \tab III+.)  The DECmate II can support either the RX278\sub 99\nosupersub  option or \tab the RD50\sub 99\nosupersub  option (but not both simultaneously).\par
\par
\tab Option boards for the DECmate II series are incompatible with the \tab DECmate III series.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab The DECmate III models are housed in the smallest chassis ever \tab released by DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  for 12-bit computers; due to limited space, the \tab DECmate III+ has a single diskette drive and a half-height hard \tab disk drive.\line\line\tab Both the DECmate II and DECmate III series support a graphics\par
\tab option board and an option board with a Z80 processor chip\par
\tab capable of running CP/M-80.  An option board for the DECmate II\par
\tab was released which includes an 8086 processor chip as well as the\par
\tab Z80 processor.\cf3\par
\par
\cf0\tab Note: A similar board for the DECmate III series was considered, \tab but was withdrawn as production for DECmates was being\par
\tab discontinued.\par
\par
\tab A major software consideration of all DECmate models is that all \tab interfaces are based on the 6121 support chip; each 6121 chip\par
\tab supports five identical device interfaces.  However, all 6121\par
\tab interfaces are incompatible with the device 03/04 console \tab requirements of all 12-bit models from the PDP-5\sub 99\nosupersub  through the\par
\tab VT78\sub 99\nosupersub .  As such, only bastard subsets of OS/8\sub 99\nosupersub  can be supported\par
\tab (with various system features missing).\line\line\tab Note: Due to superior design, P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  (as of Version 8V) can be\par
\tab run from any DECmate model once a suitable system device handler\par
\tab is made available.  The P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  system device handler for RX01\sub 99\nosupersub  \tab supports the DECmate I (and the DECmate II using the RX278 option\par
\tab board and an extended bootstrap).\par
\par
 \tab Time permitting (and access to a suitable hardware\par
\tab configuration), a functional RD50:\sub 99\nosupersub  hard disk system device \tab handler should be achievable.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: Due to interaction with Master Menu\sub 99\nosupersub , and other \tab considerations, additional utilities will be needed to prepare \tab the hard disk prior to system installation.  OS/278 Version 2\sub 99\nosupersub\par
\tab is not installed conventionally on these systems; instead an \tab image backup of a system is provided with an expedient restore \tab facility.  As such, the configuration is quite arbitrary; the\par
\tab installation of P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  demands greater flexibility for proper\par
\tab operation.\par
\par
 \tab The development of a P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  RX50:\sub 99\nosupersub  system device handler has\par
\tab been deferred until P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  system generation techniques can \tab support certain new features required specifically for this \tab configuration.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab The design size of the extended memory device handler extension\par
\tab (required by certain P?S/8\sub 99\cf3\nosupersub  \cf0 system device handlers) has evolved\par
\tab over the course of years to meet the challenges of various\par
\tab devices as necessary.  The present length of eight additional\par
\tab pages of memory (and a two-page additional area to prepare the\par
\tab system for initial loading and related issues) was increased\par
\tab expressly for the RX50:\sub 99\nosupersub ; due to specific issues of the\par
\tab bootstrap environment, unique problems must be solved that are\par
\tab far more complicated than any other configuration.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: OS/278 Version 2\sub 99\nosupersub  exhibits (documented) fatal flaws caused \tab by failure to overcome these system problems.  As such, execution\par
\tab of certain commands (that function normally on other\par
\tab configurations) causes permanent corruption of system boot\par
\tab diskettes.  One of the goals of P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  RX50:\sub 99\nosupersub  support is the \tab elimination of this serious issue within P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub .\par
