 16.\tab DECtape\par
\par
\tab DECtape is a removable media storage device based on the earlier\par
\tab LINCtape\sub 99\nosupersub  used on the (classic) LINC\sub 99\nosupersub , LINC-8\sub 99\nosupersub  and PDP-12\sub 99\nosupersub  as\par
\tab an embedded subsystem of these machines.\cf3\par
\par
\tab\cf0 Note: While the media used for both DECtape and LINCtape\sub 99\nosupersub  is\par
\tab nominally the same magnetic material and tape width, the two\par
\tab formats differ greatly.  The most obvious difference is that when\par
\tab using DECtapes, the supply reel is mounted on the left side of\par
\tab the tape drive; when using LINCtapes\sub 99\nosupersub , the supply reel is\par
\tab mounted on the right side of the tape drive.  Forward motion for\par
\tab either device is defined as winding the tape media onto the\par
\tab take-up reel (mounted on the other side of the drive).\par
\par
\tab Where applicable, plastic canisters for tape media storage exist.\par
\tab By convention, blue plastic canisters are used for DECtapes while\par
\tab green plastic canisters are used for LINCtapes\sub 99\nosupersub .  (This\par
\tab convention is often violated.)\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab DECtapes, much like LINCtapes\sub 99\nosupersub , are individually block-\par
\tab replaceable devices and can be considered to be analogous to more\par
\tab typical rotating disk drives; however, block placement is far\par
\tab more critical due to the inherent overhead of locating a selected\par
\tab block by traversing all intervening blocks starting from the\par
\tab present tape position.\par
\par
\tab Additionally, there are considerations regarding the choice of\par
\tab initial search direction: Using the wrong initial direction will\par
\tab cause undue delays because of the need to turn the tape around\par
\tab after performing a sufficient undershoot (which may have been\par
\tab avoidable).  As such, more successful operating systems use\par
\tab performance-enhancing techniques when accessing the storage\par
\tab device; less efficient operating systems may take profoundly\par
\tab longer time to accomplish analogous tasks.\line\line\tab A case in point regarding DECtape is the ability to transfer\par
\tab multiple blocks in the same device handler call; once the current\par
\tab block has been transferred in the latest read or write operation, \tab starting a new device handler call must restart the overall\par
\tab operation for the latest desired block (because the motion bit\par
\tab must be cleared at the end of the latest transfer).  Assuming the\par
\tab next desired block is adjacent to the last block transferred\par
\tab previously, the delay in completing the next transfer will be\par
\tab palpable.  As such, more successful software strategies combine\par
\tab multiple block transfers in the same device handler operation to\par
\tab the extent possible.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab LINCtapes\sub 99\nosupersub  can only transfer data moving in the forward\par
\tab direction.  Tapes in either format can search while moving in\par
\tab the reverse direction; however, DECtapes can also transfer data \tab once the desired block is located.  Certain software utilities\par
\tab take advantage of reverse motion data transfers, especially on\par
\tab certain DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  machines based on various word lengths.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab Unlike LINCtapes\sub 99\nosupersub , the count of words in a DECtape block is\par
\tab subject to a restriction of being a whole multiple of 18-bit\par
\tab words.  As such, the standard format for the PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  is 129 12-\par
\tab bit words (or 86 18-bit words).\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: The underlying format of DECtape (first used on the PDP-1\sub 99\par
\nosupersub\tab and PDP-4\sub 99\nosupersub ), was changed with the release of the type 552\sub 99\nosupersub  \tab DECtape controller originally designed for the \i PDP-5\sub\i0 99\nosupersub .  As such, \tab DECtapes for the PDP-1\sub 99\nosupersub  and PDP-4\sub 99\nosupersub  are merely compatible with\par
\tab both systems and cannot share data with other machines that\par
\tab support DECtape.  (A tentative solution to this problem is\par
\tab available on the LINC-8\sub 99\nosupersub )\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab For detailed information on other DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  machines, the reader is\par
\tab directed to various documents on early computer architecture; the\par
 \tab PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  is the first successful DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  12-bit computer based on\par
\tab two's complement arithmetic (which is used throughout the\par
\tab computer industry on virtually all newer systems).\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab With the exception of very early Typeset-8\sub 99\nosupersub  computers, all\par
\tab PDP-8\sub 99 \nosupersub systems equipped with DECtape use a compatible DECtape\par
\tab controller as follows:\par
\par
\tab a) TC01\sub 99\par
\par
\nosupersub\tab b) TC08\sub 99\nosupersub\line\line\tab c) TD8E\sub 99\par
\nosupersub\par
\tab DECtape drives used with the above DECtape controllers are as\par
\tab follows:\line\cf3\par
\tab\cf0 a) TU55\sub 99\nosupersub\par
\par
\tab b) TU56\sub 99\nosupersub\par
\tab\par
\tab Notes for this section:\par
\par
\tab a) The TU55\sub 99\nosupersub  is a single drive that can be set to select as any\par
 \tab    drive in the range of 0-7.\par
\par
\tab b) The TU56\sub 99\nosupersub  is generally a dual drive where each drive can be\par
\tab    individually set to select as any drive in the range of 0-7.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab c) The TU56\sub 99\nosupersub  is also available in a single-drive version known\par
\tab    as the TU56H\sub 99\nosupersub ; while nominally less expensive than the\par
\tab    standard TU56\sub 99\nosupersub , the savings was disproportionate given the\par
\tab    lack of a second drive.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab d) The TD8E\sub 99\nosupersub  simple DECtape controller can only address one or\par
 \tab    two drives on a single TU56\sub 99\nosupersub  chassis within a specific drive\par
\tab    pair (such as drives 0-1); additionally, the chassis must\par
\tab    include special modifications and modules unique to the TD8E\sub 99\par
\nosupersub\tab    configuration.\par
\par
\tab e) The device codes set aside for the TD8E\sub 99\nosupersub  describe a\par
\tab    configuration of up to four TD8E\sub 99\nosupersub  controllers; as such, up to\par
\tab    eight drives can be supported with a superficial resemblance\par
\tab    to a TC01\sub 99\nosupersub  or TC08\sub 99\nosupersub  system in terms of total drive capacity.  \tab    However, each TU56\sub 99\nosupersub  can only select a designated drive pair\par
\tab    (such as drives 6-7 on the fourth controller).  This is in\par
\tab    sharp contrast to the TC01\sub 99\nosupersub  or TC08\sub 99\nosupersub  configurations where any\par
\tab    drive can be set to any unit in the range of 0-7.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab f) The TD8E\sub 99\nosupersub  has many reliability issues and cannot be used with\par
\tab    interrupts enabled.  A prototype controller was produced with\par
\tab    full backward compatibility with the TC01\sub 99\nosupersub  and TC08\sub 99\nosupersub  (known\par
\tab    as the TC8E\sub 99\nosupersub ); however, the TC8E\sub 99\nosupersub  was never marketed.\par
\par
\tab The P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  "standard-bearer" storage configuration consists of\par
\tab the TC01\sub 99\nosupersub  (or TC08\sub 99\nosupersub ) DECtape controller with up to eight TU55\sub 99\par
\nosupersub\tab (or TU56\sub 99\nosupersub ) drives.\par
\par
 17.\tab DECtape Library System\par
\par
\tab The \ul DECtape Library System\ulnone  is a primitive limited-purpose \par
\tab operating system designed to load and save memory images of (up\par
\tab to) the entirety of Field 0 (with the exception of 07600-07777 \tab which is reserved for the system kernel).\par
\par
\tab The only system configuration supported is the TC01\sub 99 \nosupersub (or TC08\sub 99\nosupersub )\par
\tab DECtape controller with a TU55\sub 99\nosupersub  (or TU56\sub 99\nosupersub ) drive.\line\line\tab While not a general-purpose operating system, images of common\par
\tab programs from the \ul Paper-Tape Operating System\ulnone\sub 99\nosupersub  can easily be\par
\tab saved for repetitive loading at a later time.  Commands are\par
\tab available to specify memory ranges during a save operation, list\par
\tab all saved images and delete an existing image.  Saved images are\par
\tab loaded into memory by invoking the (unique) name used when saving\par
\tab the image.\par
\par
\tab While program development using the \ul Paper-Tape Operating System\ulnone\sub 99\par
\nosupersub\tab software is a tedious process, the time spent loading programs is\par
\tab dramatically shortened using the \ul DECtape Library System\ulnone  to load\par
 \tab each program as required.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab The \ul DECtape Library System\ulnone  handles multiple block transfers in a \tab reasonable manner and also provides the basis for bootstrap\par
\tab compatibility for all other PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  operating systems with the\par
\tab exception of the \ul Disk Monitor System\ulnone\sub 99\nosupersub .\cf3\par
\par
\cf0  18.\tab DECUS\par
\par
\tab The \ul Digital Equipment Corporation User's Society\ulnone  (DECUS) was an\par
\tab official arm of DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  used as a repository for user-contributed\par
\tab programs.  With few exceptions (expressly stated by the owners), \tab submission to DECUS was \tab tantamount to transferring all relevant\par
 \tab software rights to\tab DEC\sub 14\nosupersub .\par
\par
\tab This was (arguably) abused when the authors of POLY BASIC\sub 99\nosupersub  were\par
\tab cajoled into submitting their work to DECUS; however, the program \tab was actually written while they were college students prior to\par
\tab their employment at DEC\sub 14\nosupersub .\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: The only DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  contribution to POLY BASIC\sub 99\nosupersub  was to include a\par
\tab presentable user manual with the submission.  However, there was \tab a hidden agenda: DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  was anticipating the marketing \tab of the\par
\tab software (which was later sold as EDU-System 30\sub 99\nosupersub ); the POLY\par
\tab BASIC\sub 99\nosupersub  user manual submitted to DECUS became the basis for the\par
\tab EDU-System 30\sub 99\nosupersub  manual (which included DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  copyright \tab notices\par
\tab and other boilerplate information).\par
\par
 19.\tab DF32\par
\par
\tab The DF32 is a small fixed-head disk for the PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  (and most \tab newer models) supported by a variety of operating systems.  The\par
\tab basic unit contains 32K of storage (the size of the largest\par
\tab standard PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  with full extended memory) and can be expanded\par
\tab using up to three DS32 expander units to a total of 128K of\par
\tab storage.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab While P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  can support the DF32 as the system device, only the\par
\tab largest configuration is of any practical consideration.  It is\par
\tab recommended that P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  usage of the DF32 be deferred until the\par
\tab P?S/8 SHELL\sub 99\nosupersub  is available; this will allow additional file\par
\tab storage on other devices while allowing a very fast system\par
\tab device.\par
\par
\tab Various user projects have yielded emulated versions of this\par
\tab storage system causing renewed interest in the device.  However,\par
\tab for new designs, it is highly recommended the RF08\sub 99\nosupersub  be used due \tab to superior speed and size, especially when expanded to the\par
\tab largest size supported.\line\line\tab Note: It is advisable that DF32 emulation projects include data \tab transfer throttling mechanisms, as certain programs cannot\par
\tab support excessive DMA speed.  Existing user projects fail to obey\par
\tab proper data transfer timing which can affect the P?S/8 Slurp\sub 99\nosupersub  \tab binary file loader, POLY BASIC\sub 99\nosupersub  and other programs.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
 20.\tab DIAL\par
\par
\tab\ul LAP6-DIAL\ulnone  and \ul DIAL-MS\ulnone  are related operating systems for the\par
\tab PDP-12\sub 99\nosupersub .  These systems are partially (and primarily \tab cosmetically) based on earlier operating systems for the LINC\sub 99\nosupersub  \tab and LINC-8\sub 99 \nosupersub implemented using LINC\sub 99\nosupersub  code (as required on the \tab earlier architecture).  The DIAL systems take advantage of the\par
\tab PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  processor capabilities of the PDP-12\sub 99\nosupersub ; the implementation \tab primarily consists of PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  code using LINC\sub 99\nosupersub  mode programming\par
\tab only when addressing LINC\sub 99\nosupersub  subsystem devices (which are directly\par
\tab interfaced via specific LINC\sub 99\nosupersub  instructions).\cf3\par
\cf0\line\tab\ul LAP6-DIAL\ulnone  only runs from LINCtapes\sub 99\nosupersub , whereas \ul DIAL-MS\ulnone  can be\par
\tab configured to include a choice of several hard disk drives that\par
\tab may be available on any particular machine configuration.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
 \tab Either system is started using a standard bootstrap operation \tab sequence to read the system LINCtape\sub 99\nosupersub : A two-word LINCtape\sub 99\nosupersub  read\par
\tab instruction is entered on the front panel switches, then executed\par
\tab by pressing the front panel \highlight1 DO\highlight0  key.  This is followed by pressing\par
\tab the \highlight1 START 20\highlight0  key which reads in the rest of the operating system.  \tab If relevant, this will initiate an internal secondary boot \tab operation to a supported hard disk instead of the system\par
\tab LINCtape\sub 99\nosupersub .\par
 \par
\tab Note: Both DIAL systems can only support a maximum of 8K memory.\par
\tab (The PDP-12\sub 99\nosupersub  supports up to 32K memory.)\line\par
\tab\ul LAP6-DIAL\ulnone  runs in 4K of memory; \ul DIAL-MS\ulnone  requires 8K.  The largest\par
\tab program that can be assembled (and saved) is the entirety of\par
\tab fields 0 and 1 (with few restrictions beyond starting address\par
\tab limitation to one of a few standard possibilities).\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: Unlike PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub -based operating systems, DIAL memory loading\par
\tab (nearly) lacks location-specific restriction; however, all DIAL\par
\tab programs are required to start in one of a few designated \tab starting locations (based on the ability to directly execute tape\par
\tab instructions instead of utilizing subroutine calls).\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab Depending on specific configurations, certain additional memory\par
\tab locations may be reserved such as when a supported disk drive is\par
\tab in use.  For \ul DIAL-MS\ulnone , the restrictions always apply to the \tab highest addresses of Field 1.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab Few (if any) standard programs written for DIAL will negatively\par
\tab impact the optional disk drive support; the worst case scenario\par
\tab would require system reload by the DIAL standard bootstrap\par
\tab operation as described above (perhaps placed in memory locations\par
\tab 04016-04017).\par
\par
\tab Note: A PDP-12\sub 99\nosupersub  front panel reset operation sets the current \tab program memory bank to 04000-05777.  Pressing the front panel\par
\tab\highlight1 START 20\highlight0  key will start the machine at 04020.\par
\par
\tab DIAL restart conventions are in sharp contrast with PDP-8\sub 99\par
\nosupersub\tab standards; memory locations 07600-07777 (and perhaps other memory \tab locations depending on the specific operating system) are \tab reserved as the system kernel.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab DIAL text files can be converted from/to P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  using the P?S/8\par
\tab L6DCON\sub 99\nosupersub  utility.  P?S/8 L6DCON\sub 99\nosupersub  can only constructively run\par
\tab from P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  systems on the LINC-8\sub 99\nosupersub  or PDP-12\sub 99\nosupersub ; the program\par
\tab includes internal LINCtape handlers dynamically selected for \tab either system.\par
  \tab\par
\tab Note: The appropriate handler is determined by CPU discovery.\tab Execution on other hardware configurations will lead to error\par
\tab messages and program abort back to P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub .\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
  \tab More detailed information about P?S/8 L6DCON\sub 99\nosupersub  can be obtained\par
\tab from the \ul P?S/8 System Programs Guide\ulnone  available separately.\cf3\par
\cf0\line\tab Note: With the exception of P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  and OS/8\sub 99\nosupersub , (virtually) all\par
\tab operating systems based on LINCtape\sub 99\nosupersub  use 256 12-bit words/block\par
\tab LINCtapes\sub 99\nosupersub .  Various block count values are associated with\par
\tab either standard or custom tape lengths for any of these systems.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab The total tape capacity was initially based on the restrictions\par
\tab of the LINC\sub 99\nosupersub , which can only address 512 blocks (effectively \tab using 150 feet tape media).  Newer systems written for the\par
\tab PDP-12\sub 99\nosupersub  take advantage of longer tapes; the PDP-12\sub 99\nosupersub  includes\par
\tab extended operation capability to address any existent physical\par
\tab block up to 3777 (octal).\par
\par
\tab Note: Practical limits for DIAL systems are usually 1600 (octal)\par
\tab blocks on 260 foot tape media; however, it is possible to create\par
\tab 2000 (octal) block LINCtapes\sub 99\nosupersub  if selected tape media are used.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
 21.\tab DIBOL\par
\par
\tab DIBOL (Digital Business Oriented Language) is a proprietary \tab computer programming language with structures vaguely analogous \tab to that of COBOL (Common Business Oriented Language).\par
\cf3\par
\cf0\tab The earliest implementations of DIBOL run on various PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub\par
\tab configurations; generally compatible versions exist for other\par
\tab DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  computer systems.\par
\par
 22.\tab DIBOL-8\par
\par
\tab DIBOL-8 is the marketing term for the first implementation of the\par
\tab DIBOL\sub 21\nosupersub  language sold by DEC\sub 14\nosupersub .  Normally it could be presumed \tab this would be a new operating system; however, DIBOL-8 is \tab actually a thinly-disguised variant of the \ul R-L Monitor System\ulnone\sub 99\nosupersub .\par
