 24.\tab Direct Memory Access\par
\par
     Most computer architectures support a facility for peripheral\par
\tab devices to access memory by including a Direct \tab Memory Access\par
\tab (DMA) mechanism.  Where relevant, many DEC\sub 14\nosupersub , engineering\par
\tab documents refer to Data Break\sub 99\nosupersub  transfers between \tab memory and \tab peripheral devices; the terms are generally \tab interchangeable.\par
\par
 25.\tab Disk Monitor System\par
\par
\tab The \ul Disk Monitor System\ulnone  is an ill-conceived operating system for \tab very specific configurations of the PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub .  The internal \tab structures of the \ul Disk Monitor System\ulnone  implement an arguably poor\par
\tab tradeoff of performance versus storage efficiency; most notably,\par
\tab system performance is only tolerable on the few fixed-head disk\par
\tab units compatible with the system design requirements.\par
\par
\tab The list of supported fixed-head disks is as follows:\par
\par
\tab a) DF32\sub 18\cf3\nosupersub\par
\cf0\line\tab b) RF08\sub 99\page\cf3\nosupersub Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\par
\cf0\tab The \ul Disk Monitor System\ulnone  also runs on TC01\sub 99\nosupersub  or TC08\sub 99\nosupersub  DECtape\sub 99\par
\nosupersub\tab using TU55\sub 99\nosupersub  or TU56\sub 99\nosupersub  drives; the DECtape\sub 99\nosupersub  handling can be \tab described as truly horrible.  Because of the internal design\par
\tab limitations, it is impossible to transfer multiple blocks in a\par
\tab single handler call.  As such, each DECtape\sub 99\nosupersub  read/write block\par
\tab transfer operation must perform a new search operation to\par
\tab locate the target block (in the forward direction of motion).\par
\par
\tab Most storage blocks are arranged in a (nearly) adjacent order\par
\tab with no attempt at creating a useful inter-block offset (which \tab would be ignored as the device handler is incapable of taking \tab advantage of it).  As a result, the next call to the device\par
\tab handler will always require a reverse search to a block located\par
\tab before the last block transferred, followed by a turn-around to\par
\tab transfer the desired block in the forward direction; this form of\par
\tab inefficient tape operation is sometimes referred to as either\par
\tab "shoe-shining" or "yo-yo" motion.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: Once this system is observed in operation, it is hard to\par
\tab fathom how DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  could have actually released this software to\par
\tab end users.  Simply by including interleaving in the storage block\par
\tab allocation routines (and coding a better device handler to take \tab advantage of it), performance could have been notably improved\par
\tab (albeit still inferior to all other PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  operating systems).\cf3\par
\par
\tab\cf0 Acceptable (or better) DECtape\sub 99\nosupersub  block search technique is\par
\tab practiced in other PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  operating systems such as OS/8\sub 99\nosupersub ,\par
\tab P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  and the \ul DECtape Library System\ulnone\sub 99\nosupersub  (which predates the \tab release of the \ul Disk Monitor System\ulnone ).  All of these systems \tab support multiple block transfers during a single device handler\par
\tab call, which avoids the turn-around overhead associated with\par
\tab\b every\b0  mono-block tape operation in the \ul Disk \ulnone\tab\ul Monitor System\par
\ulnone\tab environment.\par
\par
\tab Note: An advanced user of both the \ul Paper-tape operating system\ulnone\sub 99\nosupersub  \tab software and the \ul Disk Monitor System\ulnone  observed that a skilled\par
\tab programmer/operator can develop software using the high-speed\par
\tab reader/punch\sub 99\nosupersub  \ul\b faster\ulnone\b0  than using the \ul Disk Monitor \ulnone\tab\ul System\ulnone  running \tab on DECtape\sub 99\nosupersub .\cf3\par
\cf0\line\tab Some of the poor design aspects of the \ul Disk Monitor System\ulnone  were\par
\tab demanded by ignorant DEC managers for inclusion in the system \tab that was ultimately releases as OS/8\sub 99\nosupersub ; as such,  that system's\par
\tab performance was permanently ruined.  It can be shown that all of\par
\tab these notions are superfluous (at best).\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: Despite these important issues, OS/8\sub 99\nosupersub  (which also requires\par
\tab 8K or perhaps 12K memory) performs somewhat better as compared to\par
\tab the \ul Disk Monitor System\ulnone .  The forthcoming P?S/8 SHELL\sub 99\nosupersub  overlay\par
\tab will define a system largely analogous to OS/8\sub 99\nosupersub  with these\par
 \tab design deficiencies eliminated.  The present basic P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  system\par
\tab (as implemented and released as P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  Version 8Z), already\par
 \tab exhibits some of these issues.  The command structure for the\par
\tab basic system and SHELL\sub 99\nosupersub  overlay have a common design base.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab A critical design flaw of the \ul Disk Monitor System\ulnone  is as follows:\line\par
\tab When the system is restarted, a portion of memory is saved\par
\tab\ul\b every\ulnone\b0  time a program exits to 07600; however, only rarely is\par
\tab this feature constructively meaningful: when an image of memory\par
\tab is saved as an executable file.\par
\par
\tab Note: As mentioned above, this design weakness was replicated in\par
\tab OS/8\sub 99\nosupersub  as part of a series of managerial edicts.\par
\par
\tab As a useful comparison, consider the following P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  \tab alternatives:\par
\par
\tab a) P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  system restart at 07600 is a read-only operation; the\par
\tab    system device can be write-locked (for safety) if desired.\par
\par
\tab b) Should the user require the contents of memory be saved,\par
\tab    memory loading can be initiated with the P?S/8 GET\sub 99\nosupersub  command.\par
 \tab    All of Field 0 will be saved in the system scratch area\sub 9\par
\nosupersub  \tab    (which is reserved for this purpose).\par
\par
\tab c) Following the memory save operation as described in b) above,\par
\tab    a read-only system restart as described in a) is performed.\par
\tab    System commands are immediately available to interact with\par
\tab    the saved program image; this includes saving designated\par
\tab    memory locations in Slurp\sub 99\nosupersub  format binary output files using\par
\tab    the P?S/8 BSAVE\sub 99\nosupersub  command,\par
\par
\tab d) Debugging sessions can be initiated using P?S/8 ODT\sub 99\nosupersub  (which\par
\tab    implements a superset of OS/8\sub 99\nosupersub  ODT commands).  If desired,\par
\tab    conforming programs can be run as impromptu versions of\par
\tab    P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  system programs by invoking a single execution\par
\tab    commands\par
\par
\tab    For detailed information on P?S/8 ODT\sub 99\nosupersub , consult the \ul P?S/8\par
\ulnone\tab    \ul System Programs Guide\ulnone  available separately.\par
\par
\tab In the forthcoming P?S/8 SHELL\sub 99\nosupersub  overlay, these concepts will\par
\tab be extended further to allow additional operations consistent\par
 \tab with the SHELL\sub 99\nosupersub  environment; the underlying concepts will be\par
\tab supported at all levels of system implementation.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab This fundamental design flaw (as implemented in the \ul Disk\ulnone\par
\tab\ul Monitor System\ulnone ) is superfluous and potentially risky in routine\par
\tab usage, yet cannot be worked around or prevented.\par
\par
\tab As a further restriction on \ul Disk Monitor System\ulnone  configuration, \tab all supportable storage devices are required to be expressible in\par
\tab 129 word logical blocks; the 129th word is required \tab as a link \tab to the next logical block in every file.\par
\par
\tab Unfortunately, this design can only be implemented on the DF32\sub 99\nosupersub  \tab and RF08\sub 99\nosupersub  fixed-head disks which can be organized on any 12-bit \tab word basis.  PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  format DECtape\sub 99\nosupersub  requires this physical block\par
\tab length (which is likely the inspiration for this closed-end\par
\tab design requirement).\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab Newer PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  storage devices are based on block lengths that are\par
\tab exact multiples of powers of two (12-bit words).  As such, the\par
 \tab\ul Disk Monitor System\ulnone  cannot be implemented on these devices (which \tab are supported by P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub , OS/8\sub 99\nosupersub  and other operating systems).\par
\par
\tab While all other discussed PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  operating systems are not based\par
\tab on storage allocation tables, certain user-written application\par
\tab systems have been created taking advantage of storage allocation\par
\tab schemes; there are no dependencies on odd quirks only found on \tab unusual storage devices.\par
\par
\tab Note: Well designed single-user systems do not require storage\par
\tab allocation tables.  As such, neither OS/8\sub 99\nosupersub  nor P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  \tab include\par
\tab these structures.\par
\par
