 27.\tab DTORG\par
\par
\tab The DTORG directive is a versatile feature of the PAL\sub 99\nosupersub  language \tab that (as of this writing) has gone into disuse.  DTORG\i  \i0 was first\par
\tab implemented on TOPS-10 PAL10\sub 99\nosupersub  for the Typeset-8\sub 99\nosupersub  group to aid in\par
\tab system generation; specific storage blocks can be written with\par
\tab 128 12-bit words per logical block.\par
\par
\tab At the time, the symbolic name \i DTORG\i0  was appropriate as the\par
\tab Typeset-8 bootable application system only runs on PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  format\par
\tab DECtapes\sub 99\nosupersub .  The symbol suggests an \ul\i OR\ulnone i\ul G\ulnone in\i0  (meaning a designated\par
\tab storage block) on \ul\i D\ulnone EC\ul T\ulnone ape\sub\i0 99\nosupersub ; however, the concept can be applied\par
\tab to any application that is oriented towards logical blocks that\par
\tab are 128 12-bit words long.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: The DTORG directive is generally not used in OS/8\sub 99\nosupersub  because\par
\tab the logical records within OS/8\sub 99\nosupersub  are 256 words long; as such,\par
\tab this would require some additional features to create an aligned\par
\tab double-page memory space in the corresponding loading program.\par
\tab Programming for virtually all other PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  operating systems\par
\tab (with the exception of the \ul Disk Monitor System\ulnone\sub 99\nosupersub  which uses 129\par
 \tab words per block) can (theoretically) take advantage of this\par
\tab feature.\line\line\tab The Typeset-8\sub 99\nosupersub  group made unreasonable demands on the design of\par
\tab TOPS-10 PAL10\sub 99\nosupersub  such that binary output intended to be \tab compatible \tab with DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  BIN\sub 2\nosupersub  format, is defective when the DTORG directive is\par
\tab used in the source code.\line\line\tab This situation went largely unnoticed because it has no direct\par
\tab effect on any other TOPS-10\sub 99\nosupersub  program; however, this makes the\par
\tab DTORG directive virtually useless for any other application that \tab might contemplate PAL10 development.\par
\par
\tab Note: The use of the DTORG directive creates incompatible output\par
\tab that cannot be used as part of any application that requires\par
\tab extended memory.  Binary output frames for DTORG are incompatible \tab with field settings used in any program larger than 4K; attempts \tab to load binary paper-tapes\sub 99\nosupersub  containing DTORG frames will cause\par
\tab checksum errors within the DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  BIN\sub 2\nosupersub  loader.  A special loader is\par
\tab required to handle DTORG statements that can only be functional\par
\tab in a strictly 4K environment (such as Typeset-8\sub 99\nosupersub ).\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab Some time later, managerial edicts were made regarding OS/8 \tab PAL8\sub 99\nosupersub , which was seen as the OS/8\sub 99\nosupersub  analog of the TOPS-10 PAL10\sub 99\nosupersub  \tab product.  Lock-step compatibility was considered mandatory\par
\tab regardless of the consequences.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\cf0\tab\par
\par
\tab Note: Taken in context, this is pointless and unjustified.  There\par
 \tab are numerous incompatibilities between OS/8 PAL8\sub 99\nosupersub  and TOPS-10\par
\tab PAL10\sub 99\nosupersub .  For the most part, P?S/8 PAL\sub 99\nosupersub  is fully compatible with\par
 \tab TOPS-10 PAL10\sub 99\nosupersub ; however, when programming for OS/8 PAL8\sub 99\nosupersub  \tab compatibility, certain PAL\sub 99\nosupersub  language elements must be avoided.\cf3\par
\par
\tab\cf0 While the goal was to provide an alternate development\par
\tab environment amenable to Typeset-8\sub 99\nosupersub  programmers, the OS/8 PAL8\sub 99\nosupersub\par
\tab implementation of the DTORG directive was performed in an\par
\tab expedient manner; as such, binary output of OS/8 PAL8\sub 99\nosupersub  cannot be\par
\tab loaded by any OS/8\sub 99\nosupersub  utility if the DTORG directive is present in\par
`\tab the source code.\line\line\tab Note: For the purpose of advancing the development of P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  in\par
\tab general, there are proposed changes to this situation that can\par
\tab satisfy all interested groups (with some cooperation).  For more\par
\tab information, consult the \ul\i P?S/8 PAL Version 8S Technical Guide\ulnone\i0  \tab available as a separate document.\par
\par
 28.\tab DW8E\par
\par
\tab The DW8E is a (partially compatible) bus converter that can be\par
\tab attached to PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  systems that predate the OMNIBUS\sub 99\nosupersub  models.\par
\tab The DW8E-P is for positive bus machines such as the PDP-12\sub 99\nosupersub , the\par
\tab PDP-8/L\sub 99\nosupersub  and positive bus PDP-8/I\sub 99\nosupersub  systems.\par
\par
\tab Note: Positive bus PDP-8/I\sub 99\nosupersub  systems are rare due to the \tab requirement for a large-scale addition to the back-plane wire\par
\tab list; this was added after model production had terminated. \tab However, DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  eventually marketed refurbished systems with all\par
\tab\ul engineering change order\ulnone  (ECO) modifications performed.\par
\line\tab The DW8E-N is for the PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub , LINC-8\sub 99\nosupersub  and the PDP-8/I\sub 99\nosupersub  (with the\par
\tab more common negative bus).\par
\par
\tab Note: The LINC-8\sub 99\nosupersub  cannot provide DMA\sub 99\nosupersub  support for the DW8E-N\par
\tab because of the LINC\sub 99\nosupersub  Central Processing Unit; only the program\par
\tab transfer portion of the DW8E-N is supported.\par
\par
\tab Within the DW8E, the only supported DMA\sub 99\nosupersub  peripheral is a\par
\tab modified version of the RK8E\sub 99\nosupersub  known as the RK8F\sub 99\nosupersub , which is\par
\tab compatible with the RK8E\sub 99\nosupersub  except for the specific DMA\sub 99\nosupersub  interface\par
\tab modifications made for use in the DW8E.\par
\par
\tab Additionally, certain OMNIBUS\sub 99\nosupersub  peripherals support instructions\par
\tab of the form \i 6xy0\i0  which are not supported on the DW8E.  In certain\par
\tab specific instances, this may be problematic.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\cf0\tab\par
\par
 29.\tab Edit buffer\par
\par
\tab The P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  keyboard monitor\sub 99\nosupersub  includes a set of embedded commands\par
\tab for the purpose of editing a text file directly (without loading\par
\tab a separate editing program).  This design feature allows rapid\par
\tab editing of a portion of a larger project that generally extends \tab across several TFS\sub 99\nosupersub  files.  A file can be created from scratch\par
\tab or an existing file can be loaded into the edit buffer (which is \tab an internal memory buffer integral to the keyboard monitor\sub 99\nosupersub ). \tab\tab Files are tentative until the edit buffer contents are saved to a\par
\tab named file in the TFS\sub 99\nosupersub  directory on the system device (or other\par
\tab supported logical device unit).\line\line\tab While there are tradeoffs when comparing this approach to more\par
\tab traditional file editing methods, there is only incidental need\par
\tab for additional storage space during the editing process; most\par
\tab files are updated in the edit buffer and then written back to the\par
\tab same file slot blocks occupied prior to the edit operation.\par
\par
\tab Note: In general, traditional editing schemes require a tentative\par
\tab storage space at least as large as the entire project file\par
\tab because the entire file must exist both in the original and\par
\tab edited forms until the edit session is closed.  By allowing\par
\tab sections of a project to be independently modified, this storage \tab space requirement is eliminated; additionally, each section of \tab the project is small enough to allow an indefinite number of edit\par
\tab operations to be quickly carried out within the edit buffer\par
\tab memory (which can be updated rapidly).\par
\par
\tab Since all modifications are performed in memory, all updates are \tab essentially instantaneous.  Compare this to virtualized buffer\par
\tab implementations such as the embedded editor within the various\par
\tab DIAL\sub 99\nosupersub  systems; certain pathological cases exist (such as \tab removing a single line near the beginning of the file) where \tab palpable delays occur before further editing can continue.  (The\par
\tab user cannot interact with the operating system until the latest \tab editing operation completes.)\par
\par
\tab Certain P?S/8\sub 99 \nosupersub users also create backup copies of important files\par
\tab to ensure the preservation of the file under edit, but this\par
\tab practice is not mandatory.  File names can be pre-allocated in\par
\tab the TFS\sub 99\nosupersub  directory to avoid directory overhead associated with \tab file backup. \par
\par
\tab Note: All files of a project may be saved to an alternate logical \tab unit with the same (or different) file names.  Backing up project \tab files (periodically) in this manner generally makes the creation \tab of individual backup files unimportant; many P?S/8\sub 99 \nosupersub users do not\par
\tab bother with any form of file backup for routine use.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\cf0\tab\par
\par
 30.\tab EDU-System 15-30\par
\par
\tab EDU-System 15-30 is the DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  marketing term for an adaptation of \tab POLY BASIC\sub 99\nosupersub  designed expressly for the TD8E\sub 99\nosupersub  Dectape\sub 99\nosupersub  controller \tab and a TU56\sub 99\nosupersub  (or TU56H\sub 99\nosupersub ) DECtape\sub 99\nosupersub  drive.  Unlike the original \tab product release, the TD8E\sub 99\nosupersub  is the only device supported.\par
\par
\tab The only system configuration option is to select either extended \tab memory (raising the memory requirement from 4K to 8K) or the use \tab of the MR8E-C ROM (which is programmed expressly for the benefit\par
\tab of TD8E\sub 99\nosupersub  systems to limit the need for more RAM-based memory).\par
\par
\tab Note: Despite the obvious technical advantages, EDU-System 15-30\par
\tab was released as a separate marketing product instead of merely\par
\tab upgrading the configuration table of EDU-System 30\sub 99\nosupersub  to add TD8E\sub 99\nosupersub\par
\tab support; however, an incompetent new hire was assigned the task \tab of implementing the modifications as an first-time PDP-8\sub 99\par
\nosupersub\tab project.  It is known that (unofficially) Richard Lary\sub 99\par
\nosupersub\tab personally rendered assistance to ensure the release of a\par
\tab functioning product.\par
\par
 31.\tab EDU-System 30\par
\par
\tab EDU-System 30 is the DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  marketing term for a (nearly\par
\tab unmodified) version of POLY BASIC\sub 99\nosupersub ; as with the original \tab product, the system can be configured for a variety of available\par
 \tab storage devices.  Extended memory is never used.  (This is \tab strictly a 4K system).\par
\par
\tab For more information see DECUS\sub 18\nosupersub , Elekman\sub 32\nosupersub  and POLY BASIC\sub 99\nosupersub .\par
 \par
