 33.\tab Extended Arithmetic Element\par
\par
\tab The \ul Extended Arithmetic Element\ulnone  (EAE) is an optional component of\par
\tab most PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  models to add an additional instruction set known as \tab the OPR III instructions.  Where required, a new hardware\par
\tab register is added known as the \i Multiplier Quotient\i0  (\i MQ\i0 ).\par
\par
\tab Note: All DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  12-bit machines starting with the OMNIBUS\sub 99\nosupersub  series\par
 \tab include the MQ register whether or not EAE is present; some\par
\tab models cannot support EAE, however the basic instructions to \tab manage the MQ register are always present.\par
\par
\tab EAE adds several new instructions to perform multiplication and \tab division, as well as instructions to perform long shifts and \tab normalization, etc.\par
\par
\tab Note: When the PDP-8/E\sub 99\nosupersub  model was announced, bogus EAE\par
\tab specifications were released suggesting an incompatible "new" EAE\par
\tab option.  It is surmised this was done to stifle competition.\par
\par
\tab The actual implementation of the EAE option on the PDP-8/E\sub 99\nosupersub  (and\par
\tab related models released later) is a fully compatible superset of\par
\tab the original EAE; new instructions include a set of double-\par
\tab precision instructions and a mode change mechanism to freely\par
\tab allow all EAE instructions.\page  \cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\cf0\par
\par
 34.\tab Extended-length text files\par
\par
\tab P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  Extended-length files partially resemble TFS\sub 99\nosupersub  directory \tab text files except no line number or line pointer data exists\par
\tab within the file.  Additionally, there are no inherent file length\par
\tab restrictions (other than the applicable limits of the storage\par
\tab device).\par
\par
\tab In general, extended-length files are used in situations where \tab there is no likelihood of further file editing.  Most extended-\tab length files are created directly by various utility programs. \tab Once fully processed, they are generally discarded and made \tab available for other tasks.\par
\par
\tab Since extended-length files tend to be rather long, they are only \tab available on a basis of one extended-length file per logical\par
\tab unit.\par
\par
\tab Note: Due to known restrictions that will be eliminated in the \tab next release after P?S/8\sub  \nosupersub  Version 8Z, access to extended-length \tab files must be carried out by documented procedures that are fully \tab functional, albeit not fool-proof.  For the present, only \tab advanced users should make use of extended-length files to avoid \tab potential data loss.\par
\par
\tab For more information on the use of extended-length files, consult\par
\tab the \ul P?S/8 Keyboard Monitor Command Guide\ulnone  document available\par
\tab separately.\par
\par
 35.\tab Family of 8\par
\par
\tab\ul Family of 8\ulnone  is a DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  marketing term used to euphemisticly \tab describe support in specific software products of all DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  12-\par
\tab bit computers with the exception of the PDP-5\sub 99\nosupersub  and the PDP-8/S\sub 99\nosupersub .\par
\par
\tab The term was inspired by a coincident marketing campaign using\par
\tab the phrase \ul Pieces of Eight\ulnone\sub 99\nosupersub  with a pirate-inspired theme.\par
\par
\tab The underlying technical reasons are the lack of support for\par
\tab certain combined micro-operate instructions (such as complement\par
\tab and rotate operations in the same instruction).  Compatible\par
\tab machines implement all combined micro-operate instructions of the\par
\tab PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub ; however, newer models tend to implement supersets where\par
\tab additional combinations are also valid (such as increment \tab the\par
\tab accumulator and rotate operations in the same instruction).\par
\par
\tab The older PDP-5\sub 99\nosupersub  is not compatible with this requirement and\par
\tab also exhibits additional incompatibilities; curiously, the\par
\tab PDP-8/S\sub 99\nosupersub  model has the dubious distinction of being the only \tab DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  12-bit machine released that is less capable than the\par
\tab previous model.  This is not due to serial logic implementation,\par
\tab but rather failure to support the full \tab original instruction set.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab Nearly all storage-based software packages for the PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  \tab require \ul Family of 8\ulnone  compatiblility; the notable exception is the\par
\tab\ul Disk Monitor System\ulnone\sub 25\nosupersub  which can run on the PDP-8/S\sub 99\nosupersub  using a \tab special (reduced data transfer speed) configuration of the DF32\sub 99\nosupersub  \tab fixed-head disk.\par
\par
\tab The original release of FOCAL, 1969\sub 99\nosupersub  runs on all 12-bit models\par
\tab by including special internal self-modification for the PDP-5\sub 99\nosupersub  \tab and the PDP-8/S\sub 99\nosupersub  which is applied during program initialization.\par
\par
\tab When FOCAL-71\sub 99\nosupersub  was released, all support for models prior to the \tab OMNIBUS\sub 99\nosupersub  was removed; however, no actual benefit from the newer \tab instruction set was implemented.\par
\par
\tab Additionally, a program overlay was made available (at extra\par
\tab cost) to restore \ul Family of 8\ulnone  compatibility to FOCAL-71\sub 99\nosupersub .  This \tab achieves less capability than the original FOCAL, 1969\sub 99\nosupersub  which \tab supported all models released (as well as custom display support\par
\tab for the AX08 laboratory peripheral device).\par
\par
\tab For additional information on withdrawn 12-bit software support,\par
`\tab see OS/8\sub 99\nosupersub  and Pre-OMNIBUS machines\sub 99\nosupersub .\par
\par
