 37.\tab FLP\par
\par
\tab FLP is the group designation for devices attached to the diskette\par
\tab ports of designated SCSI\sub 99\nosupersub  controllers under the control of the \tab MDC8\sub 99\nosupersub  SCSI\sub 99\nosupersub  host adapter for the PDP-8/A\sub 99\nosupersub .  Device handlers are\par
\tab available for both OS/8\sub 99\nosupersub  and P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  written by Charles Lasner\sub 99\nosupersub .\par
\tab The media used are 5.25" HD diskettes as used on PC-AT\sub 99\nosupersub  class\par
\tab personal computers except for the specifics of the diskette\par
\tab geometry.\par
\par
\tab Through a series of conversion programs, image copies of these\par
\tab diskettes have been used with the SIMH\sub 99\nosupersub  simulator and related\par
\tab utilities to recover many important PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub -centric files.\par
\par
 38.\tab FOCAL\par
\par
\tab The FOCAL (\ul\i FO\ulnone rmula\i0  \ul\i CAL\ulnone culator\i0 ) language is a ubiquitous higher-\tab level language implemented on several DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  computer \tab architectures.  The original implementation of FOCAL was written\par
\tab by Richard Merrill, an accounting department employee at DEC\sub 14\nosupersub ,\par
\tab for various models of the PDP-8\sub  \nosupersub series.\par
\par
\tab Mr Merrill created several releases of FOCAL for the PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub .  \tab The last complete release was FOCAL, 1969\sub 99\nosupersub  which includes \tab several optional packages for different configurations (such as \tab an extension to use 8K of memory) as well as overlays for \tab multiple users.  For more information see FOCAL, 1969\sub 99\nosupersub .\par
\par
\tab FOCAL is generally implemented as a brute force interpreter.  For \tab many DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  users, FOCAL serves a similar purpose to BASIC as \tab implemented throughout the entire computer industry.\par
\par
\tab While there are implementations of BASIC for the PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub , none\par
\tab are particularly useful when run on 4K systems unless a storage\par
\tab device is available.  Additionally, the 4K-oriented BASIC\par
\tab operating systems strictly support only BASIC language programs.\par
 \tab As such, these stand-alone bootable application systems are not\par
\tab suitable for general purpose usage.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
  \tab FOCAL, 1969\sub 99\nosupersub  has been interfaced to 4K operating systems such as\par
\tab P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  and the \ul R-L Monitor System\ulnone\sub 99\nosupersub ; while the original paper-\tab tape\sub 99\nosupersub  version does serve certain purposes, the interfaced\par
\tab version provides far more capability.  Additionally, some of the \tab optional packages (such as the 8K extension) can be applied \tab merely by passing the appropriate binary overlay files.  User-\tab written binary patches can also be applied in a similar manner.\par
\par
\tab A major advantage of the interfaced version is the ability to\par
 \tab process FOCAL program and data files limited only by the storage \tab capability of the operating system.  Program examples are \tab availabe to illustrate how certain users implemented specific \tab applications that cannot be expressed in the original paper-\par
\tab tape\sub 99\nosupersub  release.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
 39.\tab FOCAL-12\par
\par
\tab This is a release of FOCAL\sub 38\nosupersub  for the \ul DIAL-MS\ulnone\sub 20\nosupersub  operating system.\par
\par
\tab Since there are several optional packages associated with FOCAL-\tab 12, an alternate binary compatibility\sub 3\nosupersub  \tab standard applies to this\par
 \tab version.\par
\par
\tab It is surmised that FOCAL-12 is based on an internal FOCAL\sub 38\nosupersub  \tab release beyond the complete distribution of FOCAL, 1969\sub 40\nosupersub ; a\par
\tab minor bug was fixed that affected specific performance of the \tab FOCAL\sub 38\nosupersub  "FOR\i "\i0  command.  A new gemeral release was not undertaken\par
\tab due to consequential support requirements regarding the various\par
\tab optional packages (and new documentation); however, the bug fix\par
\tab coding is present within FOCAL-12.\par
\par
\tab Note: The same bug fix is also present in the source code of\par
\tab FOCAL-8\sub 42\nosupersub  (aka FOCAL-71\sub 41\nosupersub ) which does not maintain binary\par
\tab compatibility\sub 3\nosupersub  with FOCAL, 1969\sub 40\nosupersub  (the previous release).\par
\par
 40.\tab FOCAL, 1969\par
\par
\tab FOCAL, 1969 is the most popular version of the original 12-bit \tab FOCAL\sub 38\nosupersub  for the PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub ; it was released circa April, 1969.  \tab FOCAL, 1969 (and several earlier \tab releases) were written by \tab Richard Merrill, a DEC accounting \tab department employee.\par
\par
\tab A large number of patches have been written over the years by \tab many FOCAL, 1969 users to implement various enhancements.  It is \tab important to maintain binary compatibility\sub 3\nosupersub  with the \tab original\par
\tab paper-tape\sub 99\nosupersub  release to ensure all conforming binary patches (not\par
\tab in conflict with each other) will work as expected.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: certain patches must use dynamic memory allocation to \tab locate available program space for such as user-callable\par
\tab functions; this is necessary since FOCAL, 1969 includes two\par
\tab optional trigonometric packages, each of which may or may not\par
 \tab be present.  As such, the start of free memory space for an added\par
\tab function must be dynamically determined.  Proper program\par
\tab relocation techniques must be used to maintain binary\par
 \tab compatibility\sub 3\nosupersub .  Additionally, multiple added functions may be\par
\tab present simultaneously.\par
\par
\tab A well-known source of information on these subjects and related\par
\tab issues is:\par
\par
\tab DECUS FOCAL-17\line\line\tab FOCAL\line\line\tab How to write new subroutines and use internal routines\par
\par
\tab Note: A copy of this document is available at the author's \tab archive website:\line\par
\tab http://www.ibiblio.org/pub/academic/computer-science/history\par
\tab\tab  /pdp-8/FOCL69%20Files/DECUS-FOCAL8-17.pdf\par
\par
\tab Additional information is available for the P?S/8 FOCAL\sub 99\par
\tab\nosupersub implementation (which is essentially a binary-compatible\sub 3\nosupersub  \tab superset of the paper-tape\sub 99\nosupersub  release); more information about\par
\tab P?S/8 FOCAL\sub 99\nosupersub  can be obtained from other documents such as the\par
 \tab\ul P?S/8 System Programs Guide\ulnone  available separately.\par
\par
\tab The author can also provide source code files of various\par
\tab practical modifications made to P?S/8 FOCAL\sub 99\nosupersub  in the form of\par
\tab relocatable subroutines that conform with the FOCAL, 1969\par
\tab environment.\par
