\tab    OPR instruction is created by adding two instructions together\par
\tab    as constants and then adding an additional value to create the\par
\tab    desired instruction; this value is then stored in-line and\par
 \tab    executed.  If the accumulator (and link) change in the\par
\tab    expected way, certain internal pointers within FOCAL-71 are\par
\tab    destroyed; the net effect is that FOCAL-71 tends to self-\par
 \tab    corrupt in a manner suggestive of a hardware failure (to \par
\tab    manipulate users to not consider the competitor's product).\par
\par
\tab    Additionally, (poorly) printed assembly listings of FOCAL-71\par
\tab    take advantage of limitations of PAL8 and CREF that would\par
\tab    suggest the sabotage cod doesn't exist.\par
