21.\tab LINC\par
\par
\tab The classic \ul L\ulnone aboratory \ul IN\ulnone strument \ul C\ulnone omputer (LINC) was developed \tab at the MIT Lincoln Laboratories prior to DEC's release of the \tab PDP-5 (an unsuccessful precursor to the PDP-8).  Both machines\par
\tab were constructed by hand using soldered wires on a backplane\par
\tab which was then populated with standard DEC System Modules.\line\line\tab Most programs written for the LINC can be run on other models \tab such as the LINC-8 or PDP-12; there are restrictions with regard \tab to certain of the features due to certain hardware variations on \tab the newer models, or the need to emulate certain instructions in \tab PDP-8 software.\par
\par
\tab Note: The LINC interrupt feature was not carried into the newer\par
\tab models; the PDP-12 LINC mode interrupt function is patterned\par
\tab after the PDP-8 interrupt implementation.\par
\par
22.\tab LINC-8\par
\par
\tab The LINC-8 is a variant of the original PDP-8 model that includes\par
\tab a (mostly functional) LINC central processing unit added onto a\par
\tab special adaptation of the PDP-8.  Instead of supporting DMA\par
\tab peripherals, the data break channel is directly connected to the\par
\tab LINC processor for maximum instruction speed.\line\line\tab When running classic LINC operating systems (or specific LINC-8\par
\tab variant versions) it is necessary to reserve the first 1024 \tab memory locations for an elaborate interrupt handler and\par
\tab instruction emulator known as the \ul PROG\ulnone ram \ul OF\ulnone  \ul OP\ulnone eration\par
\tab (\i PROGOFOP\i0 ).\par
\par
\tab One of the most notable features of the LINC-8 is the \b physical\b0  \par
\tab presence of the classic LINC front panel.  With few exceptions,\par
\tab the actual implementation is a PDP-8 peripheral device that is\par
\tab primarily handled by PROGOFOP\i  \i0 to allow complete LINC front panel \tab emulation.\par
\par
\tab While LINCtape is implemented within the hardware of the classic\par
\tab LINC, the LINC-8 LINCtape implementation is actually a PDP-8\par
\tab peripheral using the major registers of the LINC CPU for various\par
\tab functions (analogous to the operation of the LINC hardware, but\par
 \tab under PDP-8 software control).\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\cf0\line\line\tab As such, LINCtapes can be used on the LINC-8 that are\par
\tab deliberately incompatible with the LINC in terms of number of\par
\tab words in each \tab block, total number of blocks and even certain\par
\tab block particulars such as checksum conventions.\cf3\par
\par
\cf0\tab Notes for this section:\par
\par
\tab 1) It is possible to repurpose the LINC front panel using\par
\tab    alternate PDP-8 programming.  If the LINC CPU is not running,\par
\tab    many of the registers displayed on the LINC-8 front panel can\par
\tab    be used to create light-chaser display programs.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab 2)\cf3  \cf0 Since PDP-8 software is organized around logical blocks the\par
\tab    size of the PDP-8 page (128 12-bit words), most PDP-8 software\par
\tab    for the LINC-8 is run from 128 words/block LINCtapes (which\par
\tab    the classic LINC cannot read).\par
\par
\tab 3) Due to unforeseen timing limitations of the PDP-8 memory bus,\par
\tab    it is not possible to implement more than 8K of standard PDP-8\par
\tab    memory on the LINC-8;  Additionally, the 8K implementation\par
\tab    requires modification to slow the processor speed to obtain\par
\tab    acceptable reliability.\par
\par
\tab 4) It is not possible to implement P?S/8 in 4K on the \tab LINC-8\par
\tab    unless a series of hardware modifications are implemented.\par
\par
\tab    The modifications were designed by Kurt Metzger of the Cooley\par
\tab    Laboratories of the University of Michigan, Charles Lasner and\par
\tab    David Soergel of Syracuse University.  When fully implemented,\par
\tab    P?S/8 can be implemented in 4K and OS/8 can be implemented in\par
\tab    8K.  Additionally, the modifications allow the ability to\par
\tab    reliably read and write DECtapes using programming written by\par
\tab    Charles Lasner.\par
\par
\tab 5) DECtapes for the PDP-1 and PDP-4 are incompatible with later\par
\tab    DECtape implementation as used on the PDP-5, PDP-8, PDP-9 and\par
\tab    newer computers.  Using additional software, it is possible to\par
\tab    read and write PDP-1/PDP-4 DECtapes on the modified LINC-8.\par
\par
\tab 6) An alternate implementation of P?S/8 can run on the unmodified\par
\tab    LINC-8 with 8K.  Since OS/8 would require 12K for an analogous\par
\tab    system, it is not possible to run LINCtape-based OS/8 on an\par
\tab    unmodified LINC-8 (with 8K).\par
\par
22.\tab LINCtape\par
\par
\tab LINCtape is a removable media storage device originally\par
\tab implemented on the classic LINC and later on the LINC-8 and\par
\tab PDP-12 computers; LINCtape controller support is implemented as\par
\tab an embedded subsystem on all models; however, there are third-\par
\tab party devices that may have some level of compatibility on other\par
\tab machines.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab The DECtape media (developed later) is based on the LINCtape\par
\tab media using identical underlying tape media and reels (with\par
\tab perhaps different tape length); however, the tape format is\par
\tab incompatible (although there are conversion programs available\par
\tab for the LINC-8 and PDP-12).\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
23.\tab Logical Console Overlay\par
\par
\tab The \ul Logical Console Overlay\ulnone  is an optional component of P?S/8.\par
\tab When enabled, all conforming programs will direct all system\tab console (and lineprinter) input/output to subroutine calls to \tab designated  memory locations instead of the usual physical\par
\tab devices.  While the use of the Logical Console Overlay requires\par
\tab the availability of extended memory, certain advanced features\par
\tab become available as described below:\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab a) If the P?S/8 system device handler detects an error, the usual\tab    action is to halt the CPU (often displaying device-dependent\par
\tab    status bits in the accumulator); pressing \highlight1 CONTINUE\highlight0  on the\par
\tab    system front panel will safely retry the latest read/write\par
\tab    operation.  Users need to be familiar with the particular\par
\tab    system device handler in use to gain insight as to what is\par
 \tab    causing the specific problem by examining the contents of the\par
\tab    accumulator (if feasible).\par
\par
\tab    Note: A system option is available to disable the system halt,\par
\tab    resulting in an infinite retry after any error; depending on\par
\tab    hardware particulars, this setting is generally not\par
\tab    recommended.  (Ironically, using this setting causes error\par
\tab    handling to resemble the mediocre error recovery techniques\par
\tab    usually implemented in many OS/8 device handlers.)\par
\par
\tab    When the \ul Logical Console Overlay\ulnone  is enabled, the default\par
\tab    action taken by the system device handler is transformed into\par
\tab    issuing a status report on the system console device (which is\par
\tab    potentially redefined by the \ul Logical Console Overlay\i\par
\ulnone\tab    \i0 configuration to be directed to alternative hardware).  This\par
\tab    generally includes details of the latest read/write attempt.\par
\tab    The user can choose which action to take (at the point of\par
\tab    error) by entering one of the options appearing on the system\par
\tab    console keyboard.  This allows the user to correct the\par
\tab    problem (if possible) and retry the latest read/write\par
\tab    operation, or to abort the latest operation (if necessary).\par
 \tab    Even without having specific knowledge regarding the vagaries\par
\tab    of the current system device, any user can easily manage all\par
\tab    system device handler recovery operations (perhaps after\par
\tab    consulting some device-specific documentation).\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab b) Not every system device handler is capable of optimal\par
 \tab    performance; this is especially true of system device handlers\par
        designed to run on 4K systems (without requiring loading of a\par
        portion of the handler into extended memory).  Due to the\par
\tab    limited memory space on 4K systems, it is often necessary to\par
\tab    compromise performance to obtain basic functionality.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
        When the \ul Logical Console Overlay\ulnone  is enabled, additional\par
        functionality (designed into the system device handler for\par
\tab    this purpose) is also enabled.  This generally implements\par
\tab    additional performance improvements that should overcome the\par
 \tab    original design compromises needed to obtain basic\par
\tab    functionality.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
        Note: On hardware configurations where the system device\par
\tab    handler requires an extended-memory segment, the \ul Logical\ulnone\par
\tab    \ul Console Overlay\ulnone  is designed to load into lower memory\par
\tab    locations within the same (highest) memory field.  As such,\par
\tab    for conforming programs (such as P?S/8 PAL) attempting to\par
\tab    locate extended memory fields, the presence of the \ul Logical\ulnone\par
\tab    \ul Console Overlay\ulnone  does not change the count of fully available\par
\tab    memory fields.  In certain circumstances, programs may take\par
\tab    advantage of the 3/4 memory field available if the \ul Logical\i\par
\ulnone\tab    \ul\i0 Console Overlay\ulnone  is disabled.  For example, P?S/8 PAL may\par
\tab    support up to 768 additional symbols if this memory space is\par
\tab    available.\par
\par
\tab c) Certain configurations of the \ul Logical Console Overlay\ulnone  may use\par
 \tab    alternate console hardware (such as a console terminal with\par
\tab    interface device codes differing from 03/04).  In certain\par
\tab    configurations (based on terminal emulators), an error panel\par
\tab    screen may temporarily replace the normal display of console\par
\tab    output until the error is resolved.\par
\par
