25.\tab MARK12\par
\par
\tab LINCtapes cannot be used on any supporting system until the tapes\par
\tab are preformatted as intended.  On the PDP-12 this is accomplished \tab by a program generally known as \i MARK12\i0 .  This is reminiscent of \tab the original tape formatting process on the classic LINC system \tab which used a front-panel switch labeled \cf2\highlight3\f0 MARK\cf0\highlight0\f1 .  Additionally, the\par
\tab user must press the \cf2\highlight3\f0 MARK\cf0\highlight0\f1  switch on the PDP-12 front panel at the\par
\tab indicated point in the program; this is essentially an upgraded\par
\tab process which is used to safeguard against accidental data loss.\line\line\tab While the original MARK12 program is designed to run under the\par
\tab DIAL operating system(s), an adaptation of MARK12 is available as\par
\tab a P?S/8 system program.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab A notable difference between the original and P?S/8 versions is\par
\tab support for 128-word LINCtapes.  Apparently programmers assigned \par
\tab to produce the DIAL version were not informed that there are no\par
\tab applications for the PDP-12 requiring 129 words/block LINCtapes.\cf3\par
\par
\tab\cf0 Worse still, since alternate versions of MARK12 have generally\par
\tab been unavailable, the design of OS/8 and P?S/8 device handlers\par
\tab for PDP-12 LINCtape had to be compromised to handle potential\par
\tab data corruption that may be caused by the unwanted additional\par
\tab data word.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab While there are no constructive advantages to using the 129 word\par
\tab format, what follows are known downsides over 128 word usage:\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab a) The potential for more data on the tape is hampered by the\par
\tab    presence of the unwanted word in every block; all other things\par
\tab    equal, more physical tape is required for any logical tape\par
\tab    length.\par
\par
\tab b) Unwanted searching overhead applies to all blocks that must be\par
\tab    traversed between the current tape position and the desired\par
\tab    block(s) to transfer.  This effectively makes all tape \par
\tab    operations take more time.\par
\par
\tab c) Since the tape is effectively used more, the overall condition\par
\tab    of tape media and tape guides is needlessly diminished.\par
 \tab    Inevitably, this increases maintenance costs, etc.\par
\par
\tab d) For use with PDP-8 operating systems on the LINC-8, LINCtape\par
\tab    cannot be other than 128 words per block.  The use of 129\par
\tab    words per block causes needless loss of compatibility.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: It is possible to retrofit the P?S/8 version of MARK12 to\par
\tab be compatible with OS/8 or other systems; however, this requires\par
\tab specific source code changes.  P?S/8 MARK12 conforms with P?S/8\par
\tab features such \tab as the \ul Logical Console Overlay\ulnone  and DECmate-\tab specific dynamic modifications to device 03/04 console routines\par
\tab (for the purpose of providing appropriate messages when the\par
\tab program starts).  The main program is hard-coded to ignore these\par
\tab features in part due to its creation as a DIAL application\par
\tab program; as such, it is recommended the useful subset of the\par
\tab program initialization section be retained to achieve a\par
\tab standalone version which can then be transferred to an OS/8 or\par
\tab other operating system.  The most appropriate implementation\par
\tab would use a global conditional parameter to create either\par
\tab version; this would tend to make future program maintenance\par
\tab simpler, etc.  All issues discussed within this section also\par
\tab apply to the TC12F program described elsewhere in this document.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
26.\tab Master Menu\par
\par
\tab\ul\i Master Menu\ulnone\i0  is an umbrella utility for the \ul\i DECmate II\ulnone\i0  with the\par
\tab hard disk option and the \ul\i DECmate III+\ulnone\i0  (which always includes a\par
\tab hard disk).  While totally optional, the installation of \ul Master\par
\ulnone\tab\ul Menu\ulnone  allows the use of multiple operating systems using a text-\par
\tab based menu system to choose options such as the default operating\par
\tab system and ways to override defaults, and various\par
\tab copy/delete/backup/restore functions, etc.\cf3\par
\par
\cf0\tab The DECmate hard disk controller implementation includes named\par
\tab logical volumes; this feature allows easier implementation of a\par
\tab menu-driven system for multiple subordinate operating systems.\par
\tab Since virtually all storage devices for the PDP-8 are generally\par
\tab defined for independent operating systems, the notion of an\par
\tab umbrella system never developed.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab On systems based on the MDC8 SCSI host adapter, it is easier to\par
\tab create a semblance of an umbrella system, in part due to the\par
 \tab large storage capability (which is comparable to the DECmates \tab with hard disks, and can even support far larger disk drives).\par
\par
\tab Charles Lasner developed the rudiments of such a system for the\par
\tab MDC8 known as MENU-8\i .\i0   More information on MENU-8 (and the MDC8 \par
\tab and Charles Lasner) is available elsewhere in this document.\par
\par
