27.\tab MDC8\par
\par
\tab The MDC8 is a SCSI host adapter and storage controller for the \tab PDP-8/A series of hex-wide Omnibus machines made by \ul\i Computer\ulnone\i0\par
\tab\ul\i Extension Systems, Inc.\ulnone\i0  (\i CESI\i0 ).  The \ul\i Disk Maintenance Utilities\ulnone\i0\par
\tab (\i DMU\i0 ), all existing P?S/8 and OS/8 handlers and (the rudiments\par
\tab of) Menu-8 for the MDC8 were written by Charles Lasner (author of\par
 \tab this document).\par
\par
\tab Note: All MDC8 software as described above have been recovered at\par
\tab the time of this writing with the (minor) exception of the P?S/8\par
\tab Winchester disk system device handler.  This exception case will\par
\tab be resolved at some future date when an actual MDC8-based system \tab is available.\par
\par
\tab Fortunately, the internal description and much of the source code\par
\tab in the OS/8 system device handler for the Winchester disk was\par
\tab originally derived from the missing file; as such, it is a\par
\tab derivative process to completely recover all additional source\par
\tab code by selection of source code sections from the other\par
\tab available files.  (Writing the non-system device handler for any\par
\tab device is a relatively trivial matter once the system device\par
\tab handler for the same device is available.)  The primary reason\par
\tab that MDC8 source code recovery is (relatively) trivial is the\par
\tab elegant nature of the MDC8 design.\par
\par
\tab In essence, a SCSI read or write command is formatted out in\par
\tab memory; then the controller is directed to execute the command\par
\tab to the SCSI controller while the PDP-8 waits for the entire\par
\tab process to be completed within the host adapter (which has DMA\par
\tab controller of the Omnibus); as such, the source code is quite\par
\tab small (a design principle from the inception of the MDC8\par
\tab project).\par
\par
\tab When emulating a high-performance disk controller for new work,\par
\tab use of the MDC8 design is highly recommended.\par
\par
\tab Note: The P?S/8 Winchester disk non-system handler was never\par
\tab written; however, this is a relatively straight-forward matter \par
\tab once all other MDC8-related issues are resolved.  Since few users\par
\tab operate MDC8-based systems currently, device handler\par
\tab implementation will be deferred until the release of P?S/8\par
\tab Version 9A; the non-system device handler for all supported\par
\tab devices is being revamped for better support of the P?S/8 SHELL.\cf3\par
\par
\cf0\tab The lone program that currently uses non-system handlers in P?S/8\par
\tab Version 8Z (BLKCPY Version 10f) will also be modified to conform\par
\tab to the new handler standards including double-precision block\par
\tab number support.  (As a stopgap measure, BLKCPY Version 10f\par
\tab supports the FLP diskette device.)\page  \tab\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
28.\tab MENU-8\par
\par
\tab The MENU-8 multiple operating system utility for the MDC8 is an \par
\tab unfinished project for the PDP-8/A with the MDC8 Winchester hard \tab disk subsystem.\line\line\tab Unlike other storage devices for the PDP-8, there is inherently\par
\tab storage space reserved for the MENU-8 routines next to the area\par
\tab at the rear-most storage blocks of the disk already reserved for\par
\tab bad block management.\line\line\tab All OS/8 and P?S/8 handlers for the MDC8 Winchester disk support\par
\tab a one-track (32 blocks of 128 words/block) offset to allow\par
\tab reservation for the bootup/hand-off routines of MENU-8 which can\par
\tab boot the default operating system or use keyboard input to load\par
\tab the keyboard monitor of MENU-8 itself.\par
\tab\par
\tab MENU-8 source code has been recovered; there is sufficient \tab functionality to perform the basic hand-off/reboot function by \tab performing a patch of the double-precision absolute disk record \tab value where the desired operating system is stored.\par
\par
\tab Note: In many instances, the selected operating system will be \tab adjacent to the absolute boot block code (starting at absolute \tab block 000040 or block 000032 decimal).\par
\par
29.\tab MS/8\par
\par
