30.\tab ODT\par
\par
\tab The OCTAL DEBUGGING TECHNIQUE (ODT) is the common name for a\par
\tab series of PDP-8 debugging programs.  Various implementations\par
\tab exist as follows:\cf3\par
\par
\tab\cf0 a) ODT-LO - This is a memory resident version of ODT for use with\par
\tab    the \ul Paper-tape Operating System\ulnone .  This version occupies\par
\tab    relatively low addresses in memory to allow the object program\par
\tab    to occupy higher addresses in memory.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab b) ODT-HI - This is a memory resident version of ODT for use with\par
\tab    the \ul Paper-tape Operating System\ulnone .  This version occupies\par
\tab    relatively high addresses in memory to allow the object\par
\tab    program to occupy lower addresses in memory.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab    Note: Both ODT-LO and ODT-HI are somewhat difficult to use due\par
\tab    to the arbitrary nature of their intrusion into the program\par
 \tab    design.  (It would have been far better if ODT could be\par
\tab    assembled into available memory space on a case-by-case\par
\tab    basis.)\par
\par
\tab c) OS/8 ODT - This is an "invisible" implementation of ODT that\par
\tab    occupies memory locations already reserved for the operating\par
\tab    system; as such, this is a more flexible debugger than ODT-LO\par
\tab    or ODT-HI.\par
\par
\tab d) P?S/8 ODT - This is a proper superset of OS/8 ODT which is\par
\tab    less intrusive due to the smaller reserved memory area of\par
\tab    P?S/8 as compared to OS/8.  The main advantages of P?S/8 ODT\par
\tab    include the implementation of a table of 16 breakpoints\par
\tab    (instead of a single preakpoint as in all versions discussed \par
\tab    above).\par
\tab    \par
\tab    Commands are provided to allow compatibility with\par
\tab    other implementations where only a single breakpoint is available.\par
\tab    Additional breakpoints can be used by reference to breakpoints\par
\tab    either by sequence number or by breakpointed memory address as\par
\tab    desired.  Additionally, commands are available to dump out\par
\tab    blocks of memory in a variety of formats.\par
\par
\tab    With regard to the present method of P?S/8 system generation,\par
\tab    all P?S/8 starter systems includes P?S/8 ODT to allow any\par
\tab    impromptu program patches if necessary.\par
\par
31.\tab OS/278\par
\par
\tab The only OS/8 variant that can run on the DECmate II, III or III+\par
\tab models is OS/278 Version 2.\par
\par
\tab Note: Both versions of OS/278 are compromised and incompatible\par
\tab subsets of OS/8.  This is partially due to the console hardware\par
\tab incompatibility of DECmates as compared to all of the PDP-8\par
\tab models and partially due to the profound incompetence of the\par
\tab programmers who worked on the source code files associated with \tab the release.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab OS/278 Version 1 was never released as an operating system as\par
\tab much of the coding was (hopelessly) broken.  Certain components\par
\tab of OS/278 Version 1 were made (partially) functional by\par
\tab retrofitting the source code from OS/78 Version 4 (which is\par
\tab described elsewhere in this document).  The apparent goal for\par
\tab this system was to create an incompatible OS/8 variant that could\par
\tab self-start into image files directly (which is not an inherent\par
\tab feature of the OS/8 design concept).\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab Certain components of OS/278 Version 1 can be found on released\par
\tab diagnostic diskettes for RX50-based DECmates; some have been\par
\tab hacked up to create various automatic operations when booted\par
\tab without any user interaction.\par
\par
\tab Note: By way of comparison, P?S/8 BATCH can be made to run in an\par
\tab infinite loop that can be interrupted by pressing certain keys\par
\tab (to effect program abort).  It is possible to prevent this\par
\tab interruption from occurring using a variety of methods (such as\par
\tab using a custom variant of the Logical Console Overlay specially\par
\tab configured for the exact purpose); as such, it is far easier to\par
\tab create the intended environment in P?S/8 without having to make\par
\tab radical changes to the operating system.  When the P?S/8 SHELL is\par
\tab implemented (in a future release), automatic startup/login script\par
\tab files will allow the desired function in a straight-forward\par
\tab manner comparable to other operating systems (but not OS/8).\par
\par
32.\tab OS/78\par
\par
\tab The original intent of the product name variant to OS/78 was to\par
\tab point out the release of the VT78; however, actual functionality\par
\tab (or the lack thereof) greatly varies from this notion.\par
\par
\tab Properly written OS/8 family operating system variants may (or\par
\tab may not) include various OS/78 variants; OS/78 Version 4 is a\par
\tab bastardized system attempting to use much of the OS/8 operating\par
\tab system on DECmate I systems; known incompatible components have\par
\tab been removed producing partial compatibility (to avoid complete \tab failure).  However, certain performance-oriented features were\par
\tab removed (such as Control-C abort checking in non-system device\par
\tab handlers) due to the incompatible device 03/04 hardware of all\par
\tab DECmates.  In general, a program taken from OS/78 Version 4 can\par
\tab be expected to perform equally poorly on PDP-8 systems due to\par
\tab the missing code sections (but might otherwise provide partial\par
\tab functionality).  (For many such programs, the original OS/8\par
\tab release is the proper choice for PDP-8 and VT78 systems despite\par
\tab the system name.)\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: It is extremely likely that components of OS/78 Version 4\par
\tab will not run correctly on Pre-Omnibus machines.  This is not for\par
\tab technical reasons; in general less-skilled programmers worked on \tab this project with no training in how to navigate the relevant\par
\tab issues.  In general, it is possible to retrofit the OS/78 Version\par
\tab 4 release to function on the rest of the \ul Family-of-8\ulnone  machines,\par
\tab but it is debatable if this even worth doing.  (Where relevant,\par
\tab this level of attention should be directed towards a version\par
\tab associated with OS/8; some of these programs exhibit the \tab compatibility problems.)\par
\cf3\line\tab\cf0 One of the OS/78 releases is merely a subset of the corresponding\par
\tab OS/8 release created for dubious purpose.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: It is the author's recollection this pertains to OS/78 V2\par
\tab (any clarification from readers of this document is welcome).  \tab The variation associated with this release functions as follows:\par
\par
\tab Each OS/8 core-image program has an associated \ul\i Job Status Word\ulnone\i0  \tab (\i JSW\i0 ) stored within the image file.  A \i feature\i0  was added to OS/8\par
\tab whereby the only way to execute image files (with certain bits\par
\tab set) was by use of the (normally) optional CCL mechanism.  Common\par
\tab sense dictates this is worthless, yet DEC marketing released a\par
\tab subset of OS/8 (with a marketing name of some nominal version \tab number of OS/78) where all image programs had this bit set.\par
\par
\tab Note: The obvious folly of this release was quickly discovered by\par
\tab some savvy users.  The CCL mechanism itself is controlled by the\par
 \tab execution of the CCL.SV program (which also has the same bits \tab set).  The command R (or RUN \i dev\i0 :) CCL enables the CCL mechanism. \tab The explicit CCL command \i CCL\i0  disables the entire CCL mechanism.  \tab Since CCL.SV is also affected, once the CCL mechanism is turned\par
\tab off, no programs can ever run again because there is no way to\par
\tab restore the CCL mechanism.  (This entire mechanism is enabled in\par
\tab OS/8 by a SET command; however the relevant files were not part \tab of this OS/78 release due to limited storage space.)\par
\line\tab Certain aspects of OS/78 Version 4 were used to retrofit the \tab failed OS/278 Version 1 into Version 2 as discussed elsewhere in \tab this document.\line\line\tab Releases of OS/78 (with lower release numbers than 4) generally\par
\tab reveal an absurd alternate agenda (as described above), or merely\par
\tab removing programs from OS/8 due to lack of storage space on the\par
\tab provided media; in some cases, a few programs were added that\par
\tab have little application outside of being useful on the VT78.\par
 \tab These are extremely pragmatic; they were designed to run on\par
\tab contrived configurations of PDP-8-based systems (or the VT78\par
\tab only).  By nature, this means that most OS/8 systems cannot run\par
\tab any of them.\par
\tab\line\tab Only an expert can tell the difference between several of these\par
\tab seemingly similar products; they do not represent a coherent set\par
\tab of product options as the overall product name might suggest.\par
\par
33.\tab OS/8\par
\par
\tab OS/8 is the name given to the most popular system in this family\par
\tab of operating systems.  For purely marketing purposes, the\par
\tab software was renamed from the former name of \i PS/8\i0  due to claims\par
\tab from sales personnel regarding the difficulty of selling\par
\tab customers on a \i programming\i0  system as opposed to an \i operating\par
\i0\tab system.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab As discussed elsewhere in this document, OS/8 was never intended\par
\tab to be a product; it was created over a weekend purely as a \tab demonstration (proof of concept); the consensus of the competent\par
\tab programmers who were already brainstorming a complete replacement\par
\tab (retaining the look and feel) was that it was too much work\par
 \tab compared to just starting all over again at a rational design\par
 \tab pace; moreover, many features were misimplemented, half-baked or\par
 \tab just plain missing.\line\line\tab When incompetent management overruled the programmers, most of\par
\tab them walked out on the project.  Further, management insisted\par
\tab upon forcing the system to always require 8K of memory (and later\par
\tab 12K for certain configurations).  Needless restrictions were\par
\tab added to (overly) manage memory in a manner consistent with (what\par
\tab is wrong \tab with) the \ul\i Disk Monitor System\ulnone\i0  known for its poor\par
\tab performance and needless misimplementation of unnecessary system\par
\tab functions.\line\line\tab Over the course of several years following these foolish\par
\tab management edicts, the effects of poor design became apparent as\par
\tab follows:\par
\par
\tab a) Since the number of storage records was prematurely fixed, it\par
\tab    was not possible to add on important features such as BATCH\par
\tab    operations; as such, a belated poor attempt produced a\par
\tab    semblance of a BATCH which is not device-independent (which\par
\tab    differs from all of the rest of the system design) and\par
\tab    additionally requires 12K; this is due to the need to be\par
\tab    memory-resident.  As such, it is not possible to operate\par
\tab    across system reload operations.\par
\par
\tab    Note: Despite the trivial nature of the implied change (to a\par
\tab    non-fixated design), non-structured device handlers are\par
\tab    restricted to loaded into Field 0 only.  This prevents BATCH\par
\tab    from operating as intended being completely resident in\par
\tab    extended memory (Field 2).  Since file-structured device\par
\tab    handlers are also restricted to being loaded into Field 2, the\par
\tab    BATCH output cannot be sent to a log file.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\cf0\par
\par
\tab    Contrast this with P?S/8 which supports BATCH operations in 4K\par
\tab    with optional checkpointing across reboots.  P?S/8 is in turn\par
\tab    partially based on the \ul R-L Monitor System\ulnone  described elsewhere\par
\tab    in this document; this forerunner system had been presented to\par
\tab    DEC management.  However, the managers were profoundly\par
\tab    incompetent and were clueless as to what they were looking at.\par
\tab    Even this older system, which also runs in 4K, supports BATCH\par
\tab    operations.\par
\par
\tab b) The OS/8 directory structure includes the overhead of using a\par
\tab    linked overall structure, yet the directory is required to be\par
\tab    located in fixed adjacent records.\par
\par
\tab    Because of the fixed size, the directory capacity is too\par
\tab    limited for many users; directory entries cannot be added yet\par
\tab    the underlying storage is available and wasted (even on\par
\tab    logical storage devices that are quite smaller than the\par
\tab    maximum storage capacity,\par
\par
\tab c) The directory entry for all files in any directory are subject \par
\tab    to removal of a special \ul\i extra-information-word\ulnone\i0  to allow more\par
\tab    entries at the expense of loss of file date.  Clearly, users\par
\tab    affected most by b) above were forced into this situation.\line\tab    However, this drastic action is only partially helpful; the\par
\tab    effective storage space actually used for file storage is\par
\tab    often remains compromised.\par
\par
\tab d) System designers often start with relatively round starting\par
\tab    date years; OS/8 is no exception since it uses 1970 as the\par
\tab    inaugural year.  However, the constructive years for most\par
\tab    users starts several years later.\par
\par
\tab    Note: If the functional date range were longer, the issues\par
\tab    discussed in d) above wouldn't be important.\par
\par
\tab    The poor design of the date range results in a maximum range\par
\tab    of eight years using a single 12-bit word resident in the\par
\tab    system kernel.  Thus, it is impossible to uniquely express\par
\tab    file dates past 31-Dec-1977.\par
\par
\tab    This problem was the point of many heated discussions as far\par
\tab    back as 1974; this occurred because there was an analogous\par
\tab    problem in the TOPS-10 system for the PDP-10 computer that\par
\tab    would similarly end file date support in 1975.  Despite this,\par
\tab    management was totally unreasonable and insisting on the\par
\tab    impossible - don't change anything.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\cf0\line\line\tab    What was implemented is quite unacceptable:\line\line\tab    Because the OS/8 design was nearly completely locked in, only\par
\tab    two bits remained available within the system kernel; these\par
\tab    were assigned to extend the existent date range (1970-1977) to\par
\tab    1970-2001.  However, because of pressure from users already\par
\tab    resorting to removing the single \ul extra-information-word\ulnone , there\par
\tab    would be no support for an additional \ul extra-information-word\par
\ulnone\tab    to properly implement the date year extension specifics.\line\line\tab    As released, this is tantamount to perpetrating a hoax on the\par
\tab    user community.  Since the date year only has eight possible\par
\tab    values, the range was recast as ending with the present year\par
\tab    and starting with the seventh preceding year.  For files\par
\tab    actually created prior to this contrived range, it is\par
\tab    impossible to reference the actual date; instead, the claimed\par
\tab    file date is anomalous to the true date plus exactly eight\par
\tab    years.\line\line\tab    This situation might be minimally acceptable to new users.\par
\tab    However, eventually all users are subject to the problem\par
\tab    rooted in the poor design.  It is not possible to determine\par
\tab    which group of eight years an accurate file date pertains to\par
\tab    without specific independent documentation or perhaps proper\par
\tab    commentary within a source file; obtaining the correct file \par
\tab    date is often a matter of guesswork.  (And after 2001, all\par
\tab    date settings are always in some uncertain portion of the\par
\tab    past.)\line\line\tab    Perhaps worse, early in the indicated present year, there is a\par
\tab    high probability that existent files from eight (or sixteen or\par
\tab    twenty-four) years earlier are presented as if in the future.\par
\par
\tab    Note: The P?S/8 system kernel reserves locations 07600-07777\par
\tab    with no extended memory support included (unless the system\par
\tab    device handler requires an extended memory segment).  Despite\par
\tab    this, seven new bits have been allocated to be implemented in\par
\tab    the next release of P?S/8 Version 9A.\par
\par
\tab e) There are many other problems not documented here; most could\par
\tab    have either been avoided or perhaps remedied if use of the\par
\tab    failed demo version not been insisted on by ignorant managers.\par
\par
34.\tab P?S\par
\par
\tab The \ul\i Poly Question Society\ulnone\i0  (P?S) is the (self-appointed) name \tab\tab of a group of questioners most of whom met at the school they \tab\tab affectionately called \ul Brooklyn Poly\ulnone  (or \ul\i Brooklyn Polytech\ulnone\i0 ) or\par
\tab the more formal name of \ul\i The Polytechnic Institute of Brooklyn\ulnone\i0 .\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: Various other names of the school have existed or been\par
\tab proposed over the years; as of the time of this writing, the\par
\tab school has now become a division of \ul\i New York University\ulnone\i0  (\i NYU\i0 ).\par
\tab As of 2015, any form of original name is buried in an honorarium\par
\tab as a result of a large private grant; as such, it is now known as\par
\tab\ul\i New York University Tandon School of Engineering\ulnone\i0 .\par
\ul Brooklyn Poly\ulnone  (or \ul\i Brooklyn Polytech\ulnone\i0 )\par
\tab Certain groups of people have many names for this institution,\par
\tab some of which are not necessarily suitable for publication (or\par
\tab even discussion in mixed company).\par
\par
\tab Most of the members of the P?S (whether living, dead, or \tab\tab\tab mythological creatures) are additionally somewhat legendary in\par
\tab the annals of computer programming (and in some cases beyond the\par
\tab limitations of their own minds); certain individuals are\par
\tab responsible for many programming projects, most of which were\par
\tab eventually products of Digital Equipment Corporation (DEC).\par
\par
\tab When and where possible, P?S should be spelled \ul P?S\ulnone  to be \tab\tab\tab pronounced as \ul\i pee.kew.ess\ulnone\i0 ; if necessary, P?S can be printed as\par
\tab\ul\i PQS\ulnone\i0  which corresponds to the pronunciation.  (This generally\par
\tab applies to limited character set situations such as associated\par
\tab file names; the \ul\i ?\ulnone\i0  character is often reserved for wild-card\par
\tab searches.)\par
\par
\tab Note: The P?S also has an official symbol which closely resembles\par
 \tab the large \cf2\highlight3\f0 ?\cf0\highlight0\f1  as seen on various costumes worn by the late \ul\i Frank\par
\ulnone\tab\ul Gorshin\ulnone\i0  as \ul\i The Riddler\ulnone\i0  on the 1966 TV show \ul\i Batman\ulnone\i0  (starring \ul\i Adam\par
\ulnone\tab\ul West\ulnone\i0  as \i Batman\i0  and \ul\i Burt\ulnone  \ul Ward\ulnone\i0  as \i Robin\i0 ).\par
\par
\tab In print, the \ul\i Lucida Console\ulnone\i0  font (as used in this document) \tab\tab renders the \ul\i ?\ulnone\i0  character in a acceptable manner.\par
\par
35.\tab P?S/8\par
\par
\tab This Glossary section explains key aspects of P?S/8, a\par
\tab versatile operating system for the 4K (or larger) PDP-8\par
\tab (requiring extended memory only if the system device handler\par
\tab cannot be entirely implemented in the system kernel area of\par
\tab 07600-07777).  As is the case with certain other operating\par
\tab systems for the PDP-8, P?S/8 runs on all 12-bit PDP-8 models with\par
\tab the exception of the PDP8/S (which is not fully compatible) and\par
\tab the PDP-5 (which is more incompatible than the PDP-8/S).\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: DEC marketing created definitive terms for the various\par
\tab PDP-8 models sold at different times as follows:\line\line\tab a) \ul Family of 8\ulnone  - This term conjures up references to pirates and\par
\tab    \ul\i pieces of 8\ulnone\i0  (which is a similar sounding measurement unit for\par
\tab    ancient coinage also used by DEC marketing at the time).\par
 \tab    However, \ul Family of 8\ulnone  refers to the definition of the PDP-8\par
\tab    models as described above.  While it may have been originally\par
\tab    part of a sales pitch, it is an adequate term often seen in\par
\tab    various surviving technical documents.\par
\par
\tab    Most importantly, other than for the express purpose of cost-\par
\tab    cutting (such as the inability for the PDP-8/L and many newer\par
\tab    models to support the \ul Extended Arithmetic Element\ulnone\sub 99\nosupersub\par
\tab    option), The PDP-8/S is the only PDP-8 model to be\par
\tab    architecturally less capable than its immediate predecessor.\par
\par
\i\tab    \i0 P?S/8 runs on all \ul Family of 8\ulnone  models, as well as several\par
\tab    (generally) compatible machines made by competitors.  while\par
\tab    EAE is not required, system status mechanisms exist to report\par
\tab    the presence of EAE; additionally, certain P?S/8 system\par
\tab    programs may take (partial) advantage of EAE (such as\par
 \tab    displaying current program status information in the MQ\par
\tab    register).\par
\par
\tab b) \ul\i Pre-OMNIBUS\ulnone\i0  - This term was used in a rather ominous manner\par
\tab    when it was first introduced (perhaps the obvious word play is\par
\tab    no coincidence).  \ul Pre-OMNIBUS\ulnone  in this case means all 12-bit\par
\tab    DEC computers made prior to the introduction of the PDP-8/E\par
\tab    (and related OMNIBUS-based models such as the PDP-8/F and\par
\tab    PDP-8/M) for the express purpose of dropping support for these\par
\tab    machines.\par
\par
\tab    However, the underlying reasoning was caused by management\par
\tab    losing control of the competency of programmers working for\par
\tab    the PDP-8 program development group.  While seasoned members\par
\tab    easily conformed their work to \ul Family of 8\ulnone  restrictions,\par
\tab    poorly trained new hires were left to fend for themselves\par
\tab    without proper supervision (or even proper documentation of\par
\tab    the issues).\par
\par
\tab    As a consequence of their own limitations (and as an over-\par
\tab    reaction), an edict was declared that from then onwards there\par
\tab    were no guarantees that new software would work on existing\par
\tab    older machines.  This was especially disappointing because\par
\tab    certain hardware had been purchased (as encouraged by the same\par
\tab    marketing people) for the purpose of extending the useful life\par
\tab    of existing machines.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab    Note: This was not taken well by certain customers who were\par
\tab    torn as to whether to upgrade existing machines or to replace\par
\tab    the CPU with a newer model; the economic decision rang hollow\par
\tab    when it was announced their \ul Pre-OMNIBUS\ulnone  machines were now\par
\tab    considered obsolete too soon after the purchase of the\par
\tab    relevant upgrades including backward means of backward\par
\tab    hardware compatibility.\line\line\tab    Fortunately, since P?S/8 runs on all \ul Family of 8\ulnone  models, this\par
\tab    is not a consideration.  In truth, this issue is rather\par
\tab    overblown and merely amounts to some modest discipline when\par
\tab    potentially programming a very small class of group I operate\par
\tab    instructions and the CAF and other interrupt-related\par
\tab    instructions where necessary.\par
\par
\tab    Note: The author of this document (Charles Lasner) has\par
\tab    personally discovered many of these problems within OS/8\par
\tab    source programs and provided work-arounds (where feasible).\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab    Thus, the only consideration, with regard to P?S/8\par
\tab    compatibility, is the one implied by the \ul Family of 8\ulnone  marketing\par
\tab    term.  All P?S members who have developed components of P?S/8\par
\tab    laugh at the notion of \ul Pre-OMNIBUS\ulnone  machines; only truly\par
\tab    incompetent newbies are incapable of producing fully-compliant\par
\tab    code.\par
\par
\tab P?S/8 also supports the DECmate series, the final DEC 12-bit\par
`    computers based on the 6120 chip.  Due to the use of the \tab incompatible 6121 I/O support chips, these machines are\par
\tab incompatible with the PDP-8 device 03/04 console device; OS/8\par
\tab does not support these machines (although there are incompatible\par
\tab software variant systems that partially function).\par
\par
\tab P?S/8 uses fully conforming console terminal handling in all\par
\tab system components.  DECmate compatibility is achieved using\par
\tab additional software features not available in other operating\par
\tab systems.  Programs that will always avoid DECmate execution by\par
\tab circumstance (such as utility programs for devices that cannot\par
\tab run on DECmates) need not include the conforming console\par
\tab routines.  The P?S/8 keyboard monitor routines check for both\par
\tab variations to achieve full compatibility.\line\line\tab DECmate support also requires a concept unique to the P?S/8 \tab system kernel: The software abort status bit.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab On all DEC 12-bit models (from the PDP-5 through the VT78) the \tab console handling is identical.  The key features of concern as\par
\tab follows:\line\line\tab a) It is possible to detect that the Control-C character has been\par
\tab    pressed when the keyboard flag is raised; the detection\par
\tab    process doesn't change the status of the keyboard flag.\line\line\tab    Unfortunately, in the DECmate implementation the instruction\par
 \tab    to skip on keyboard flag raised also clears the keyboard flag.\par
\tab    It is impossible for a portion of the keyboard monitor read\par
\tab    into memory to check for the same keyboard flag event; as\par
 \tab    such, this prevents proper abort actions concerning Control-C\par
\tab    such as issuing abort messages and canceling BATCH operations,\par
\tab    etc.\par
\par
\tab b) There is an ability to read the latest character without \par
\tab    initiating the process of reading the next character.\line\line\tab    Unfortunately, in the DECmate implementation the only\par
\tab    instruction available to read the latest character initiates\par
\tab    reading the next character which prevents re-read operations.\line\line\tab As such, fully conforming P?S/8 components must communicate the \tab abort event by setting the software abort status bit in the P?S/8\par
\tab system kernel.  Fully conforming programs may continue to use the\par
 \tab earlier mechanism on strictly PDP-8 family models; however, when\par
\tab the DECmate-specific console hardware is detected, the program\par
\tab must dynamically self-modify to use the software abort method.\line\par
\tab This is easily accomplished in virtually every instance due to\par
\tab ample code space in the initialization section of all conforming\par
\tab programs; the appropriate coding will be in effect during the\par
\tab entire main program execution, etc.\par
\par
\tab P?S/8 has been developed (sporadically) over the course of\par
\tab multiple decades by several P?S members with the (sometime)\par
\tab assistance of several other computer programmers.  A few of these\par
\tab other programmers have actually written PDP-8 programs prior to\par
\tab their participation in the P?S/8 project (or perhaps sometime\par
\tab afterwards).  In certain cases, remnants of their participation\par
\tab are actually still considered a portion of the overall source\par
\tab code collection, and not necessarily commented out; as such, some\par
\tab of their works produces binary code that is part of relatively\par
\tab recent system releases.  In specific cases, the names of these\par
\tab individuals have been withheld for a variety of reasons, none of\par
\tab which have anything to do with known witness protection programs.\par
\par
\tab P?S/8 should be pronounced as \ul\i pee.kew.ess.ate\ulnone\i0 ; if necessary,\par
\tab P?S/8 can be spelled as \i PQS/8\i0  and if applied to portions of\par
\tab related file names, abbreviated to \i PQS8\i0  in all capital letters.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\cf0\line\par
\tab At the time of this writing, virtually all current support for\par
\tab P?S/8 is performed by Charles Lasner, who has written much of the\par
\tab source code, and is also the author of this document.\line\line\tab Various releases of P?S/8 may be of interest as follows:\par
\par
\tab a) P?S/8 Version 8Z - This is the current complete release as\par
\tab    described elsewhere in this document (which explains all known\par
\tab    official patches to the system and system programs).\par
\par
\tab b) P?S/8 Version 8T - This is a very old P?S/8 release that\par
\tab    requires mastery of the \ul\i P?S/8 Version 8T Keyboard Monitor\par
\ulnone\i0\tab    \ul\i Guide\ulnone\i0  (available as a separate document).  Many of the\par
\tab    keyboard monitor commands are different from newer releases.\par
\tab    P?S/8 Version 8T actually corresponds closely to the \ul R-L\i\par
\ulnone\tab    \ul\i0 Monitor System\ulnone  since the commands were changed in later P?S/8\par
\tab    releases to closely resemble the keyboard monitor commands and \par
\tab    conventions of \ul\i COS-300/310\ulnone\i0 .\cf3\par
\cf0\line\tab    Note: Only the TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 configuration of P?S/8\ul\i  \par
\ulnone\tab    \i0 Version 8T is known to have survived.  It is available as a\par
\tab    portion of the \ul\i P?S Enhanced PDP-8 Simulator\ulnone\i0  (\i PEPS\i0 ) package for\par
\tab    \ul\i Microsoft Windows\ulnone\super\i0 tm\nosupersub , also available from the author of this\par
\tab    document.\par
\par
\tab    For the most part, the only reason to use this older release\par
\tab    is to illustrate just how much progress was made in the nine\par
\tab    or ten years between the releases of P?S/8 as described above.\par
\par
\tab c) P?S/8 Version 9A - This is the tentative title of the next\par
\tab    full release of P?S/8.\par
\par
\tab    With the exception of a few internal specifics, P?S/8 Version\par
\tab    9A will hardly differ from Version 8Z from a user's\par
\tab    perspective.  This is basically a code cleanup release to\par
\tab    solve a host of small technical issues that are required to\par
\tab    include the P?S/8\i  SHELL\i0 , an optional overlay which will\par
\tab    include virtually all of the useful features of OS/8.\par
\par
\tab    Additional changes will be made to the keyboard monitor\par
\tab    implementation and internal specifics related to extended-\par
\tab    length file support that affects several system programs, etc.\par
\par
\tab    Note: Due to the complexity of the P?S/8 SHELL, the initial\par
\tab    release of P?S/8 Version 9A will not include the P?S/8 SHELL.\par
\tab    If all underlying issues are solved (perhaps by applying\par
