34.\tab P?S/8 BLKCPY\par
\par
\tab The P?S/8 BLKCPY system program is used to copy (or compare) sets \tab of contiguous blocks on any device(s) supported by the P?S/8 non-\tab system device handlers.  Blocks are specified in absolute form by\par
\tab either a first through last block range or a starting block and a\par
\tab block count, etc.\line\line\tab The command syntax is similar to that of command decoders\par
\tab associated with several systems (except that absolute blocks are\par
 \tab specified instead of file names).\line\line\tab The exact function performed is determined by the command \tab separator character.  As in the execution-class commands of the \tab P?S/8 keyboard monitor, commands can be specified in either an \tab output first or an input first manner.  Comparisons are specified \tab using an alternate separator character between block \tab specifications.\line\line\tab Input files can be passed to BLKCPY using execution-class\par
 \tab commands.  The contents of the file must consist of valid BLKCPY \tab command specifications or designated comments.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\par
\cf0\tab As of P?S/8 Version 8Y, P?S/8 BLKCPY supports blocks in the range\par
 \tab of 0000 through 7777.  P?S/8 Version 9A will upgrade the non-\tab system device handlers to handle blocks as high as 77777777.  As \tab such, a command-line option switch will be added to BLKCPY to \tab enable extended transfers with up to eight digit block numbers.\par
\line 33.\tab P?S/8 BLKODT\par
\par
\tab The P?S/8 \i BLKODT\i0  utility is used to display and/or modify the\par
\tab contents of a specified block on any logical unit that can be\par
\tab accessed using the system device handler.\par
\par
\tab BLKODT commands are identical to those of ODT with several \tab exceptions as follows:\line\line\tab a) Instead of accessing memory locations, all commands are\par
\tab    oriented to relative locations within a specified block.  As\par
\tab    such, the range is limited to 000-177.\line\line\tab b) All commands associated with breakpoints are removed.\par
\par
\tab c) Command extensions exist to specify logical unit and block.\line\line\tab BLKODT can be run by the contents of input files passed to any\par
\tab execution-class command used to run BLKODT.\par
\par
\tab Due to the straightforward and compact nature of BLKODT, all \par
\tab patches described in this document are shown as BLKODT commands.\par
\par
47.\tab P?S/8 DUMP\par
\par
 26.\tab P?S/8 DUMP\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  DUMP is a small versatile utility program that is \tab included in the P?S/8\sub 99\nosupersub  starter system.\cf3\par
\cf0\line\tab DUMP is used for a number of specific tasks initiated by commands\par
\tab as follows:\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab a) DU\{MP\} and display the contents of a designated block on the\par
\tab    system device (or any other logical unit controlled by the\par
\tab    system device).  The display consists of a formatted listing\par
\tab    of (up to) the entire contents of the designated block; each\par
\tab    line includes the relative index number of the left-most value\par
\tab    followed by the eight values starting from that particular\par
\tab    relative index.  To the right of the octal listing is a\par
\tab    character display representing the same values in six-bit\par
\tab    ASCII text.  (The character display format was chosen\par
\tab    arbitrarily because it is the most likely text display to be\par
\tab    relevant to the contents of various P?S/8 system blocks.)  The\par
\tab    output is always directed to the system console device (which\par
\tab    may be redirected to the \ul Logical Console Overlay\ulnone  if enabled).\cf3\par
\par
\cf0\tab    The display can be limited to the highest portion of the\par
\tab    listing as indicated above by the setting of the computer's\par
\tab    front panel switches; only bits[5-8] are used.  If desired,\par
\tab    the /S command-line option switch can be invoked to override\par
\tab    this; the value of \i =xxxx\i0  passed on the command line will\par
\tab    control all displayed output.  If the =xxxx is not explicitly\par
\tab    given, the value 0000 will be used (which causes display of\par
\tab    all output lines).\par
\par
\tab b) TR\{ANSFER\} a set of contiguous blocks from any accessible\par
\tab    logical device unit and starting block to another accessible\par
\tab    logical device unit and starting number.\line\line\tab    Note: Only advanced users should INVOKE this option as\par
\tab    transfers that overlay certain system blocks can cause\par
\tab    permanent damage to the operating system.\par
\par
\tab    The transfer mode checks for available memory when DUMP is\par
\tab    first loaded into memory.  In 4K, the largest number of blocks\par
\tab    that can be transferred in any one operation is a function of\par
\tab    the program length.  As of P?S/8 Version 8Z, the largest\par
\tab    read/write transfer allowed is 27 (octal) blocks per command.\cf3\par
\par
\tab    \cf0 DUMP fully conforms to P?S/8 extended memory allocation rules.\par
 \tab    On certain systems, the first 3/4 of Field 1 may also be\par
\tab    available; in this case, DUMP can always transfer 30 (octal) \tab\tab    blocks because the read/write transfer occurs in 10000-15777.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab    For all systems that run on 8K (or larger) memory, the largest\par
\tab    transfer is 40 (octal) blocks (an entire memory field) because\par
\tab    this is the largest value that can be obtained on any\par
\tab    supported configuration (generally as a hardware restriction).\par
\par
\tab c) ZA\{P\} (or patch) the contents of a designated block on any\par
\tab    accessible logical device unit.  The designated block is first\par
\tab    displayed in the same format as described above for the DU\{MP\}\par
\tab    command.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab    Various single-character commands can be used to examine and\par
\tab    change the contents of any of the relative locations in the\par
\tab    range of 000-177.  As such, it is possible to completely\par
\tab    change the contents of the block being patched.\par
\par
\tab    Note: As stated above with regard to the TR\{ANSFER\} operation,\par
\tab    only advanced users should invoke this option as patching\par
\tab    certain system blocks can cause permanent damage to the\par
\tab    operating system.\par
\par
\tab d) EX\{IT\} is used to terminate the DUMP utility and reload P?S/8.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: All P?S/8 system configurations handle logical units in\par
\tab system-specific ways.  The general unit range is 0-7; \tab however, \tab depending on system device handler peculiarities, attempts to\par
\tab access certain logical units may cause errors (which will require\par
\tab some form of restart); on some configurations accessing certain\par
\tab logical units may be anomalous to other logical units.\par
\par
\tab In general, anomalies arise due to certain bits of the three-bit\par
\tab field expressing the logical unit (throughout any specific\par
\tab configuration of P?S/8) being ignored.  For example, the RX01\par
\tab variation of P?S/8 will support units 0-3 on some supported\par
\tab hardware configurations and units 0-1 on others.  For all\par
\tab supported RX01-compatible configurations, the high-order bit of\par
\tab the logical unit is always ignored.  As such, units 4-7 are\par
\tab anomalous to units 0-3.  Future releases of the RX01-based system\par
\tab may include better internal testing of the logical unit bits.  \tab Attempts to access unavailable logical units will (predictably)\par
\tab lead to errors.\line\line\tab At present, it is up to the user to understand which logical\par
\tab drive units are actually available (and non-anomalous).\par
\par
\tab Most importantly, it is possible to fully automate all DUMP \tab operations through the use of .DUMP command input files passed to\par
\tab the program.\par
\par
\tab .DUMP files are ASCII text files compatible with the P?S/8 TFS\par
\tab (or extended-length) file structure.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: The designation of .DUMP files refers to the way they are\par
\tab stored in other file structures (such as the forthcoming P?S/8\par
\tab SHELL overlay file structure); this file extension is used to\par
\tab indicate the contents are valid commands to the P?S/8 DUMP\par
\tab utility (or perhaps a future implementation of a similar program \tab for the P?S/8 SHELL).  The actual files (once stored on a basic\par
\tab P?S/8 system) are ordinary text files that can be prepared (or\par
\tab modified) with the P?S/8 embedded text editor (when stored as TFS\par
\tab files).\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: .DUMP command files are generally compact in terms of total\par
\tab characters; this is due to the simplistic nature of most DUMP\par
\tab commands.  To date, the largest .DUMP command input used to \tab facilitate P?S/8 system generation spans three TFS files; most\par
\tab useful .DUMP command input is far shorter (and occupy a single\par
\tab TFS file).\par
\par
\tab .DUMP command input can (and should) include internal commentary. \tab Any file line within a .DUMP file starting with the \highlight1\\\highlight0  character\par
\tab is considered a comment (which will otherwise be ignored).\par
\par
\tab Note: P?S/8 system generation .DUMP command files include profuse\par
\tab commentary to explain the purpose of each operation.\par
 \par
\tab The operations of the P?S/8 starter system make extensive use of\par
\tab DUMP controlled by .DUMP command input to create initial\par
\tab structures; this is done to facilitate adding on additional\par
\tab system programs and other settings to produce a complete release.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: Future releases of P?S/8 will use alternate techniques to\par
\tab generate system releases.  For more information, see the\par
