34.\tab P?S/8 OS8CON\par
\par
\tab The P?S/8 \i OS8CON\i0  utility is used to convert text files between\par
\tab the OS/8 operating system format and the P?S/8 operating system\par
\tab formats as supported by the TFS file system and the extended-\tab length file system.\par
\par
\tab Conversion specifics are determined by command-line option\par
\tab switches and file names passed in execution-class commands.\line\line\tab Transfers from/to P?S/8 extended-length output files is\par
\tab implemented with a temporary restriction in P?S/8 Version 8Z\par
\tab requiring the user to exercise caution when using this conversion\par
 \tab mode.  This problem will be eliminated in the next release.\par
 \tab Additionally, several minor character conversion changes will be\par
\tab implemented (that few users are affected by).  More information\par
\tab about inter-system character conversion can be obtained from\par
\tab Appendix C of the \ul\i P?S/8 Keyboard Monitor Command Guide\ulnone\i0  available\par
\tab as a separate document.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\cf0\par
\par
35.\tab P?S/8 PAL\par
\par
\tab The P?S/8 \i PAL\i0  is an implementation of the PDP-8 assembly language\par
\tab (also known as \i PAL\i0 ).  In general, P?S/8 PAL is a superset of OS/8\par
\tab PAL8 and TOPS-10 PAL10.  LINC mode dual assembly is available as\par
\tab a modular option enabled by the use of command-line options.\par
\tab When LINC mode dual assembly is enabled, P?S/8 PAL is a superset\par
\tab of the DIAL embedded assembler (described elsewhere in this\par
\tab document).\par
\par
  \tab More information about P?S/8 PAL can be obtained from the \ul\i\f0 P?S/8\par
\ulnone\tab\ul PAL Version 8S Technical Guide\ulnone\i0\f1  available as a separate document.\par
\par
36.\tab P?S/8 SHELL\par
\par
\tab The P?S/8 \i SHELL\i0  overlay is an optional overlay to the basic P?S/8\par
\tab system (as described by P?S/8 Version 8Z).  The SHELL overlay is\par
 \tab currently being developed for release some time after the initial\tab release of P?S/8 Version 9A; this will allow additional internal\par
\tab features to be implemented (and upgrade of many other internal\par
\tab issues) required by the P?S/8 SHELL overlay.\line\line\tab When the P?S/8 SHELL overlay is fully implemented, P?S/8 will\par
\tab have an optional environment reminiscent of other DEC operating\par
\tab systems capable of running (modified) versions of existing OS/8\par
\tab programs, etc.  Users can choose from two very different file\par
\tab structures as desired.\par
\par
37.\tab PAL8\par
\par
\tab The OS/8 PAL8 assembler is the standard implementation of the\par
\tab PDP-8 Assembly Language (PAL) (which can also be run under the\par
\tab COS-300/310 operating system).\line\line\tab OS/8 PAL8 is a subset of P?S/8 PAL and has some problematic\par
\tab quirks which makes program development somewhat more difficult to \tab accomplish.\par
\par
\tab Note: As of this writing, all current P?S/8 source files can be\par
\tab assembled by PAL8 or P?S/8 PAL; this has been accomplished by\par
\tab avoiding the use of certain P?S/8 PAL superset features.  Future\par
\tab releases of P?S/8 may abandon PAL8 compatibility as new methods\par
\tab of generating P?S/8 are developed.\par
\par
38.\tab PAL10\par
\par
\tab TOPS-10 \i PAL10\i0  is a PDP-8 cross-assembler that runs on PDP-10\par
\tab systems.  At one point, virtually all PDP-8 program development\par
\tab was performed on this system.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\cf0\line\line\tab Unfortunately, PAL10 is incompatible regarding certain key\par
\tab features added to both OS/8 PAL8 and P?S/8 PAL in more recent\par
\tab years; as such, PAL10 had become far less useful as a system\par
 \tab development tool.\par
\par
\tab With regard to certain features, P?S/8 PAL and TOPS-10 PAL10 are\par
\tab fully compatible while OS/8 mis-implements these features.  As\par
\tab such, P?S/8 development has avoided these features (as described\par
\tab above).\par
\par
Paper tape\par
\par
39.\tab Paper-Tape Operating System\par
\par
\tab The (so-called) \ul Paper-Tape Operating System\ulnone  is a collection of\par
\tab programs provided by DEC to all customers of PDP-8 systems.  The\par
\tab only requirement to run any of these programs is a 4K system\par
\tab equipped with either an Teletype Model 33 ASR (or Model 35 ASR)\par
\tab console device; this allows these programs to be read into memory\par
\tab using the DEC BIN loader (as described above).  An optional high-\par
\tab speed reader/punch is recommended if serious use of this software\par
\tab is contemplated.\par
\par
\tab Note: With the exception of the \ul Disk Monitor System\ulnone , program\par
\tab development is markedly faster on any of the storage-based PDP-8\par
\tab operating systems such as OS/8 and P?S/8 (which also include\par
\tab additional advanced features).\par
\par
\tab The components of the \ul Paper-Tape Operating System\ulnone  are generally\par
\tab bundled with system-specific diagnostics and packaged in plastic\par
\tab bins designed for fan-fold paper-tape storage.  Utilities are\par
\tab included to allow copying of existing paper-tapes (which can be\par
\tab worn out or damaged).\par
\par
\tab Note: PAL III is the standard paper-tape assembler for PDP-8\par
\tab Assembly Language (PAL).  Modifications were made to PAL III to\par
\tab allow use with the \ul R-L Monitor System\ulnone  (described elsewhere in\par
\tab this document).\par
\par
\tab Certain design aspects of the P?S/8 PAL assembler are inspired by\tab features (otherwise) unique to PAL III.  The lack of these\par
\tab features in OS/8 PAL8 causes excessive time spent during the\par
\tab early stages of program development.\par
\par
40.\tab PDP-12\par
\par
\tab The\i  PDP-12\i0  is a follow-on model to the earlier LINC-8 system.\par
\par
\tab Note: The PDP-12 was originally intended to be known as the LINC-\par
\tab 8/I (which would be consistent with the description of the\par
\tab original PDP-8 versus the PDP-8/I).\par
\par
\tab P?S/8 supports PDP-12 LINCtape on 4K or larger machines.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\par
\cf0 POLY BASIC\cf3\par
\par
\cf0 41.\tab PS/8\par
\par
\tab The ill-advised demo version of the system that eventually was\par
\tab known as OS/8 was first marketed as Programming System for the \tab PDP-8 (PS/8).  The name was later changed to Operating System for\par
\tab the PDP-8 (OS/8).  This change was made at the request of DEC\par
\tab sales personnel claiming it was more difficult to sell a customer \tab a \ul Programming System\ulnone  as opposed to an \ul Operating System\ulnone  (despite\par
\tab the fact that the software releases were virtually identical).\par
\par
46.\tab R-L Monitor System.\par
\par
\tab P?S/8 is partially based on a forerunner operating system known\par
\tab as the \ul R-L Monitor System\ulnone .  It was later submitted to DECUS\sub 15\nosupersub  as\par
\tab\ul Mass Storage for the PDP-8\ulnone  (MS/8) by several P?S members sometime\par
\tab after Charles Lasner added FOCAL, 1969 support.\par
\par
\tab The \ul R-L Monitor System\ulnone  was created by Richard Lary and Lenny\par
\tab Elekman with the help and encouragement of the rest of the WCFMPG\par
\tab members over a four month period when the PDP-8 at Brooklyn Poly\par
\tab was upgraded with the addition of a TC01 DECtape controller and a\par
\tab single TU55 DECtape drive.\line\line\tab The most profound aspect of this work is that it was created\par
\tab completely by hand.  This consisted of several stages of hand\par
\tab assembly language programming and coding into binary (expressed\par
\tab as four digit octal numbers) later directly entered into the\par
\tab PDP-8 memory using the front panel switches.\line\line\tab The only original component not written by WCFMPG members was a \tab binary copy of PAL III (the standard assembler for use with input\par
\tab on paper tapes).  Modifications were made to read files on the \tab system DECtape and write binary output to a dedicated storage\par
 \tab area on the system DECtape.\par
\par
\tab Note: It is likely the image copy of PAL III was obtained from\par
\tab the TC01/TC08 version of the DECtape Library System.  A copy of\par
\tab this minimal utility system was supplied by DEC when the hardware\par
\tab upgrade was installed.  The system configuration lacked a high-\tab speed reader/punch; the system console device was a Teletype \tab Model 35 KSR.  As such, it was not possible to read binary paper-\tab tapes on this machine at the time.  (The author of this document \tab cannot confirm or deny alternative methods of obtaining PAL III \tab in DEC BIN binary paper-tape format; however, at the time, other \tab PDP-8 installations existed within the school building.)\line\line\tab Once the \ul R-L Monitor System\ulnone  was viable, a fraction of the\par
\tab original source code was typed into source files after the fact.\par
\tab Additionally, some additional system programs were added written\par
 \tab on the base system (such as \ul\i POLY SNOBOL\ulnone\i0  and \ul\i String Language 8\ulnone\i0 ).\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\cf0\line\line\tab Virtually all of the original members of the P?S learned to\par
\tab program the PDP-8 using this pioneering system.  All development\par
\tab of P?S/8 was inspired by the desire to improve the original\par
\tab system by more conventional programming; this included source\par
\tab code editing and assembly on the \ul R-L Monitor System\ulnone  and\par
\tab eventually other DEC systems supporting PDP-8 assembly language.\par
\par
