50.\tab System Console Device\par
\par
\tab In general, the System Console Device for a properly configured\par
\tab PDP-8 system consists of a device 03/04 interface for input and\par
\tab output connected to a variety of compatible terminal devices.  If\par
\tab the need to support reading paper-tapes on the low-speed reader\par
\tab is considered important, the terminal must be either a Teletype\par
\tab Model 33 ASR or Model 35 ASR.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: This is not a consideration if the system includes a high-\tab speed reader and punch connected to a device 01/02 interface.\tab\par
\par
\tab In general, when paper-tape media is not a consideration, the\par
\tab terminal device need only be compatible with a Teletype Model 33\par
\tab KSR or Model 35 KSR.\line\line\tab Note: Most PDP-8 models were sold with a Teletype 33 ASR.  The\par
\tab Brooklyn Poly PDP-8 used the Teletype Model 35 KSR as paper-tape\par
\tab operations were never a consideration.\par
\line\tab All compatible models of teletypes can handle any combination of\par
\tab ASCII characters with a single exception: Sending a \cf2\highlight3\f0 <CR>\cf0\highlight0\f1  must be\par
\tab assumed to require a non-printing idle situation for one\par
\tab character time (based on the worst-case assumption the carriage\par
\tab is positioned on the right margin).  Fortunately, with extremely\par
\tab few exceptions, (invariably) the next character to be printed is\par
\tab\cf2\highlight3\f0 <LF>\cf0\highlight0\f1 , which is non-printing and can overlap the carriage-return\par
\tab function.  As such, general-purpose console output printing\par
\tab routines need not have any special time-out considerations.\line\line\tab Note: The DEC LA-30 printing terminal was available as a serial\par
\tab or a parallel device.  Extremely complex protocol was specified\par
\tab on the serial version that designated different amounts if idle\par
\tab time depending on the exact position of the carriage at the point \tab the \cf2\highlight3\f0 <CR>\cf0\highlight0\f1  character was sent (or a simplistic routine could always\par
\tab output the worst-case idle time); regardless, this device cannot\par
\tab conform to the situation described above.  As such, few serial\par
\tab LA-30 terminals were sold as PDP-8 console devices.  The LA30-P\par
\tab (parallel interface) was available as an add-on to several PDP-8\par
\tab models; the done flag is always delayed as necessary to maintain\par
\tab full print compatibility.\line\line\tab Eventually, several display terminals were released that were\par
\tab also compatible with teletypes (with much higher speed).  The\par
\tab VT05 also had a problem with \cf2\highlight3\f0 <LF>\cf0\highlight0\f1  because one video retrace\par
\tab interval (one 1/60th second) was required before the device could\par
\tab respond to another (printing) character.  This was mitigated by\par
\tab one of two methods as follows:\line\line\tab a) Set the interface speed to 600 baud or less.  Each character\par
\tab    time is the minimum timeout.  (This leads to poor performance\par
\tab    as the VT05 is designed to run at 2400 baud or less.)\line\line\tab b) Eventually, the KL8-JA (M8655) console interface was\par
\tab    introduced for the PDP-8/E and similar models.  An option\par
\tab    switch was added to accommodate the VT05.  The done flag was\par
\tab    not raised until four character times had elapsed.  At 2400\par
\tab    baud, this is the proper minimum timeout (on systems based on\par
\tab    60 Hz. power).\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\line\tab    Note: The implementation of the delay on the KL8-JA is flawed\par
\tab    and heavy-handed.  The proper delay is not exactly four\par
\tab    character output times, but actually is dependent on local AC\par
\tab    power considerations.  In areas where 50 Hz. power is used,\par
\tab    the video retrace delay must be 1.50th second; as such, this\par
\tab    requires the VT05 be slowed to 1200 baud (which is slower and\par
\tab    actually slightly excess delay time).  Furthermore, the\par
\tab    hardware engineers didn't understand that the requirement was\par
\tab    to just wait the appropriate time before raising the done \par
\tab    flag; instead, they over-implemented an interface with an\par
\tab    output character counter: When a \cf2\highlight3\f0 <LF>\cf0\highlight0\f1  was transmitted, it was\par
\tab    followed by four \cf2\highlight3\f0 <NUL>\cf0\highlight0\f1  characters, the last of which then\par
\tab    raised the done flag when completed.  The extra characters are\par
\tab    ignored, but are extraneous.  The design would have been\par
\tab    improved to raise the done flag after a settable delay with 60\par
\tab    Hz. and 50 Hz. options.\line\line\tab    The VT05 was the last DEC terminal that allowed general-\par
\tab    purpose output routines such as are used throughout OS/8\par
\tab    (which does not enforce any particular discipline).\line\line\tab    When the VT50 was introduced, a minor problem became apparent:\par
\tab    Since OS/8 keyboard handling doesn't enforce\par
\tab    Control-S/Control-Q protocol, the VT50 pause key is ignored.\par
\par
\tab    Note: While this was not the inevitable worst-case problem, it\par
 \tab    was a harbinger of what was to come as a result of poor\par
\tab    design.  All display terminals from the VT50 onward include\par
\tab    the pause key.  Pressing the key outputs Control-S and\par
\tab    Control-Q alternately; this allows the user to start and stop\par
\tab    the output as desired.  With rare exception, OS/8 programs\par
\tab    fail to support this operation.  (Notable exceptions include\par
\tab    Kermit-12 for OS/8 and the companion utilities which were\par
\tab    written by the author of this document.)\par
\par
\tab    The next DEC terminal released was the VT-100 family.  Unlike\par
\tab    the VT50/VT52, the speed of the VT-100 is extremely dependent\par
\tab    on proper support of the Control-S/Control-Q protocol for all\par
\tab    display operations.  Unfortunately, many programs in OS/8 do\par
\tab    not support the necessary protocol; users were faced with two\par
\tab    alternatives as follows:\par
\par
\tab    a) Generally disappointed due to higher expectations, the baud\par
\tab       rate had to be dropped to around 600 baud to avoid\par
\tab       voluminous garbled output (such as error messages issued by\par
\tab       PAL8 during program assembly).\par
\par
\tab       b) Abandon OS/8 entirely.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\cf0\line\par
\tab    Many users were frustrated and put up with the horrible (lack\par
\tab    of) support.  The problem was partially mitigated by the use\par
\tab    of the so-called \i KL8E\i0  handler which supports\par
\tab    Control-S/Control-Q protocol correctly; unfortunately,\par
\tab    every OS/8 program performs its own console routines,\par
\tab    generally devoid of proper Control-S/Control-Q protocol\par
\tab    support.  The output directed to the KL8E handler is strictly\par
\tab    display of the contents of files (which is unrelated to\par
\tab    the console messages during program operations).\par
\par
\tab    Contrast this with P?S/8 where console output is 100%\par
\tab    disciplined on many levels.  Control-S/Control-Q support is\par
\tab    \b always\b0  available universally.\par
\par
\tab    P?S/8 also supports a unique feature conceptually unavailable\par
\tab    in OS/8 called the \ul Logical Console Overlay\ulnone .  When this feature\par
\tab    is enabled, all conforming programs redirect all console\par
\tab    routines (and lineprinter support) to subroutine calls to\par
\tab    designated Page Zero locations in extended memory.  The actual\par
\tab    output is determined by the \ul Logical Console Overlay\par
\ulnone\tab    configuration; this could involve the use of terminal\par
\tab    emulators (on devices such as the VT8E, which is not a\par
\tab    terminal) or to equivalent terminals connected to alternate\par
\tab    device codes (such as device 40/41).\par
\par
\tab    Non-conforming programs can be run from P?S/8; however, the\par
\tab    rules with respect to OS/8 operational limitations will apply.\tab    As such, it is strongly recommended that all new PDP-8 console\par
\tab    programming at least include proper Control-S/Control-Q\par
\tab    support; additionally, it is advisable to add dynamic discovery\par
\tab    of the presence of P?S/8 to allow proper conformance to P?S/8 \tab    conventions as necessary.\line\line\tab    Note: All information on how to completely conform with P?S/8\par
\tab    console conventions is always available in the P?S/8 System\par
\tab    Kernel.\par
\par
51.\tab System Kernel\par
\par
\tab Successful PDP-8 programming relies upon a resident system kernel\par
\tab residing in the last page of Field 0 (07600-07777).  All relevant\par
\tab operating systems use this convention; in some circumstances,\par
\tab additional extended memory segments may be required.\par
\par
\tab The \ul Paper-Tape Operating System\ulnone  is the most fundamental system\par
\tab kernel (equivalent).  No actual services are available, but the\par
\tab binary paper tape loading programs reside in (most of) this area\par
\tab of memory.\par
\par
\tab Due to the PDP-8 3-cycle data-break (DMA) architecture, locations\par
\tab 07750-07755 are reserved for specific supported devices as \tab follows:\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\par
\cf0\tab a) 07750-07751 - These locations are reserved for the DF32 or\par
\tab    RF08 disk storage devices.  (The two devices are mutually\par
\tab    exclusive and are programmed nearly identically.)\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab b) 07752-07753 - These locations are reserved for the TC58 half-\par
\tab    inch magnetic tape controller.\par
\par
\tab c) 07754-07755 - These locations are reserved for the TC01 (or\par
\tab    TC08) DECtape controller.\line\line\tab In general, it must be assumed that one (or more) of these\par
\tab devices may be present; any software that accesses these devices\par
\tab will modify the corresponding data-break memory locations.\line\line\tab The \ul Paper-Tape Operating System\ulnone  avoids access to these locations.\par
\tab The RIM loader occupies 07756-07776 (regardless of which of the\par
\tab two versions is in memory).\line\line\tab The BIN loader consists of one of several similar programs, any \tab one of which load into lower memory locations on the same page.  \tab As such, location 07777 is reserved as a JMP instruction to the\par
\tab actual starting address of the particular BIN loader present. \tab This allows the user to access the BIN loader present without\par
\tab requiring specific knowledge of the internal startup location.\par
\par
\tab Note: Most of the existent BIN loaders are flawed in one or more\par
\tab respects with regard to the 3-cycle data-break reserved \tab locations.  Running programs that access the specific device will\par
\tab corrupt the loader.   The most used BIN loader (first released in\par
\tab 1967 with patches over the original loader release to avoid\par
\tab corruption by the use of the TC01 DECtape controller) can be\par
\tab destroyed by using the DF32 or RF08.  The BIN loader from P?S/8 \tab Version 8Z can be destroyed by running programs that use the TC58\par
\tab magnetic tape controller. The only known exception is the BIN\par
\tab loader that will be available in P?S/8 Version 9A; recent changes \tab to P?S/8 (under development) will implement a BIN loader that \tab cannot be destroyed by running any of the 3-cycle data-break\par
\tab devices.\par
\par
\tab The P?S/8 system kernel treats the 3-cycle data-break locations\par
\tab as temporary storage; as such, even if a program is run that\par
\tab accesses any of these device, all calls to the system device \tab handler will initialize the locations as required.\line\line\tab Unlike the \ul Paper-Tape Operating System\ulnone  resident coding, the P?S/8 \tab system kernel provides a range of services including device-\tab independent access to the system device and all related logical\par
\tab units.  Additionally, information is available to executed\par
\tab programs to interact with passed files, command-line option\par
\tab switches and extended memory control definitions.\page\tab\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\par
\cf0\tab In certain P?S/8 configurations, a portion of extended memory may \tab also be reserved as required.  If enabled, the \ul Logical Console\par
\ulnone\i\tab\ul\i0 Overlay\ulnone  information will also be present to allow conforming \par
\tab programs to use this optional feature.\line\line\tab The optional P?S/8 SHELL overlay may be present.  The SHELL also\par
\tab adds additional information to allow SHELL-conforming programs to \tab properly interact with the file system, etc.\par
\par
\tab The OS/8 system kernel is roughly analogous to the P?S/8 system\par
\tab kernel; however, the last page of Field 1 is also reserved\par
\tab (17600-17777).  In certain configurations, the last page of Field\par
\tab 2 is also reserved.\par
\par
\tab Note: It has been amply demonstrated that the decision to require\par
\tab 8K to run OS/8 is contrived and created by ignorant managers for\par
\tab the express purpose of forcing users to purchase additional\par
\tab memory.  This is especially disappointing for a class of users\par
\tab that to not require execution of their own programs in extended\par
 \tab memory (such as embedded systems that were a material portion of\par
\tab users of the PDP-8/A).\line\line\tab The more rational approach would be to allow OS/8 to be bootable \tab in 4K and only when and where necessary strongly recommend 8K (or\par
\tab more) to allow programs to be executed that require additional\par
\tab memory.\line\line\tab Note: One of the goals of the P?S/8 SHELL is to demonstrate the\par
\tab points made above in practice.  Unless the system device handler\par
\tab requires extended memory, the P?S/8 SHELL will operate in 4K of\par
\tab memory (although programs requiring more memory must gracefully\par
\tab exit issuing messages informing the user of the additional memory\par
\tab requirements, etc.).\par
\line 52.\tab TC01 DECtape Controller.\par
\par
\tab The original PDP-8 was introduced without mass storage\par
\tab peripherals in late 1964.  At first, only minimal tasks could be\par
\tab accomplished using the \ul Paper-Tape Operating System)\ulnone .  Several\par
\tab years later, mass storage peripherals were introduced; this\par
\tab allowed the machine to perform for more complex computing tasks\par
\tab (including the maintenance of program-related files on the\par
\tab storage device for development purposes).\line\line\tab Note: The mass storage device hardware only provided the means to\par
\tab perform more complex tasks.  Storage-based operating systems\par
\tab needed to developed to allow this to be a reality.\par
\par
\tab One of the first practical devices to be added is the TC01\par
\tab DECtape Controller which connected one or more TU55 DECtape\par
 \tab drives (up to a total of eight drives numbered 0 through 7).\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: The PDP-5 computer was introduced in 1963; it was an\par
\tab economic and technical failure because it was quite expensive at\par
\tab the time it was released; in a futile effort to (partially) lower\par
\tab the cost, inappropriate measures were taken to cheapen the\par
\tab product.  This actually backfired because while a smaller and\par
\tab cheaper model was technically feasible, it was too impractical\par
\tab for other than a contrived application.  For a typical\par
\tab configuration, the machine's actual cost was higher.  Other\par
\tab changes were made that merely added incompatibility and lowered\par
\tab performance in an effort to lower costs; unfortunately, the\par
\tab design was still based in part on premium speed memory with\par
\tab attendant high cost.  However, due to the ill-advised cost-\par
\tab cutting, the overall performance isn't consistent with the use of\par
\tab expensive memory; many cycles are wasted maintaining a transient\par
\tab copy of the program counter in memory location 00000; the high\par
\tab overhead causes the effective overall speed to be very\par
\tab disappointing given the quality (and cost) of the memory.\line\line\tab An earlier DECtape controller was introduced for the PDP-5 known\par
\tab as the Type 552 controller.  Apparently, production had started\par
\tab on this controller, as well as a companion dual DECtape drive\par
\tab known as the Type 555.  (The type 555 was also used on other DEC\par
\tab computer models from the same era such as the PDP-6 and PDP-7.)\par
\line\tab Nearly all of the Type 552 controllers were actually made part of\par
\tab very early Typeset-8 systems using a mechanical bus converter.\par
\tab The Type 552 controller is actually incomplete; it is not\par
\tab possible to transfer data on systems larger than 4K with\par
\tab interrupts enabled.  (When there is an interrupt, the memory\par
\tab field is forced to 0 and the former value is saved in an\par
\tab interrupt buffer to allow restoration when dismissing the\par
\tab interrupt; however, if a DMA operation is in progress, it will\par
\tab now be subject to transfer into the wrong field unless all \tab transfers are limited to field 0.)\line\line\tab The Typeset-8 system considered DECtape as an option; this system\par
\tab can run entirely in memory.  When needed, an additional program\par
\tab section is read into memory (from either DECtape or paper-tape).\par
\tab As such, the only facility gained by using DECtapes is to speed\par
\tab up program phase changes as needed.  (The special-purpose\par
\tab Typeset-8 DECtape software is hardly a general-purpose operating\par
\tab system.)\par
\par
\tab When the TC01 was released, a companion drive was made available\par
\tab known as the TU55 DECtape drive.  While the TU55 is a single-\par
\tab drive transport, it can be configured to be compatible with the\par
\tab Type 555 controller or the TC01 controller.  As such, every Type \tab 552 controller could be marketed as part of a Typeset-8 system,\par
\tab perhaps configured with a TU55 (instead of the Type 555 dual-\par
\tab drive).\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\par
\cf0\tab Eventually, Typeset-8 systems were configured with the TC01\par
\tab controller and (at least one) TU55 drive.  Later systems were\par
\tab produced with newer drives and/or controllers such as the TU56\par
\tab dual-drive (which could be purchases as the TU56H with only one \tab drive); the TC01 design was upgraded to the TC08 as a replacement \tab that could be configured for the older negative bus machines or\par
\tab the newer positive bus machines.  TC08 controllers are\par
\tab implemented using the newer M-series logic (which was also used\par
\tab in the follow-on PDP-8/I computer).  When being used as a\par
\tab replacement for the TC01 (which can only be negative bus, the\par
\tab TC08 is designated TC08N; the positive bus variant is TC08P.\par
\par
\tab Note: The controller bus to the DECtape drives is always\par
\tab implemented using negative bus signals for all TC01 or TC08\par
\tab systems.\line\line\tab The TC01 or TC08 with TU55 (or TU56) drives is essentially the \tab starting point for practical PDP-8-based storage-based operating \tab systems.  The original peripherals included a .5 inch magnetic\par
\tab tape controller; however, this device is not block-replaceable.  \tab While DECtape is inherently a serial access device, it supports\par
\tab fixed storage blocks which can always be rewritten without \tab affecting the contents of adjacent blocks.\line\line\tab The DF32 Mini-disk was also released in the same time period.\par
\tab However, this device has many limitations as follows:\line\line\tab a) One DF32 holds as much as the full complement of a 32K\par
\tab    machine's memory.  This meager storage is not very useful.\par
\tab    Expansion units are available to add one, two or three DS32\par
\tab    expander units; however, this still aggregates to less than\par
\tab    the standard capacity of a single DECtape.\par
\par
\tab b) The drive is quite expensive considering how little storage is\par
\tab    provided.  Having four mini-disks total is prohibitively\par
\tab    expensive.\line\line\tab c) Due to the inability to retract the heads, the drives must be\par
\tab    powered on continuously 24 hours a day.  Any power-down event\par
\tab    causes the need for a service call.\par
\par
\tab d) DF32 disks are not dismountable.  A practical system likely\par
\tab    requires DECtape or other storage medium to perform backup and\par
\tab    restore operations.\par
\line\tab The RF08 is twice as large as the four mini-disks together for\par
\tab the largest DF32 configuration.  Additionally, the configuration\par
\tab can be expanded with up to three RS08 expansion drives analogous \tab to the DS32 expansion, etc.  Such a system is physically very\par
\tab large and is a major maintenance burden.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\cf0\line\line\tab Note: A modification was made to the RF08 to allow up to four\par
\tab additional drives.  This was performed by DEC Computer Special\par
\tab Systems (CSS) for use with the Clinical LAB-12 software.  A\par
\tab Clinical LAB-12 system represents the most expensive PDP-8\par
\tab configuration by far.\line\line\tab As such, the TC01 or TC08 and one or more DECtape drives is the\par
\tab best value; additionally, the storage is quickly dismountable if\par
\tab additional storage is required.\par
