57.\tab Three-for-two\par
\par
 7.   Three-for-two\par
\par
\tab This is an informal reference to the method used in certain PDP-8\par
\tab file systems to pack three bytes of seven (or eight) bits each\par
\tab into two adjacent 12-bit words within a larger structure (such as\par
\tab a storage device block or record).  This packing scheme is used\par
\tab inside of OS/8 binary files and also text files in both OS/8 and\par
     the P?S/8 SHELL environments.\par
\line\tab The first character occupies the low-order eight bits of the\par
\tab first twelve-bit word.  The second character occupies the low-\tab order eight bits of the second twelve-bit word.  The third \tab character is split into the upper-most four bits and the lower-\par
\tab most four bits.  The upper-most four bits are placed into the\par
\tab four high-order bits of the first 12-bit word; the lower-most\par
\tab four bits are placed into the four high-order bits of the second\par
\tab 12-bit word.\line\line\tab While several other (less than satisfactory) methods of\par
\tab implementing \ul three-\tab for-two\ulnone  packing and unpacking have been\par
\tab devised, clearly the most elegant way is to use co-routines such\par
\tab as how the author of this document implemented file support in\par
\tab the OS/8 program \i Kermit-12\i0 .  The coding space occupied by both\par
\tab routines together is slightly less than half of that used by\par
\tab commonly used routines nominally written for the same purpose in\par
\tab other OS/8 programs, yet the co-routines are also faster and\par
\tab have higher overall data throughput.  This is one of the few\par
\tab counter-examples where speed and small size are not in tradeoff\par
\tab opposition. \par
\par
     The P?S/8 SHELL file system will store text format files in a\par
     manner similar to OS/8 with certain considerations as follows:\line\line      a) OS/8 (nearly) always sets the high-order bit of every frame to\par
        (over) simplify the process of conversion of the data to\par
        paper-tape frames; however, since this is a trivial\par
        consideration (and is also often ignored), it is always\par
        necessary to mask off the high-order bit (which contains no\par
        useful information) when processing the latest byte.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\cf0\line\line      b) All OS/8 files are meant to be terminated with trailing\par
        Control-Z characters (with the high-order clear); however,\par
\tab    this convention is often misimplemented with the high-order\par
\tab    bit inadvertently set; in some cases, files end at the last\par
\tab    byte of the last block of the file, entirely lacking the\par
\tab    trailing Control-Z character.  The co-routines used are able\par
\tab    to correct for these occasional file formatting  blunders.\par
\par
     c) P?S/8 SHELL text files will always clear the high-order bit of\par
        every byte; in special cases, setting the high-order bit will\par
        be allowed for special formatting purposes using proprietary\par
        extended character codes beyond the normal seven-bit ASCII\par
\tab    character set.\par
\par
     d) P?S/8 SHELL text files will always include trailing Control-Z\par
        characters with the high-order bit clear.  Co-routine\par
\tab    implementation makes this easy to achieve in minimal code\par
\tab    space.\line\line      e) P?S/8 SHELL binary files will use Slurp binary loading format\par
\tab    as is used in the basic P?S/8 TFS file system.  Some slurp\par
\tab    format binary loading or other utilities are best implemented\par
\tab    using co-routines; \ul three-\tab for-two\ulnone  packing methods play no\par
\tab    role in Slurp format binary files (which are multiples of\par
\tab    12-bit words).\par
\par
58.\tab TFS\par
\par
 8.\tab Tiny File System (TFS) and the TFS directory\par
\par
\tab The \ul\i Tiny File System\ulnone\i0  (\i TFS\i0 ) is the primary file format used in the\par
 \tab basic P?S/8 system.  Each TFS file is of fixed size allowing the\par
\tab TFS directory structure to consist of allocatable slots that are\par
\tab adjacent and cannot overlap.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
 \tab If a TFS directory filename is modified or deleted, the\par
\tab underlying storage is not changed (unless an explicit write\par
\tab operation is performed).  This allows files to be easily renamed\par
\tab as needed.\par
\par
\tab There are unique advantages to the TFS structure which, for\par
\tab certain users, requires some measure of orientation and \par
\tab experience.  Once mastered, rapid editing of large projects can\par
\tab proceed with little additional effort.\line\line\tab Files are loaded into the keyboard monitor edit buffer to modify\par
\tab the contents.  The process is not finalized until appropriate\par
\tab commands are issued to write the edit buffer contents to a slot \tab in the TFS directory.  This generally means that the same file \tab slot is reused; as such, there will be no need to access the TFS\par
\tab directory.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: For those requiring the notion of a file backup, the\par
\tab updated file can be written to a different TFS directory slot.\par
\tab Appropriate filename changes can be made later to maintain\par
\tab ongoing file names.  Advanced users are known to pre-allocate \tab additional directory slots for this purpose.\par
\par
\tab The TFS directory structure is used only for user-created files,\par
\tab including the Slurp format binary files produced by P?S/8 PAL\par
\tab assembly of TFS text files (and/or extended-length text files).\par
\tab System programs are stored in an independent file structure\par
\tab elsewhere on the system device and/or other logical device units.\par
\par
\tab For example, it is expected that PAL assembly projects of nearly\par
\tab any size will require several TFS files.  The contents of\par
\tab multiple TFS files are concatenated together and passed to P?S/8\par
\tab PAL (as required) to carry out the current assembly project.\par
\par
\tab Note: While certain P?S/8 system programs address the associated\par
\tab line numbers within TFS text files, P?S/8 PAL (and most other\par
\tab P?S/8 system programs) ignores line number data.  When necessary,\par
\tab extended-length files can be passed to P?S/8 PAL to allow\par
\tab assembly of vary large source programs.  As necessary, the source\par
\tab file input stream passed to P?S/8 PAL can be a mixture of TFS\par
\tab text files and extended-length text files.\par
