58.\tab Winchester\par
\par
\tab\i Winchester\i0  is a designation for certain hard disk drives that\par
\tab encompass various related (yet incompatible) interface types.  \tab For the purposes of various interfaces, it is necessary to use \tab matching components in terms of the specific interface used by \tab both the hard disk drive and hard disk controller.\line\line\tab The relevant usage concerns the use of a designated SCSI\par
\tab controller on the MDC8 SCSI Host Adaptor for the PDP-8/A.  This\par
\tab is analogous to the diskette devices that also can be connected \tab to the same controller series; common configurations are up to \tab four diskette drives in the range of device unit 0 through 3, but \tab limited to 2 and 3 if the Winchester disk(s) are configured (as \tab these devices are limited to device unit 0 and 1).\line\line\tab Due to the relatively large disk capacity, a common configuration\par
\tab is to have a single Winchester SCSI drive on device unit 0 and \tab diskettes on device units 0 and 1.  This configuration reserves \tab device unit 1 for a temporary connection of an additional hard \tab drive for backup purposes, etc.\par
\par
\tab Note: On some systems, device unit 1 is used for removable \tab cartridge disk drives of a lower capacity per cartridge; these \tab cartridges can be quickly changed, allowing a complete high-speed \tab backup or restore in very little time.\par
\par
\tab The use of \i Winchester\i0  in turn leads to the truncated form \i WNax: \tab\i0 where \i WN\i0  is the group designation, \i a\i0  is an alphabetic character\par
\tab such as \cf2\highlight3\f0 A\cf0\highlight0\f1  and \i x\i0  is a numeric character in the range of \cf2\highlight3\f0 0\cf0\highlight0\f1  through\par
\tab\cf2\highlight3\f0 7\cf0\highlight0\f1 .  The actual implementation is using mapped sections of a\par
\tab larger physical device.\par
\par
\tab This notation is actually an accommodation made for the benefit\par
\tab of \i OS/8\i0 , which cannot address more than 2.0 MWords per logical\par
\tab device.  As such, the logical device arrangement on any given\par
\tab hard disk starts with WNA0:, WNA1: etc.\par
\par
\tab Since OS/8 has few devices available in any practical system,\par
\tab various separately-named copies of OS/8 \i BUILD.SV\i0  were needed to\par
\tab effect non-system drives chosen from the much larger available\par
\tab pool of non-system logical drives in order to quickly change the\par
\tab instant scope of the configuration needed at any time.\line\line\tab Part of the design of the forthcoming P?S/8 SHELL overlay is to\par
\tab overcome this clumsy implementation by allowing far larger\par
\tab logical units as part of a non-system handler with a far larger \tab scope of storage allocation.  While it is theoretically possible \tab to have the entire drive be considered such as WNA0: and a second \tab hard disk as WNA1:, this is an inadequate granularity erring on \tab the side of excessive unwieldiness.  As such, it is expected that\par
\tab MDC8 support for the P?S/8 SHELL will map the entire drive into\par
\tab\i MDA0:\i0  through \i MDA7:\i0  allowing a second hard disk to be \i MDB0:\i0  \tab through \i MDB7:\i0 .\par
\par
\tab Note: P?S/8 non-system device handlers will be deliberately \tab renamed to the \i MD\i0  designation to avoid confusion with the OS/8 \tab designations (for far smaller logical storage units).\par
\par
\tab A comparison of various maximum system device sizes follows:\line\line\tab a) OS/8 devices can be as large as 2.0 MWords expressed as up to \tab    4096 records of 256 12-bit words each.\par
\par
\tab    Note: For the MDC8, only logical unit 0 can be bootstrapped.\par
\par
\tab b) P?S/8 system devices can be as large as 1.0 MWords expressed \tab\tab    as up to 4096 blocks of 128 12-bit words each.  For the MDC8,\par
\tab    each of these system devices includes eight logical units 0\par
 \tab    through 7.\par
\par
\tab    Note: Any of these eight logical units can be bootstrapped\par
\tab    with the appropriate extended boot code.\par
  \par
\tab c) P?S/8 non-system devices can be as large as 4096 MWords\par
\tab    expressed as up to 16,777,216 blocks of 128 12-bit words each\par
\tab    per logical unit.  The total capacity for an entire device\par
\tab    with eight logical units is 134,217,728 blocks of 128 12-bit\par
\tab    words each.\par
\par
\tab    Note: Assuming six-bit bytes, the largest theoretical capacity \tab    of an entire non-system device (addressable using one non-\par
\tab    system handler) is 32 GB.\line\line\tab The largest disk drive allowed on the MDC8 as developed is 416 \tab MB.  This capability is far beyond the practical scope of OS/8,\par
\tab yet is hardly a challenge for P?S/8 non-system handlers.\par
